-- ============================================================
-- c_purification_methods (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Crystallization, sublimation, distillation, extraction, chromatography
-- ============================================================
('Crystallization is used to purify', 'Liquids with different boiling points', 'Solids that are more soluble in hot solvent than cold', 'Gases', 'Metals', 'option2', 'Dissolve impure solid in hot solvent, filter, cool. Pure crystals form as solubility decreases.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sublimation is used to purify substances that', 'Melt easily', 'Pass directly from solid to vapour without melting', 'Are liquids', 'Dissolve in water', 'option2', 'Sublimable substances: camphor, naphthalene, iodine, benzoic acid. Impurities (non-sublimable) are left behind.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Simple distillation is used to separate', 'Immiscible liquids', 'Two miscible liquids with large boiling point differences ($> 25°\text{C}$)', 'Solids from solids', 'Gases from gases', 'option2', 'The liquid with lower bp vaporizes first, condenses, and is collected separately.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fractional distillation is used when', 'The boiling points of two liquids are close (within $25°\text{C}$)', 'The liquids are immiscible', 'Only one liquid is present', 'The liquids are gases', 'option1', 'A fractionating column provides multiple vaporization-condensation cycles, improving separation.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Steam distillation is used for', 'Water-soluble substances', 'Substances immiscible with water that decompose at their boiling point', 'Gases', 'Metals', 'option2', 'Steam lowers the effective bp. Used for aniline, essential oils. Distils below $100°\text{C}$ at atmospheric pressure.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Differential extraction uses', 'A separating funnel to extract a solute from one solvent into another immiscible solvent', 'Distillation', 'Sublimation', 'Crystallization', 'option1', 'Based on partition coefficient. Multiple small extractions are more efficient than one large extraction.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chromatography separates components based on', 'Their differential adsorption or partition between a stationary and mobile phase', 'Their boiling points', 'Their melting points', 'Their colour only', 'option1', 'Types: column, TLC, paper, gas, HPLC. Components move at different rates based on affinity for each phase.', 'c_purification_methods', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In column chromatography, the stationary phase is typically', 'Alumina or silica gel packed in a glass column', 'A liquid flowing through the column', 'A gas', 'A metal', 'option1', 'Solid adsorbent (stationary). Solvent (mobile phase/eluent) carries components down at different rates.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $R_f$ value in TLC (Thin Layer Chromatography) is defined as', 'Mass of solute / Mass of solvent', 'Distance moved by solvent / Distance moved by solute', 'Distance moved by solute / Distance moved by solvent front', 'Volume of solute / Volume of solvent', 'option3', '$R_f = d_{solute}/d_{solvent}$. Characteristic for a given compound under specific conditions. $0 < R_f < 1$.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vacuum distillation is used for liquids that', 'Have very low boiling points', 'Decompose at their normal boiling point', 'Are gases', 'Are solids', 'option2', 'Reduced pressure lowers the boiling point, allowing distillation without thermal decomposition. Used for glycerol.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In paper chromatography, the stationary phase is', 'Silica gel', 'The solvent', 'Alumina', 'The paper itself (cellulose fibres holding water)', 'option4', 'Water trapped in cellulose fibres acts as the stationary phase. The organic solvent is the mobile phase.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Zone refining is used to purify', 'Salts', 'Organic liquids', 'Gases', 'Semiconductors (Si, Ge) to ultra-high purity', 'option4', 'A narrow molten zone moves along a rod of impure material. Impurities concentrate in the molten zone and are swept to one end.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Distillation under reduced pressure is preferred over simple distillation when', 'Simple distillation is always better', 'The compound is very volatile', 'The compound is a gas', 'The compound has a high boiling point and may decompose on heating', 'option4', 'Lower pressure $\to$ lower bp $\to$ distillation at lower temperature $\to$ no decomposition.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Azeotropic distillation is needed when', 'Two liquids form a constant-boiling mixture that cannot be separated by simple fractional distillation', 'The liquids are immiscible', 'Only one component is present', 'The mixture is a solid', 'option1', 'Azeotropes (e.g., ethanol-water at $95.6\%$) require a third component or special techniques (e.g., molecular sieves) to break.', 'c_purification_methods', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In gas chromatography (GC), the mobile phase is', 'A liquid', 'An inert gas (He, N$_2$, Ar)', 'A solid', 'Water', 'option2', 'GC: inert carrier gas (mobile) + liquid/solid coated on column (stationary). Separates volatile compounds. Detector at the end.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('HPLC (High Performance Liquid Chromatography) differs from column chromatography in that', 'It is slower', 'It uses no solvent', 'It uses high pressure to force solvent through a finely packed column, giving faster and better separation', 'It cannot separate organic compounds', 'option3', 'HPLC: small particle size stationary phase + high pressure pump = superior resolution and speed.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The efficiency of solvent extraction can be improved by', 'Using one large portion', 'Using multiple small portions of solvent rather than one large portion', 'Heating the mixture', 'Using water as the extracting solvent for all compounds', 'option2', 'Mathematically: $n$ extractions with $V/n$ solvent each extract more than one extraction with $V$ solvent.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In adsorption chromatography, the order of elution depends on', 'The polarity of the compound relative to the adsorbent and eluent', 'Only the molecular mass', 'Only the colour', 'The temperature only', 'option1', 'On polar adsorbent (silica): less polar compounds elute first. Increasing eluent polarity elutes more polar compounds.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electrophoresis separates molecules based on', 'Their colour', 'Their boiling points', 'Their charge and size in an electric field', 'Their mass only', 'option3', 'Used for proteins and DNA. Charged molecules migrate at different rates in a gel matrix under an applied electric field.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The principle behind steam distillation is that', 'Steam heats the mixture', 'The total vapour pressure of immiscible liquids equals the sum of their individual vapour pressures, reaching atmospheric pressure below either bp', 'Water dissolves the compound', 'Steam acts as a catalyst', 'option2', '$P_{total} = P_{water} + P_{compound} = P_{atm}$. Since both contribute, the mixture boils below $100°\text{C}$.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ion-exchange chromatography separates ions by', 'Partition between two liquids', 'Adsorption on silica', 'Reversible exchange of ions between the solution and an ion-exchange resin', 'Sublimation', 'option3', 'Cation exchangers (e.g., sulfonated polystyrene) or anion exchangers. Used for water softening and amino acid separation.', 'c_purification_methods', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_qualitative_analysis_organic (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Detection of N, S, P, halogens in organic compounds
-- ============================================================
('The Lassaigne test (sodium fusion test) is used to detect', 'Metals in organic compounds', 'Extra elements (N, S, halogens) in organic compounds', 'Carbon and hydrogen only', 'Oxygen', 'option2', 'Organic compound is fused with Na metal. Na converts N, S, halogens into ionic forms (NaCN, Na$_2$S, NaX) detectable by simple tests.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Lassaigne test, nitrogen is detected by the formation of', 'NaCl', 'Na$_2$S', 'NaCN (sodium cyanide), which gives Prussian blue with FeSO$_4$/FeCl$_3$', 'NaOH', 'option3', 'Na + C + N $\to$ NaCN. NaCN + FeSO$_4 \to$ Na$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$]. Then FeCl$_3$ gives Fe$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$]$_3$ (Prussian blue).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sulphur in an organic compound is detected by the Lassaigne test through', 'Formation of NaCl', 'Formation of NaCN', 'Formation of Na$_2$S, which gives a violet colour with sodium nitroprusside', 'No reaction', 'option3', 'Na + S $\to$ Na$_2$S. Na$_2$S + Na$_2$[Fe(CN)$_5$NO] $\to$ Na$_4$[Fe(CN)$_5$NOS] (violet colour).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Halogens in an organic compound are detected by', 'Adding NaOH', 'Adding AgNO$_3$ to the Lassaigne extract (after acidifying with HNO$_3$)', 'Heating alone', 'Adding H$_2$SO$_4$', 'option2', 'Na + X $\to$ NaX. Acidify with HNO$_3$, add AgNO$_3$: AgCl (white), AgBr (pale yellow), AgI (yellow).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Before testing for halogens in the Lassaigne extract, nitrogen and sulphur must be removed because', 'NaCN and Na$_2$S interfere with the AgNO$_3$ test (form AgCN and Ag$_2$S precipitates)', 'They are not present', 'They enhance the test', 'No removal is needed', 'option1', 'Boil the extract with HNO$_3$ to decompose NaCN and Na$_2$S before adding AgNO$_3$.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If both N and S are present in a compound, the Lassaigne fusion produces', 'Only Na$_2$S', 'Only NaCN', 'NaSCN (sodium thiocyanate) instead of NaCN and Na$_2$S separately', 'No reaction', 'option3', 'Na + C + N + S $\to$ NaSCN. This gives a blood-red colour with FeCl$_3$ (not Prussian blue). Excess Na is needed to get both NaCN and Na$_2$S.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phosphorus in an organic compound is detected by', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'The Lassaigne test directly', 'Oxidizing to phosphate and testing with ammonium molybdate (yellow precipitate)', 'Adding NaOH', 'option3', 'Fuse with Na$_2$O$_2$ or HNO$_3$. PO$_4^{3-}$ + (NH$_4$)$_2$MoO$_4$ + HNO$_3 \to$ yellow precipitate of ammonium phosphomolybdate.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Beilstein test for halogens involves', 'Heating the compound on a copper wire in a flame — green flame indicates halogen', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'Lassaigne fusion', 'Distillation', 'option1', 'Cu + halogen $\to$ volatile copper halide $\to$ green flame. Quick but not specific (N compounds can also give green).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Lassaigne test, the sodium fusion extract is prepared by', 'Heating with NaOH solution', 'Dissolving in HCl', 'Fusing the organic compound with metallic sodium, dissolving the residue in distilled water, and filtering', 'Mixing with AgNO$_3$', 'option3', 'Na metal + organic compound $\to$ heat strongly $\to$ plunge into water $\to$ boil, filter. The filtrate is the Lassaigne extract.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To distinguish between Cl, Br, and I in the Lassaigne extract, the precipitate with AgNO$_3$ is tested with', 'H$_2$SO$_4$', 'HCl', 'NaOH', 'NH$_4$OH: AgCl dissolves, AgBr partially dissolves, AgI does not dissolve', 'option4', 'AgCl: soluble in dilute NH$_4$OH. AgBr: soluble in conc. NH$_4$OH. AgI: insoluble in NH$_4$OH (soluble in Na$_2$S$_2$O$_3$).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Prussian blue test confirms the presence of', 'Phosphorus', 'Sulphur', 'Halogens', 'Nitrogen', 'option4', 'NaCN + FeSO$_4 \to$ Na$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$] + FeCl$_3 \to$ Fe$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$]$_3$ (Prussian blue). Confirms N.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The sodium nitroprusside test confirms the presence of', 'Nitrogen', 'Sulphur', 'Halogens', 'Carbon', 'option2', 'Na$_2$S + Na$_2$[Fe(CN)$_5$NO] $\to$ violet colour. Specific for sulphide ions.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For compounds containing both N and S, excess sodium is used in the Lassaigne test to ensure', 'No reaction occurs', 'Only NaSCN forms', 'Both NaCN and Na$_2$S are formed (not just NaSCN)', 'The compound does not decompose', 'option3', 'With limited Na: NaSCN forms. With excess Na: NaSCN + 2Na $\to$ NaCN + Na$_2$S. Both can then be tested separately.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chloroform (CHCl$_3$) does not give a positive Beilstein test directly because', 'It actually does give a positive Beilstein test (green flame) since it contains Cl', 'It has no halogen', 'Cu does not react with Cl', 'The flame is colourless', 'option1', 'CHCl$_3$ contains Cl and gives a green flame in the Beilstein test. The statement in the question is false — it does give a positive test.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Carius method is used for', 'Quantitative estimation of halogens, sulphur, and phosphorus in organic compounds', 'Qualitative detection only', 'Estimation of carbon and hydrogen', 'Estimation of nitrogen', 'option1', 'Compound is heated with fuming HNO$_3$ in a sealed tube. Halogens $\to$ AgX, S $\to$ BaSO$_4$, P $\to$ Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Lassaigne test fails for diazo compounds (e.g., C$_6$H$_5$N$_2$Cl) because', 'N$_2$ is lost as gas during fusion and does not form NaCN', 'They have no nitrogen', 'Na does not react', 'The test always works', 'option1', 'Diazo compounds lose N$_2$ easily on heating. The nitrogen escapes before it can react with Na to form NaCN.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The lead acetate test for sulphur involves', 'Lassaigne fusion', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'Heating the compound with NaOH, then adding lead acetate — black PbS precipitate confirms S', 'Adding FeCl$_3$', 'option3', 'Organic S + NaOH $\to$ Na$_2$S. Na$_2$S + Pb(CH$_3$COO)$_2 \to$ PbS (black precipitate).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Middleton test is a modification of the Lassaigne test for', 'Gases', 'Solid compounds only', 'Compounds that are difficult to fuse with sodium (e.g., volatile liquids)', 'Metals', 'option3', 'Uses calcium oxide mixed with sodium to provide a matrix that holds volatile compounds during fusion.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the detection of fluorine, the Lassaigne test is not suitable because', 'Na does not react with F', 'F does not exist in organic compounds', 'NaF is not precipitated by AgNO$_3$ (AgF is soluble)', 'The test always works for F', 'option3', 'AgF is soluble in water, so the standard AgNO$_3$ test fails. F is detected by other methods (e.g., CaF$_2$ formation, or spectroscopic methods).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The blood-red colour with FeCl$_3$ in the Lassaigne extract indicates', 'Presence of S only', 'Presence of N only', 'Presence of both N and S (thiocyanate formed)', 'Presence of halogens', 'option3', 'NaSCN + FeCl$_3 \to$ Fe(SCN)$_3$ (blood red). Indicates both N and S are present.', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The lime test for nitrogen involves heating the compound with', 'Soda lime (NaOH + CaO) — NH$_3$ evolved turns moist red litmus blue', 'HCl', 'H$_2$SO$_4$', 'Na metal', 'option1', 'Organic N + NaOH/CaO $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ NH$_3$. Simple but not reliable for all N-containing compounds (e.g., nitro compounds may not give NH$_3$).', 'c_qualitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_quantitative_analysis_organic (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Estimation of C, H, N, halogens, S, P
-- ============================================================
('Carbon and hydrogen in an organic compound are estimated by', 'Liebig''s method (combustion with CuO)', 'Lassaigne test', 'Carius method', 'Dumas method', 'option1', 'Compound + CuO $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ CO$_2$ + H$_2$O. CO$_2$ absorbed by KOH, H$_2$O by CaCl$_2$. Weigh to find C and H.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nitrogen is estimated quantitatively by', 'Chromatography', 'Liebig''s method', 'Carius method', 'Dumas method (N$_2$ gas collected) or Kjeldahl method (NH$_3$ distilled)', 'option4', 'Dumas: compound + CuO $\to$ N$_2$ (collected over KOH). Kjeldahl: compound + H$_2$SO$_4 \to$ (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4 \to$ NH$_3$ (distilled into acid, titrated).', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Halogens are estimated quantitatively by', 'Kjeldahl method', 'Liebig''s method', 'Dumas method', 'Carius method (heating with fuming HNO$_3$, precipitating as AgX)', 'option4', 'Compound + fuming HNO$_3$ (sealed tube) $\to$ HX $\to$ AgX precipitate. Weigh AgX to calculate halogen percentage.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sulphur is estimated by the Carius method as', 'PbS precipitate', 'AgCl precipitate', 'BaSO$_4$ precipitate', 'Na$_2$S', 'option3', 'Compound + fuming HNO$_3 \to$ H$_2$SO$_4 \to$ BaSO$_4$ (with BaCl$_2$). Weigh BaSO$_4$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phosphorus is estimated as', 'CO$_2$', 'AgCl', 'BaSO$_4$', 'Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$ (magnesium pyrophosphate)', 'option4', 'Compound + HNO$_3 \to$ H$_3$PO$_4 \to$ (NH$_4$)$_3$PO$_4 \cdot 12$MoO$_3 \to$ Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$ (weighed).', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Liebig''s method, CuO acts as', 'A reducing agent', 'An oxidizing agent that converts C to CO$_2$ and H to H$_2$O', 'A catalyst', 'A drying agent', 'option2', 'CuO provides oxygen for complete combustion. CuO $\to$ Cu (reduced). C $\to$ CO$_2$, H $\to$ H$_2$O.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Kjeldahl method cannot be used for compounds containing', 'N in ring structures (pyridine), nitro groups, or azo groups', 'Only amines', 'Only amides', 'Any nitrogen compound', 'option1', 'Kjeldahl requires N to be converted to NH$_3$. Ring N, $-$NO$_2$, $-$N=N$-$ are not fully converted by H$_2$SO$_4$ digestion.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In Liebig''s combustion, $0.30\;\text{g}$ of an organic compound gave $0.88\;\text{g}$ CO$_2$ and $0.36\;\text{g}$ H$_2$O. The percentage of C and H are', 'C $= 60\%$, H $= 10\%$', 'C $= 40\%$, H $= 6.7\%$', 'C $= 80\%$, H $= 13.3\%$', 'C $= 44\%$, H $= 18\%$', 'option3', 'C $= (12/44) \times 0.88/0.30 \times 100 = 0.24/0.30 \times 100 = 80\%$. H $= (2/18) \times 0.36/0.30 \times 100 = 0.04/0.30 \times 100 = 13.3\%$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Kjeldahl method, $0.5\;\text{g}$ of a compound required $20\;\text{mL}$ of $0.1\;\text{N}$ H$_2$SO$_4$ for neutralization of evolved NH$_3$. The percentage of N is', '$5.6\%$', '$2.8\%$', '$11.2\%$', '$14\%$', 'option1', 'Meq of H$_2$SO$_4 = 20 \times 0.1 = 2$. Meq of N $= 2$. Mass of N $= 2 \times 14/1000 = 0.028\;\text{g}$. $\% = 0.028/0.5 \times 100 = 5.6\%$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Carius method, $0.284\;\text{g}$ of a compound gave $0.287\;\text{g}$ AgCl. The percentage of Cl is ($M_{\text{AgCl}} = 143.5$)', '$25\%$', '$35.5\%$', '$50\%$', '$12.5\%$', 'option1', 'Cl in AgCl $= 35.5/143.5 \times 0.287 = 0.071\;\text{g}$. $\% = 0.071/0.284 \times 100 = 25\%$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Dumas method, $0.50\;\text{g}$ of a compound gave $112\;\text{mL}$ of N$_2$ at STP. The percentage of N is', '$14\%$', '$28\%$', '$56\%$', '$7\%$', 'option2', '$n_{N_2} = 112/22400 = 0.005\;\text{mol}$. N atoms $= 0.01\;\text{mol}$. Mass of N $= 0.01 \times 14 = 0.14\;\text{g}$. $\% = 0.14/0.50 \times 100 = 28\%$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Carius method for sulphur estimation, $0.16\;\text{g}$ of a compound gave $0.466\;\text{g}$ BaSO$_4$ ($M = 233$). The percentage of S is', '$40\%$', '$20\%$', '$32\%$', '$64\%$', 'option1', 'S in BaSO$_4 = (32/233) \times 0.466 = 0.064\;\text{g}$. $\% = 0.064/0.16 \times 100 = 40\%$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The percentage of oxygen in an organic compound is usually determined by', 'Direct combustion', 'Difference: $\%\text{O} = 100 - (\%\text{C} + \%\text{H} + \%\text{N} + \%\text{S} + \%\text{X})$', 'Carius method', 'Kjeldahl method', 'option2', 'Direct estimation of O is difficult. It is calculated by subtracting all other element percentages from 100.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Kjeldahl method, the organic compound is digested with', 'HNO$_3$', 'Dilute HCl', 'NaOH', 'Concentrated H$_2$SO$_4$ (with K$_2$SO$_4$ and CuSO$_4$ as catalysts)', 'option4', 'H$_2$SO$_4$ converts organic N to (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$. K$_2$SO$_4$ raises bp. CuSO$_4$ catalyzes oxidation.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound contains $C = 40\%$, $H = 6.7\%$, $O = 53.3\%$. The empirical formula is', 'CHO', 'CH$_2$O', 'C$_2$H$_4$O$_2$', 'CHO$_2$', 'option2', 'C: $40/12 = 3.33$. H: $6.7/1 = 6.7$. O: $53.3/16 = 3.33$. Ratio $= 1:2:1$. EF $= \text{CH}_2\text{O}$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In modern organic analysis, the most common method for simultaneous C, H, N determination is', 'CHN elemental analyzer (automated combustion + gas chromatography)', 'Liebig''s method', 'Kjeldahl method', 'Carius method', 'option1', 'CHN analyzer: automated combustion at $\sim 1000°\text{C}$, products separated by GC, detected by thermal conductivity. Fast and accurate.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Dumas method, the gas collected over KOH is pure N$_2$ because', 'No other gases are formed', 'N$_2$ is the only gas produced', 'KOH absorbs N$_2$', 'KOH absorbs CO$_2$ and H$_2$O; a copper gauze reduces any nitrogen oxides to N$_2$', 'option4', 'Combustion products: CO$_2$ (absorbed by KOH), H$_2$O (absorbed by KOH), N$_2$ (collected), NO$_x$ (reduced by Cu to N$_2$).', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The silver salt method for estimating the molecular formula of a carboxylic acid involves', 'Distillation', 'Titrating with NaOH', 'Combustion analysis', 'Preparing the silver salt, determining Ag content, and calculating the equivalent mass of the acid', 'option4', 'RCOOH + AgNO$_3 \to$ RCOOAg. Ignite: RCOOAg $\to$ Ag. $\%$ Ag gives equivalent mass, hence molecular formula.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The chloroplatinate method is used to determine the molecular formula of', 'Carboxylic acids', 'Alcohols', 'Amines (by forming chloroplatinate salts)', 'Alkanes', 'option3', 'Amine + H$_2$PtCl$_6 \to$ (amine)$_2 \cdot$ H$_2$PtCl$_6$. Ignite to get Pt. $\%$ Pt gives molecular formula.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_empirical_molecular_formulae (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Calculations of empirical and molecular formulae
-- ============================================================
('The empirical formula represents', 'The structural formula', 'The actual number of atoms in a molecule', 'The molecular mass', 'The simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound', 'option4', 'Empirical formula: simplest ratio. Molecular formula: actual number. E.g., CH$_2$O (empirical) vs C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$ (molecular, glucose).', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The molecular formula is always a _____ of the empirical formula.', 'Fraction', 'Whole-number multiple', 'Square root', 'Logarithm', 'option2', 'Molecular formula $= n \times$ empirical formula, where $n = M_{molecular}/M_{empirical}$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To determine the empirical formula from percentage composition, the first step is to', 'Multiply by molecular mass', 'Add all percentages', 'Convert percentages to moles by dividing by atomic masses', 'Subtract from 100', 'option3', 'Step 1: $\%/M_{atomic}$ for each element. Step 2: Divide by the smallest. Step 3: Round to nearest whole numbers.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound has $C = 85.7\%$ and $H = 14.3\%$. The empirical formula is', 'CH', 'CH$_2$', 'CH$_3$', 'C$_2$H$_6$', 'option2', 'C: $85.7/12 = 7.14$. H: $14.3/1 = 14.3$. Ratio $= 7.14:14.3 = 1:2$. EF $= \text{CH}_2$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the empirical formula is CH$_2$O and the molar mass is $180\;\text{g/mol}$, the molecular formula is', 'CH$_2$O', 'C$_2$H$_4$O$_2$', 'C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$', 'C$_3$H$_6$O$_3$', 'option3', 'EF mass $= 12 + 2 + 16 = 30$. $n = 180/30 = 6$. MF $= \text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6$ (glucose).', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For ionic compounds, the empirical formula and molecular formula are', 'Always different', 'Always the same', 'Unrelated', 'The molecular formula is always larger', 'option2', 'Ionic compounds exist as lattices, not discrete molecules. The formula unit IS the empirical formula (e.g., NaCl, not Na$_2$Cl$_2$).', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The molecular formula can be determined if we know', 'The empirical formula AND the molar mass', 'Only the empirical formula', 'Only the molar mass', 'Only the density', 'option1', '$n = M_{molar}/M_{empirical}$. Both pieces of information are needed.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound contains $C = 92.3\%$ and $H = 7.7\%$. Its empirical formula is', 'C$_6$H$_6$', 'CH$_2$', 'C$_2$H$_2$', 'CH', 'option4', 'C: $92.3/12 = 7.69$. H: $7.7/1 = 7.7$. Ratio $= 1:1$. EF $= \text{CH}$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The empirical formula CH has molar mass $78\;\text{g/mol}$. The molecular formula is', 'CH', 'C$_2$H$_2$', 'C$_6$H$_6$', 'C$_3$H$_3$', 'option3', 'EF mass $= 13$. $n = 78/13 = 6$. MF $= \text{C}_6\text{H}_6$ (benzene).', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound has $C = 54.5\%$, $H = 9.1\%$, $O = 36.4\%$. The empirical formula is', 'CHO', 'C$_2$H$_4$O', 'C$_3$H$_6$O$_2$', 'CH$_2$O', 'option2', 'C: $54.5/12 = 4.54$. H: $9.1/1 = 9.1$. O: $36.4/16 = 2.275$. Divide by $2.275$: $2:4:1$. EF $= \text{C}_2\text{H}_4\text{O}$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$0.30\;\text{g}$ of an organic compound gave $0.44\;\text{g}$ CO$_2$ and $0.18\;\text{g}$ H$_2$O on combustion. The percentages of C and H are', 'C $= 40\%$, H $= 6.7\%$', 'C $= 80\%$, H $= 13.3\%$', 'C $= 60\%$, H $= 10\%$', 'C $= 20\%$, H $= 3.3\%$', 'option1', 'C $= (12/44) \times 0.44/0.30 \times 100 = 40\%$. H $= (2/18) \times 0.18/0.30 \times 100 = 6.67\%$. O $= 53.3\%$ (by difference).', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A hydrocarbon has empirical formula CH$_2$ and molar mass $42\;\text{g/mol}$. The molecular formula is', 'CH$_2$', 'C$_2$H$_4$', 'C$_3$H$_6$', 'C$_4$H$_8$', 'option3', 'EF mass $= 14$. $n = 42/14 = 3$. MF $= \text{C}_3\text{H}_6$ (propene or cyclopropane).', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The vapour density of a compound is $29$. Its molar mass is', '$29\;\text{g/mol}$', '$58\;\text{g/mol}$', '$14.5\;\text{g/mol}$', '$116\;\text{g/mol}$', 'option2', 'Molar mass $= 2 \times$ vapour density $= 2 \times 29 = 58\;\text{g/mol}$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound has $C = 40\%$, $H = 6.7\%$, $O = 53.3\%$ and molar mass $180\;\text{g/mol}$. The molecular formula is', 'CH$_2$O', 'C$_2$H$_4$O$_2$', 'C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$', 'C$_3$H$_6$O$_3$', 'option3', 'EF: C $= 40/12 = 3.33$, H $= 6.7$, O $= 53.3/16 = 3.33$. Ratio $= 1:2:1$. EF $= \text{CH}_2\text{O}$ (mass $30$). $n = 180/30 = 6$. MF $= \text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound contains $N = 30.4\%$ and $O = 69.6\%$. Its empirical formula is', 'NO', 'NO$_2$', 'N$_2$O', 'N$_2$O$_5$', 'option2', 'N: $30.4/14 = 2.17$. O: $69.6/16 = 4.35$. Ratio $= 2.17:4.35 = 1:2$. EF $= \text{NO}_2$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The degree of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) for C$_6$H$_6$ is', '$2$', '$3$', '$4$', '$1$', 'option3', 'IHD $= (2C + 2 - H)/2 = (12 + 2 - 6)/2 = 4$. Benzene: 3 double bonds + 1 ring $= 4$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The degree of unsaturation for C$_4$H$_6$O$_2$ is', '$1$', '$2$', '$3$', '$4$', 'option2', 'IHD $= (2 \times 4 + 2 - 6)/2 = 4/2 = 2$. O does not affect the formula. Could be a diester, acid + double bond, etc.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For a compound C$_x$H$_y$N$_z$, the degree of unsaturation formula is', 'IHD $= (2x + 2 - y)/2$', 'IHD $= (2x + 2 - y - z)/2$', 'IHD $= (2x + 2 - y + z)/2$', 'IHD $= (x + y + z)/2$', 'option3', 'N adds to H count (trivalent): IHD $= (2C + 2 + N - H)/2$. Halogens subtract like H. O has no effect.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound with molecular formula C$_5$H$_8$ has IHD $= 2$. Possible structures include', 'Only an alkane', 'A diene (2 double bonds) or a cycloalkene (1 ring + 1 double bond) or a cyclic diene', 'Only an alkyne', 'No unsaturation', 'option2', 'IHD $= 2$: could be 2 C=C, or 1 C$\equiv$C, or 1 ring + 1 C=C. Multiple structural possibilities.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Kjeldahl method, after digestion, the solution is made alkaline with NaOH to', 'Liberate NH$_3$ from (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$, which is then distilled into a known volume of standard acid', 'Precipitate the nitrogen', 'Oxidize the nitrogen', 'Reduce the nitrogen', 'option1', '(NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$ + 2NaOH $\to$ 2NH$_3$ + Na$_2$SO$_4$ + 2H$_2$O. NH$_3$ is steam-distilled into excess standard HCl or H$_2$SO$_4$.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Mass spectrometry determines the molecular formula by', 'Combustion analysis', 'Measuring the molecular ion peak ($M^+$) which gives the molar mass directly', 'Titration', 'Chromatography', 'option2', 'The $M^+$ peak in the mass spectrum gives the molar mass. High-resolution MS can determine the exact molecular formula.', 'c_quantitative_analysis_organic', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound with empirical formula CH$_2$Cl has molar mass $99\;\text{g/mol}$. The molecular formula is', 'CH$_2$Cl', 'C$_2$H$_4$Cl$_2$', 'C$_3$H$_6$Cl$_3$', 'C$_4$H$_8$Cl$_4$', 'option2', 'EF mass $= 12 + 2 + 35.5 = 49.5$. $n = 99/49.5 = 2$. MF $= \text{C}_2\text{H}_4\text{Cl}_2$.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The molecular formula of a compound with IHD $= 0$ and molecular formula C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$ represents', 'An alkane (saturated hydrocarbon)', 'An alkene', 'An alkyne', 'An aromatic compound', 'option1', 'IHD $= 0$: no rings or double bonds. C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$ is the general formula for alkanes.', 'c_empirical_molecular_formulae', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_tetravalency_hybridization (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Tetravalency of carbon, shapes, hybridization (s and p)
-- ============================================================
('Carbon is tetravalent because it has', '2 valence electrons', '4 valence electrons that can form 4 bonds', '6 valence electrons', '8 valence electrons', 'option2', 'C: $2s^22p^2$. In excited state: $2s^12p^3$ (4 unpaired electrons). Forms 4 covalent bonds.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In methane (CH$_4$), carbon is', '$sp$ hybridized', '$sp^2$ hybridized', '$sp^3$ hybridized', 'Not hybridized', 'option3', '4 equivalent C-H bonds at $109.5°$. $sp^3$: 4 equivalent hybrid orbitals directed tetrahedrally.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In ethene (C$_2$H$_4$), each carbon is', '$sp^3$ hybridized', '$sp^2$ hybridized', '$sp$ hybridized', 'Not hybridized', 'option2', 'Each C: 3 $\sigma$ bonds ($sp^2$) + 1 $\pi$ bond (unhybridized $p$). Bond angle $\approx 120°$.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In ethyne (C$_2$H$_2$), each carbon is', '$sp^3$ hybridized', '$sp^2$ hybridized', '$sp$ hybridized', 'Not hybridized', 'option3', 'Each C: 2 $\sigma$ bonds ($sp$) + 2 $\pi$ bonds (2 unhybridized $p$). Linear, bond angle $180°$.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The shape of methane is', 'Square planar', 'Trigonal planar', 'Tetrahedral', 'Linear', 'option3', '$sp^3$ hybridization gives tetrahedral geometry with bond angles of $109.5°$.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A carbon-carbon single bond ($\sigma$ bond) is formed by', 'Lateral overlap of $p$ orbitals', 'Head-on overlap of hybrid orbitals', 'Ionic bonding', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'option2', '$\sigma$ bonds: head-on overlap along the internuclear axis. Free rotation around $\sigma$ bonds.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A carbon-carbon double bond consists of', 'Two $\sigma$ bonds', 'One $\sigma$ bond and one $\pi$ bond', 'Two $\pi$ bonds', 'One $\sigma$ bond only', 'option2', 'C=C: 1 $\sigma$ (head-on overlap of $sp^2$) + 1 $\pi$ (lateral overlap of unhybridized $p$). No free rotation.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The bond angle in ethene is approximately', '$109.5°$', '$120°$', '$180°$', '$90°$', 'option2', '$sp^2$: trigonal planar, $120°$. All atoms in ethene are in the same plane.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The bond angle in ethyne is', '$109.5°$', '$120°$', '$180°$', '$90°$', 'option3', '$sp$: linear, $180°$. H-C$\equiv$C-H is a linear molecule.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The C-C bond length order is: single _____ double _____ triple.', '$<$, $<$', '$>$, $>$', '$=$, $=$', '$>$, $<$', 'option2', 'Single ($1.54\;\text{\AA}$) $>$ double ($1.34\;\text{\AA}$) $>$ triple ($1.20\;\text{\AA}$). More bonds $=$ shorter and stronger.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The C-H bond in ethyne is shorter and stronger than in ethane because', 'They are the same length', '$sp^3$ has more $s$ character', 'Ethyne has weaker bonds', 'The $sp$ carbon has more $s$ character ($50\%$) than $sp^3$ ($25\%$), holding electrons closer', 'option4', 'More $s$ character $\to$ electrons closer to nucleus $\to$ shorter, stronger bond. $sp$ ($50\%$ s) $>$ $sp^2$ ($33\%$) $>$ $sp^3$ ($25\%$).', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In allene (CH$_2$=C=CH$_2$), the central carbon is', '$sp^3$ hybridized', '$sp^2$ hybridized', '$sp$ hybridized', 'Not hybridized', 'option3', 'Central C: 2 double bonds (2 $\sigma$ + 2 $\pi$). $sp$ hybridized. The two CH$_2$ planes are perpendicular.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of $\sigma$ and $\pi$ bonds in CH$_2$=CH-CH=CH$_2$ is', '$9\sigma$, $2\pi$', '$7\sigma$, $2\pi$', '$8\sigma$, $3\pi$', '$10\sigma$, $2\pi$', 'option1', '3 C-C $\sigma$ + 6 C-H $\sigma$ + 2 C=C $\pi$ $= 9\sigma + 2\pi$.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $\%$ $s$ character in $sp$, $sp^2$, and $sp^3$ hybrid orbitals is', '$50\%$, $25\%$, $33\%$', '$25\%$, $33\%$, $50\%$', '$33\%$, $50\%$, $25\%$', '$50\%$, $33\%$, $25\%$', 'option4', '$sp$: $1s + 1p = 50\%$ s. $sp^2$: $1s + 2p = 33\%$ s. $sp^3$: $1s + 3p = 25\%$ s.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the molecule HC$\equiv$C-CH=CH$_2$, the hybridization of the four carbons (left to right) is', '$sp, sp, sp^2, sp^2$', '$sp, sp, sp^3, sp^2$', '$sp^2, sp^2, sp, sp$', '$sp, sp^2, sp^2, sp^3$', 'option1', 'C1, C2: triple bond ($sp$). C3, C4: double bond ($sp^2$).', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The electronegativity of carbon increases with increasing $s$ character. The correct order is', 'All are equal', '$sp^3 > sp^2 > sp$', '$sp^2 > sp > sp^3$', '$sp > sp^2 > sp^3$', 'option4', 'More $s$ character $\to$ electrons held closer $\to$ more electronegative. $sp$ C is most electronegative.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acidity of C-H bonds follows the order', 'Alkene $>$ alkyne $>$ alkane', 'Alkane $>$ alkene $>$ alkyne', 'All are equally acidic', 'Alkyne $>$ alkene $>$ alkane (sp $>$ sp$^2$ $>$ sp$^3$)', 'option4', 'More $s$ character $\to$ more electronegative C $\to$ stabilizes the carbanion better $\to$ more acidic C-H. Terminal alkynes are weakly acidic.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In benzene, each carbon is $sp^2$ hybridized. The unhybridized $p$ orbitals overlap laterally to form', 'Localized $\pi$ bonds', 'A delocalized $\pi$ electron cloud above and below the ring', 'Only $\sigma$ bonds', 'No bonds', 'option2', '6 parallel $p$ orbitals overlap to form a continuous delocalized $\pi$ system (3 $\pi$ MOs filled with 6 electrons).', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The total number of $\sigma$ and $\pi$ bonds in benzene (C$_6$H$_6$) is', '$12\sigma$, $3\pi$', '$6\sigma$, $3\pi$', '$9\sigma$, $3\pi$', '$12\sigma$, $6\pi$', 'option1', '6 C-C $\sigma$ + 6 C-H $\sigma$ + 3 C=C $\pi$ (delocalized) $= 12\sigma + 3\pi$.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In ketene (CH$_2$=C=O), the hybridization of the two carbons is', 'Both $sp^2$', '$sp^3$ and $sp^2$', '$sp^2$ and $sp$', 'Both $sp$', 'option3', 'CH$_2$= carbon: 3 $\sigma$ bonds ($sp^2$). =C=O carbon: 2 $\sigma$ bonds ($sp$, linear). Similar to allene.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The bond dissociation energy order for C-C bonds is', 'C$\equiv$C $>$ C=C $>$ C-C', 'C-C $>$ C=C $>$ C$\equiv$C', 'All are equal', 'C=C $>$ C$\equiv$C $>$ C-C', 'option1', 'C-C: $347\;\text{kJ}$. C=C: $611\;\text{kJ}$. C$\equiv$C: $837\;\text{kJ}$. More bonds $=$ stronger.', 'c_tetravalency_hybridization', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_classification_isomerism (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Functional groups, homologous series, structural/stereoisomerism
-- ============================================================
('A homologous series is a group of compounds with', 'The same molecular formula', 'The same general formula, differing by CH$_2$, with similar chemical properties', 'Different functional groups', 'No structural similarity', 'option2', 'E.g., alkanes: CH$_4$, C$_2$H$_6$, C$_3$H$_8$... General formula C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$. Each differs by CH$_2$ ($14\;\text{g/mol}$).', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Structural isomers have', 'The same structure', 'Different molecular formulae', 'The same molecular formula but different structural arrangements', 'Different numbers of atoms', 'option3', 'Same molecular formula, different connectivity. E.g., butane and isobutane (C$_4$H$_{10}$).', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The functional group in alcohols is', '$-$COOH', '$-$OH (hydroxyl)', '$-$CHO', '$-$NH$_2$', 'option2', 'Alcohols: R-OH. The $-$OH group determines the chemical properties of the class.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The functional group in aldehydes is', '$-$OH', '$-$COOH', '$-$CHO (formyl/aldehyde)', '$-$CO$-$ (ketone)', 'option3', 'Aldehydes: R-CHO. The carbonyl group is at the terminal carbon.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Stereoisomers have', 'Different structural formulae', 'The same structural formula but different spatial arrangements', 'Different molecular formulae', 'No relationship', 'option2', 'Same connectivity, different 3D arrangement. Types: geometric (cis/trans) and optical (enantiomers).', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chain isomerism arises due to', 'Different positions of the functional group', 'Different functional groups', 'Different arrangements of the carbon chain (straight vs branched)', 'Different spatial arrangements', 'option3', 'E.g., n-butane vs isobutane. Same formula C$_4$H$_{10}$, different carbon skeletons.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Position isomerism arises due to', 'Different spatial arrangements', 'Different carbon chains', 'Different functional groups', 'Different positions of the same functional group on the carbon chain', 'option4', 'E.g., 1-propanol vs 2-propanol. Same chain, same $-$OH, different position.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Functional group isomerism occurs between', 'Compounds with the same molecular formula but different functional groups', 'Compounds with the same functional group', 'Compounds with different molecular formulae', 'Stereoisomers', 'option1', 'E.g., ethanol (C$_2$H$_5$OH) and dimethyl ether (CH$_3$OCH$_3$). Both C$_2$H$_6$O.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Metamerism is a type of isomerism where', 'The spatial arrangement differs', 'The carbon chain differs', 'The functional group differs', 'Different alkyl groups are attached on either side of the same functional group', 'option4', 'E.g., diethyl ether (C$_2$H$_5$OC$_2$H$_5$) vs methyl propyl ether (CH$_3$OC$_3$H$_7$). Both C$_4$H$_{10}$O ethers.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Tautomerism is', 'A dynamic equilibrium between two structural isomers (tautomers) that interconvert rapidly', 'A type of stereoisomerism', 'Permanent structural isomerism', 'Optical isomerism', 'option1', 'Keto-enol tautomerism: CH$_3$COCH$_3$ $\rightleftharpoons$ CH$_3$C(OH)=CH$_2$. Rapid interconversion via proton shift.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Geometric (cis-trans) isomerism requires', 'Free rotation', 'Restricted rotation (C=C or ring) and two different groups on each carbon of the double bond', 'A chiral centre', 'Only single bonds', 'option2', 'Cis: same groups on same side. Trans: same groups on opposite sides. Requires restricted rotation + non-identical substituents.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A chiral centre (stereogenic centre) is a carbon bonded to', 'Two identical groups', 'Four different groups', 'Three identical groups', 'No groups', 'option2', 'A carbon with 4 different substituents is chiral. Its mirror image is non-superimposable (enantiomers).', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of structural isomers of C$_4$H$_{10}$ is', '$1$', '$2$', '$3$', '$4$', 'option2', 'n-Butane (straight chain) and isobutane (2-methylpropane). Two structural isomers.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of structural isomers of C$_5$H$_{12}$ is', '$2$', '$3$', '$4$', '$5$', 'option2', 'n-Pentane, isopentane (2-methylbutane), neopentane (2,2-dimethylpropane). Three isomers.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Enantiomers are', 'Non-superimposable mirror images', 'Superimposable mirror images', 'Structural isomers', 'Geometric isomers', 'option1', 'Enantiomers: same physical properties except optical rotation (equal and opposite). Different biological activity.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A meso compound has chiral centres but is optically inactive because', 'It decomposes light', 'It has no chiral centres', 'It is racemic', 'It has an internal plane of symmetry that makes the molecule achiral overall', 'option4', 'E.g., meso-tartaric acid: 2 chiral centres but a mirror plane makes it superimposable on its mirror image. Net rotation $= 0$.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The maximum number of stereoisomers for a compound with $n$ chiral centres is', '$n$', '$2n$', '$2^n$', '$n^2$', 'option3', 'Maximum $= 2^n$ (may be less if meso forms exist). E.g., 2 chiral centres: max 4 stereoisomers ($2^2$).', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('E/Z nomenclature for geometric isomers uses', 'Alphabetical order', 'Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority rules to assign priorities to substituents', 'Molecular mass', 'Boiling points', 'option2', 'Z (zusammen, together): higher priority groups on same side. E (entgegen, opposite): higher priority groups on opposite sides.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('R and S configuration at a chiral centre is assigned using', 'Alphabetical order', 'CIP priority rules: arrange groups by priority, view from opposite the lowest priority group', 'Molecular mass', 'Boiling point', 'option2', 'R (rectus, clockwise) and S (sinister, counterclockwise) when viewing from opposite the lowest priority group (#4).', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diastereomers are stereoisomers that are', 'Identical', 'Mirror images', 'Structural isomers', 'Non-superimposable and NOT mirror images of each other', 'option4', 'Diastereomers: different physical properties (mp, bp, solubility). E.g., cis/trans isomers, or compounds with multiple chiral centres that are not enantiomers.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Conformational isomers (conformers) of ethane are', 'Different spatial arrangements due to rotation around the C-C single bond', 'Different structural formulae', 'Non-interconvertible', 'Optical isomers', 'option1', 'Staggered (most stable) and eclipsed (least stable) are conformers. They interconvert rapidly at room temperature.', 'c_classification_isomerism', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_nomenclature (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Trivial and IUPAC nomenclature of organic compounds
-- ============================================================
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$CH$_3$ is', 'Propane', 'Butane', 'Pentane', 'Ethane', 'option2', 'Four carbon straight chain alkane: butane. Prefix: but- (4C). Suffix: -ane (alkane).', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC suffix for alcohols is', '-al', '-ol', '-one', '-oic acid', 'option2', 'Alcohols: -ol (e.g., methanol, ethanol, propan-1-ol).', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC suffix for aldehydes is', '-ol', '-al', '-one', '-amine', 'option2', 'Aldehydes: -al (e.g., methanal, ethanal, propanal).', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC suffix for ketones is', '-al', '-ol', '-one', '-oic acid', 'option3', 'Ketones: -one (e.g., propanone, butanone, pentan-2-one).', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC suffix for carboxylic acids is', '-al', '-ol', '-one', '-oic acid', 'option4', 'Carboxylic acids: -oic acid (e.g., methanoic acid, ethanoic acid, propanoic acid).', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In IUPAC nomenclature, the longest continuous carbon chain is called the', 'Substituent', 'Parent chain', 'Side chain', 'Functional group', 'option2', 'Step 1: Find the longest chain containing the principal functional group. This is the parent chain.', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH(CH$_3$)CH$_3$ is', 'Isobutane', '2-Methylpropane', 'Neopentane', 'Butane', 'option2', '3 carbon parent chain with a methyl branch at C2: 2-methylpropane. (Trivial name: isobutane.)', 'c_nomenclature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH$_2$OH is', 'Methanol', 'Ethanol', 'Propanol', 'Ethanal', 'option2', '2-carbon chain with $-$OH: ethanol. (Trivial: ethyl alcohol.)', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$COCH$_3$ is', 'Propanal', 'Propanone', 'Propanol', 'Propanoic acid', 'option2', 'Ketone on 3-carbon chain: propanone. (Trivial: acetone.) No locant needed — only one possible position.', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH=CH$_2$ is', 'Propane', 'Propene', 'Propyne', 'Propanal', 'option2', '3-carbon chain with one double bond: propene. (Trivial: propylene.)', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH$_2$CHO is', 'Propanol', 'Propanone', 'Propanal', 'Propanoic acid', 'option3', '3-carbon chain with $-$CHO at the end: propanal. (Trivial: propionaldehyde.)', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH(OH)CH$_3$ is', 'Propan-1-ol', 'Propan-2-ol', '2-Methylethanol', 'Propanone', 'option2', '$-$OH on C2 of a 3-carbon chain: propan-2-ol. (Trivial: isopropyl alcohol.)', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When numbering the parent chain, the principal functional group should get the', 'Highest possible number', 'Lowest possible number (locant)', 'No number', 'Middle number', 'option2', 'The principal characteristic group gets the lowest locant. Substituents are numbered accordingly.', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC priority order for principal characteristic groups (highest to lowest) is', '$-$NH$_2$ $>$ $-$OH $>$ $-$COOH $>$ $-$CHO', '$-$OH $>$ $-$COOH $>$ $-$CHO $>$ $>$C=O', '$-$COOH $>$ $-$CHO $>$ $>$C=O $>$ $-$OH $>$ $-$NH$_2$', 'All are equal', 'option3', 'IUPAC priority: $-$COOH $>$ $-$SO$_3$H $>$ $-$COOR $>$ $-$COX $>$ $-$CONH$_2$ $>$ $-$CHO $>$ $>$C=O $>$ $-$OH $>$ $-$NH$_2$.', 'c_nomenclature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH(CH$_3$)CH$_2$CH(CH$_3$)CH$_3$ is', '2,4-Dimethylpentane', '2,4-Dimethylhexane', '1,3-Dimethylpentane', '2-Ethyl-4-methylbutane', 'option1', 'Longest chain: 5C (pentane). Methyl groups at C2 and C4: 2,4-dimethylpentane.', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_2$=CHCH$_2$OH is', 'Prop-2-en-1-ol', '2-Propenol', 'Allyl alcohol', 'Propenol', 'option1', '3C chain, double bond at C2, $-$OH at C1: prop-2-en-1-ol. (Trivial: allyl alcohol.) $-$OH gets lowest locant.', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$COCH$_2$CHO is', '4-Oxobutanal', '3-Oxobutanal', 'Butane-1,3-dione', '3-Ketobutanal', 'option2', '$-$CHO (higher priority) at C1, $>$C=O at C3. Parent: butanal. C3 keto as prefix "oxo": 3-oxobutanal.', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of (CH$_3$)$_3$CCH$_2$C(CH$_3$)$_3$ is', '3,3-Di-tert-butylpropane', '2,2,4,4-Tetramethylbutane', 'Di-tert-butylmethane', '2,2,4,4-Tetramethylpentane', 'option4', 'Longest chain: 5C. Four methyl groups at C2,2,4,4: 2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentane.', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of cyclohexanone is', 'Hexanone', 'Cyclohexan-1-one', 'Cyclohexanone', 'Both option2 and option3', 'option4', 'Cyclohexanone: the "1" locant is implied for cyclic ketones. Both "cyclohexanone" and "cyclohexan-1-one" are acceptable.', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$CH(NH$_2$)COOH is', '2-Aminopropanoic acid', 'Alanine', '$\alpha$-Aminopropionic acid', 'All of the above', 'option4', 'IUPAC: 2-aminopropanoic acid. Trivial: alanine ($\alpha$-amino acid). All names refer to the same compound.', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When a compound has both $-$OH and $-$COOH groups, the IUPAC name uses', '$-$COOH as the principal group (suffix: -oic acid) and $-$OH as a substituent (prefix: hydroxy-)', '$-$OH as the principal group', 'Both as suffixes', 'Neither as suffix', 'option1', '$-$COOH has higher priority than $-$OH. E.g., HOCH$_2$COOH: 2-hydroxyethanoic acid (glycolic acid).', 'c_nomenclature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_bond_fission_intermediates (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Homolytic/heterolytic fission, free radicals, carbocations, carbanions
-- ============================================================
('Homolytic bond fission produces', 'Ions', 'Free radicals (each atom gets one electron)', 'Only cations', 'Only anions', 'option2', 'Homolysis: A:B $\to$ A$\cdot$ + B$\cdot$. Each fragment gets one electron. Produces free radicals.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Heterolytic bond fission produces', 'Free radicals', 'A cation and an anion', 'Two neutral species', 'No products', 'option2', 'Heterolysis: A:B $\to$ A$^+$ + B$^-$ (or A$^-$ + B$^+$). Both electrons go to one fragment.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A carbocation is', 'A neutral carbon radical', 'A carbon with a negative charge', 'A carbon with a positive charge (electron-deficient, 6 electrons)', 'A carbon with 8 electrons', 'option3', 'Carbocations: $sp^2$ hybridized, planar, empty $p$ orbital. Electrophilic (electron-seeking).', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A carbanion is', 'A carbon with a positive charge', 'A carbon with a negative charge (electron-rich, 8 electrons)', 'A neutral species', 'A free radical', 'option2', 'Carbanions: $sp^3$ hybridized (pyramidal) with a lone pair. Nucleophilic (electron-donating).', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An electrophile is a species that', 'Donates electrons', 'Accepts electrons (electron-loving)', 'Is neutral always', 'Has no charge', 'option2', 'Electrophiles: electron-deficient. E.g., H$^+$, NO$_2^+$, AlCl$_3$, BF$_3$, carbocations, $>$C=O.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A nucleophile is a species that', 'Accepts electrons', 'Donates electrons (nucleus-loving)', 'Is always positively charged', 'Has no lone pairs', 'option2', 'Nucleophiles: electron-rich. E.g., OH$^-$, CN$^-$, NH$_3$, H$_2$O, carbanions, alkenes ($\pi$ electrons).', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A free radical is', 'A species with an unpaired electron', 'A cation', 'An anion', 'A stable molecule', 'option1', 'Free radicals: neutral, one unpaired electron. Very reactive. E.g., Cl$\cdot$, CH$_3\cdot$, $\cdot$OH.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stability order of carbocations is', 'Methyl $>$ primary $>$ secondary $>$ tertiary', 'Tertiary $>$ secondary $>$ primary $>$ methyl', 'All are equally stable', 'Primary $>$ tertiary', 'option2', 'Alkyl groups are electron-donating (+I effect, hyperconjugation). More alkyl groups $=$ more stabilization of the positive charge.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stability order of free radicals is', 'Tertiary $>$ secondary $>$ primary $>$ methyl', 'Methyl $>$ primary $>$ secondary $>$ tertiary', 'All are equally stable', 'Primary $>$ tertiary', 'option1', 'Same order as carbocations. Hyperconjugation stabilizes the radical centre.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stability order of carbanions is', 'Tertiary $>$ secondary $>$ primary $>$ methyl', 'Methyl $>$ primary $>$ secondary $>$ tertiary', 'All are equally stable', 'Secondary $>$ primary $>$ tertiary $>$ methyl', 'option2', 'Opposite to carbocations. Alkyl groups are electron-donating, destabilizing the negative charge. Methyl carbanion is most stable among simple alkyl carbanions.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A carbocation is stabilized by', 'Electron-withdrawing groups', 'Electron-donating groups (+I, hyperconjugation, resonance)', 'Isolation', 'Low temperature only', 'option2', 'EDGs push electron density towards the positive centre, dispersing the charge and increasing stability.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The allyl carbocation (CH$_2$=CH-CH$_2^+$) is more stable than a primary carbocation because', 'It is cyclic', 'It has more alkyl groups', 'The positive charge is delocalized over two carbons by resonance', 'It has no charge', 'option3', 'CH$_2$=CH-CH$_2^+ \leftrightarrow ^+$CH$_2$-CH=CH$_2$. Resonance stabilization delocalizes the charge.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The benzyl carbocation (C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2^+$) is stabilized by', 'Resonance with the benzene ring', 'Inductive effect only', 'No stabilization', 'Steric effects', 'option1', 'The positive charge delocalizes into the aromatic ring through multiple resonance structures.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hyperconjugation (no-bond resonance) stabilizes carbocations by', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'Overlap of $\pi$ bonds', 'Ionic bonding', 'Overlap of adjacent C-H $\sigma$ bond with the empty $p$ orbital of the carbocation', 'option4', 'The $\sigma$ electrons of $\alpha$-C-H bonds delocalize into the empty $p$ orbital. More $\alpha$-H atoms $=$ more hyperconjugation $=$ more stable.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A carbene (:CH$_2$) is', 'A neutral species with a divalent carbon having two unshared electrons', 'A carbocation', 'A carbanion', 'A free radical', 'option1', 'Carbenes: 6 electrons on C. Can be singlet (paired electrons, $sp^2$) or triplet (unpaired, $sp$). Very reactive intermediates.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A nitrene (:NR) is analogous to a carbene but with', 'Carbon', 'Nitrogen as the electron-deficient centre', 'Oxygen', 'Sulphur', 'option2', 'Nitrenes: R-N: with 6 electrons on N. Generated from azides (R-N$_3 \to$ R-N: + N$_2$). Highly reactive.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The tropylium cation (C$_7$H$_7^+$, cycloheptatrienyl cation) is unusually stable because', 'It is aromatic (6 $\pi$ electrons in a planar cyclic system, satisfying Huckel''s rule)', 'It has 7 carbons', 'It is non-planar', 'It has 8 $\pi$ electrons', 'option1', 'C$_7$H$_7^+$: 7 $sp^2$ carbons, 6 $\pi$ electrons ($4n + 2$, $n = 1$). Aromatic $\to$ exceptional stability.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The cyclopentadienyl anion (C$_5$H$_5^-$) is aromatic because', 'It has 6 $\pi$ electrons ($4n + 2$, $n = 1$) in a planar cyclic system', 'It has 4 $\pi$ electrons', 'It is not cyclic', 'It has 8 $\pi$ electrons', 'option1', 'C$_5$H$_5^-$: 5 $sp^2$ carbons, each contributing 1 $p$ electron + 1 extra from the anion $= 6\pi$ electrons. Aromatic.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Among CH$_3^+$, (CH$_3$)$_2$CH$^+$, (CH$_3$)$_3$C$^+$, and C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2^+$, the most stable carbocation is', 'CH$_3^+$', '(CH$_3$)$_2$CH$^+$', '(CH$_3$)$_3$C$^+$', 'C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2^+$ (benzyl)', 'option3', '(CH$_3$)$_3$C$^+$ (tertiary) is most stable among simple alkyl carbocations. Benzyl is comparable but tertiary wins due to 3 alkyl groups + 9 hyperconjugative structures.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A 1,2-hydride shift in carbocations occurs because', 'A less stable carbocation rearranges to a more stable one by migration of H$^-$ to the adjacent positive centre', 'Carbocations are stable', 'No rearrangement occurs', 'Only methyl shifts occur', 'option1', 'E.g., primary $\to$ secondary or secondary $\to$ tertiary. The migrating group (H$^-$ or CH$_3^-$) moves with its bonding electrons.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The vinyl cation (CH$_2$=CH$^+$) is less stable than a typical secondary carbocation because', 'The positive charge is on an $sp^2$ carbon (more electronegative than $sp^2$ in alkyl carbocations) and there is no hyperconjugation from the vinyl side', 'It has more alkyl groups', 'It is aromatic', 'It has resonance stabilization', 'option1', 'Vinyl cation: $sp$ hybridized positive carbon. Higher $s$ character $=$ more electronegative $=$ less willing to bear positive charge. Very unstable.', 'c_bond_fission_intermediates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_electronic_effects (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Inductive, electromeric, resonance, hyperconjugation
-- ============================================================
('The inductive effect is', 'Only in aromatic compounds', 'The delocalization of $\pi$ electrons', 'A temporary effect', 'The permanent displacement of $\sigma$ electrons along a chain due to electronegativity differences', 'option4', 'Inductive effect: transmitted through $\sigma$ bonds. Decreases rapidly with distance. $-$I (electron-withdrawing) or $+$I (electron-donating).', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Groups with $-$I (electron-withdrawing inductive) effect include', '$-$NH$_2$, $-$OH, $-$CH$_3$', '$-$NO$_2$, $-$CN, $-$COOH, $-$F, $-$Cl', '$-$C$_2$H$_5$, $-$(CH$_3$)$_3$C', 'Only metals', 'option2', '$-$I groups: halogens, $-$NO$_2$, $-$CN, $-$COOH, $-$OR, $-$OH, $-$NH$_2$ (when considering inductive effect only, all are $-$I except alkyl groups).', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Alkyl groups ($-$CH$_3$, $-$C$_2$H$_5$) show', '$-$I effect', '$+$I effect (electron-donating)', 'No effect', 'Only resonance effect', 'option2', 'Alkyl groups push electron density towards the attached atom. $+$I effect stabilizes carbocations and destabilizes carbanions.', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Resonance (mesomerism) involves', 'Only inductive effects', 'Movement of $\sigma$ electrons', 'Delocalization of $\pi$ electrons or lone pairs over adjacent atoms', 'No electron movement', 'option3', 'Resonance: $\pi$ electrons or lone pairs delocalize through conjugated systems. Represented by resonance structures.', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hyperconjugation involves', 'Delocalization of $\sigma$ (C-H) bond electrons into an adjacent empty or partially filled $p$ orbital', 'Delocalization of $\pi$ electrons', 'Inductive effect', 'Ionic bonding', 'option1', 'Also called "no-bond resonance" or "$\sigma$-$\pi$ conjugation". Stabilizes carbocations, free radicals, and alkenes.', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The electromeric effect is', 'A permanent effect', 'A temporary shift of $\pi$ electrons to one atom under the influence of an attacking reagent', 'The same as inductive effect', 'Only in ionic compounds', 'option2', 'Electromeric effect: exists only in the presence of the reagent. $+$E: $\pi$ electrons shift towards the attacking electrophile.', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $+$M (positive mesomeric/resonance) effect is shown by groups that', 'Have no lone pairs', 'Withdraw electron density', 'Donate electron density to the conjugated system through resonance', 'Are always electron-withdrawing', 'option3', '$+$M groups: $-$OH, $-$OR, $-$NH$_2$, $-$NHR, $-$X (halogens). They donate lone pairs into the $\pi$ system.', 'c_electronic_effects', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $-$M (negative mesomeric) effect is shown by groups that', 'Donate electrons', 'Withdraw electron density from the conjugated system through resonance', 'Have no effect', 'Are always electron-donating', 'option2', '$-$M groups: $-$NO$_2$, $-$CN, $-$CHO, $-$COOH, $-$COR. They withdraw $\pi$ electron density.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The inductive effect of halogens follows the order', 'Cl $>$ F $>$ Br $>$ I', 'I $>$ Br $>$ Cl $>$ F', 'All are equal', 'F $>$ Cl $>$ Br $>$ I ($-$I effect decreases with size)', 'option4', '$-$I effect $\propto$ electronegativity. F is most electronegative $\to$ strongest $-$I effect.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acidity of carboxylic acids is enhanced by', 'Electron-donating groups', 'Electron-withdrawing groups ($-$I, $-$M) on the $\alpha$-carbon', 'Alkyl groups', 'No substituents', 'option2', 'EWGs stabilize the carboxylate anion (RCOO$^-$) by dispersing the negative charge. E.g., ClCH$_2$COOH $>$ CH$_3$COOH.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acid strength order among CH$_3$COOH, ClCH$_2$COOH, Cl$_2$CHCOOH, and Cl$_3$CCOOH is', 'CH$_3$COOH $>$ ClCH$_2$COOH $>$ Cl$_2$CHCOOH $>$ Cl$_3$CCOOH', 'Cl$_3$CCOOH $>$ Cl$_2$CHCOOH $>$ ClCH$_2$COOH $>$ CH$_3$COOH', 'All are equally acidic', 'CH$_3$COOH is the strongest', 'option2', 'More Cl atoms $=$ stronger $-$I effect $=$ better stabilization of COO$^-$ $=$ stronger acid.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Resonance stabilization of the carboxylate ion (RCOO$^-$) involves', 'Delocalization on carbon', 'Localization on one oxygen', 'No delocalization', 'Delocalization of the negative charge over both oxygen atoms equally', 'option4', 'RCOO$^-$: two equivalent resonance structures. Each C-O bond has bond order $1.5$. Charge is equally shared.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The basicity of amines follows the order (in aqueous solution)', 'Tertiary $>$ secondary $>$ primary always', 'Secondary $>$ tertiary $>$ primary $>$ NH$_3$ (approximately)', 'Primary $>$ secondary $>$ tertiary always', 'All are equally basic', 'option2', 'In water: solvation effects matter. 2° amines balance +I effect and solvation best. 3° amines are poorly solvated.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aniline (C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$) is a weaker base than cyclohexylamine because', 'Aniline has no lone pair', 'The lone pair on N in aniline is delocalized into the benzene ring ($+$M effect), reducing its availability', 'Cyclohexylamine is aromatic', 'Aniline is more basic', 'option2', 'Resonance delocalizes the N lone pair into the ring, making it less available for protonation. Cyclohexylamine has no such delocalization.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol (C$_6$H$_5$OH) is more acidic than cyclohexanol because', 'The phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilized (negative charge delocalized into the ring)', 'Phenol has a weaker O-H bond', 'Cyclohexanol is aromatic', 'Phenol is a base', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$O$^-$: 5 resonance structures (charge on O and ring carbons). Cyclohexyl-O$^-$ has no such stabilization.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The order of $+$M effect of halogens attached to a benzene ring is', 'I $>$ Br $>$ Cl $>$ F', 'F $>$ Cl $>$ Br $>$ I', 'All are equal', 'Cl $>$ F $>$ Br $>$ I', 'option2', '$+$M effect depends on orbital overlap with the ring $\pi$ system. F ($2p$) overlaps best with C ($2p$). Larger halogens overlap poorly.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Halogens are ortho/para directors in electrophilic aromatic substitution despite being deactivating because', 'They are $-$M', 'They are $+$I', 'They are $-$I (deactivating) but $+$M (directing to ortho/para). The $+$M effect dominates in directing.', 'They have no effect on direction', 'option3', 'Net effect: deactivating ($-$I $>$ $+$M in magnitude). But for directing: $+$M places electron density at ortho/para positions.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Baker-Nathan effect (reverse hyperconjugation order) is observed in', 'All reactions', 'Certain reactions where the order of reactivity is CH$_3$ $>$ C$_2$H$_5$ $>$ (CH$_3$)$_2$CH $>$ (CH$_3$)$_3$C, opposite to the +I order', 'No reactions', 'Only ionic reactions', 'option2', 'In some SN reactions and electrophilic substitutions, hyperconjugation (more $\alpha$-H $=$ more stabilization) dominates over +I effect.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Cross-conjugation in divinyl ketone (CH$_2$=CH-CO-CH=CH$_2$) means', 'The two vinyl groups are conjugated with the C=O but not with each other through the carbonyl', 'All bonds are conjugated', 'No conjugation exists', 'Only one vinyl group is conjugated', 'option1', 'In cross-conjugation, two $\pi$ systems are both conjugated with a central group but not directly with each other. Less stabilization than linear conjugation.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The dipole moment of chlorobenzene ($1.69\;\text{D}$) is less than that of cyclohexyl chloride ($2.2\;\text{D}$) because', 'The $+$M effect of Cl partially opposes the $-$I effect in chlorobenzene, reducing the net dipole', 'Chlorobenzene has no dipole', 'Cyclohexyl chloride is aromatic', 'The C-Cl bond is weaker in chlorobenzene', 'option1', 'In C$_6$H$_5$Cl: $-$I pulls electrons towards Cl, but $+$M pushes lone pair back into the ring. Partial cancellation reduces the dipole.', 'c_electronic_effects', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of hyperconjugative structures for the ethyl cation (CH$_3$CH$_2^+$) is', '$1$', '$3$', '$2$', '$6$', 'option2', 'CH$_3$CH$_2^+$: 3 $\alpha$-C-H bonds on the adjacent CH$_3$ can hyperconjugate with the empty $p$ orbital. 3 hyperconjugative (no-bond resonance) structures.', 'c_electronic_effects', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_organic_reaction_types (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Substitution, addition, elimination, rearrangement
-- ============================================================
('In a substitution reaction', 'Two molecules combine', 'An atom or group in a molecule is replaced by another atom or group', 'A molecule loses atoms to form a double bond', 'The carbon skeleton rearranges', 'option2', 'E.g., CH$_4$ + Cl$_2 \to$ CH$_3$Cl + HCl. H is substituted by Cl.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an addition reaction', 'Two or more molecules combine to form a single product', 'An atom is replaced', 'A molecule loses atoms', 'The skeleton rearranges', 'option1', 'E.g., CH$_2$=CH$_2$ + HBr $\to$ CH$_3$CH$_2$Br. The $\pi$ bond breaks and new $\sigma$ bonds form.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an elimination reaction', 'Two atoms or groups are removed from adjacent carbons to form a double bond', 'An atom is added', 'An atom is substituted', 'The skeleton rearranges', 'option1', 'E.g., CH$_3$CH$_2$Br + KOH(alc) $\to$ CH$_2$=CH$_2$ + KBr + H$_2$O. Loss of HBr forms a $\pi$ bond.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A rearrangement reaction involves', 'Addition of a new group', 'Migration of an atom or group within the same molecule to form a structural isomer', 'Removal of a group', 'No change in structure', 'option2', 'E.g., 1,2-hydride shift, 1,2-methyl shift, pinacol-pinacolone rearrangement, Beckmann rearrangement.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nucleophilic substitution (S$_N$) involves', 'Attack by a nucleophile on an electrophilic carbon, displacing a leaving group', 'Attack by an electrophile', 'Free radical attack', 'No attack', 'option1', 'E.g., CH$_3$Br + OH$^- \to$ CH$_3$OH + Br$^-$. OH$^-$ (nucleophile) replaces Br$^-$ (leaving group).', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electrophilic addition occurs typically in', 'Alkanes', 'Alkenes and alkynes (across the $\pi$ bond)', 'Aromatic compounds', 'Ethers', 'option2', 'The electron-rich $\pi$ bond attacks the electrophile. E.g., CH$_2$=CH$_2$ + HBr $\to$ CH$_3$CH$_2$Br.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS) occurs in', 'Alkanes', 'Alkenes', 'Benzene and aromatic compounds', 'Alkynes', 'option3', 'Benzene reacts with electrophiles (NO$_2^+$, Cl$^+$, RCO$^+$) via substitution (not addition) to preserve aromaticity.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('S$_N$1 reactions proceed via', 'A free radical mechanism', 'A one-step concerted mechanism', 'A two-step mechanism: first the leaving group departs (forming a carbocation), then the nucleophile attacks', 'No mechanism', 'option3', 'S$_N$1: rate $= k[\text{substrate}]$. Unimolecular. Favoured by tertiary substrates, polar protic solvents, weak nucleophiles.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('S$_N$2 reactions proceed via', 'An elimination mechanism', 'A two-step mechanism via carbocation', 'A free radical mechanism', 'A one-step concerted mechanism with backside attack by the nucleophile', 'option4', 'S$_N$2: rate $= k[\text{substrate}][\text{nucleophile}]$. Bimolecular. Inversion of configuration. Favoured by primary substrates, strong nucleophiles, polar aprotic solvents.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('E1 elimination is favoured by', 'Non-polar solvents', 'Primary substrates, strong bases', 'Low temperature', 'Tertiary substrates, polar protic solvents, weak bases, high temperature', 'option4', 'E1: unimolecular, via carbocation (like S$_N$1). Competes with S$_N$1. Higher temperature favours elimination over substitution.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('E2 elimination is favoured by', 'Strong bulky bases, high temperature, secondary/tertiary substrates', 'Weak nucleophiles, low temperature', 'Primary substrates only', 'Polar protic solvents', 'option1', 'E2: bimolecular, concerted, anti-periplanar geometry required. Bulky bases (e.g., $t$-BuOK) favour E2 over S$_N$2.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Markownikoff''s rule states that in the addition of HX to an unsymmetrical alkene', 'H adds to the carbon with fewer H atoms', 'H adds to the carbon with more H atoms (forming the more stable carbocation)', 'X adds to the carbon with more H atoms', 'Addition is random', 'option2', 'H$^+$ adds to the less substituted carbon, X$^-$ to the more substituted. The more stable (more substituted) carbocation intermediate forms.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Anti-Markownikoff addition (peroxide effect/Kharasch effect) occurs in', 'Addition of HCl', 'All HX additions', 'Addition of HBr to alkenes in the presence of peroxides (free radical mechanism)', 'Addition of HI', 'option3', 'Peroxides generate Br$\cdot$ radicals. Br$\cdot$ adds to the less substituted carbon (more stable radical intermediate). Only works for HBr.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Free radical substitution of alkanes follows the mechanism', 'Initiation $\to$ propagation $\to$ termination', 'Only initiation', 'Only termination', 'Ionic mechanism', 'option1', 'Initiation: Cl$_2 \xrightarrow{h\nu}$ 2Cl$\cdot$. Propagation: Cl$\cdot$ + CH$_4 \to$ CH$_3\cdot$ + HCl; CH$_3\cdot$ + Cl$_2 \to$ CH$_3$Cl + Cl$\cdot$. Termination: radical coupling.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Zaitsev''s (Saytzeff''s) rule states that in elimination reactions', 'The more substituted (more stable) alkene is the major product', 'The less substituted alkene is the major product', 'Only one product forms', 'No alkene forms', 'option1', 'E.g., 2-bromobutane + KOH(alc) $\to$ but-2-ene (major, more substituted) + but-1-ene (minor).', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hofmann elimination (using bulky bases) gives', 'The more substituted alkene (Zaitsev product)', 'The less substituted alkene (Hofmann product)', 'No alkene', 'A substitution product', 'option2', 'Bulky bases (e.g., $t$-BuOK) abstract the less hindered $\beta$-H, giving the less substituted (Hofmann) alkene.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Walden inversion in S$_N$2 reactions means', 'The configuration is retained', 'The configuration at the chiral centre is inverted (like an umbrella flipping)', 'Racemization occurs', 'No stereochemical change', 'option2', 'Backside attack by the nucleophile inverts the stereochemistry. S$_N$2 always gives inversion.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('S$_N$1 reactions at a chiral centre give', 'Complete inversion', 'Complete retention', 'Racemization (with slight excess of inversion)', 'No products', 'option3', 'Planar carbocation intermediate: nucleophile attacks from both sides. Slight excess inversion due to ion-pair shielding.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The competition between S$_N$2 and E2 is controlled by', 'Only solvent', 'Only temperature', 'The strength and size of the base/nucleophile: strong small nucleophile favours S$_N$2; strong bulky base favours E2', 'Only substrate', 'option3', 'Strong, unhindered nucleophile (e.g., CH$_3$O$^-$): S$_N$2. Strong, bulky base (e.g., $t$-BuO$^-$): E2. Temperature also matters (higher $T$ favours E).', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In electrophilic aromatic substitution, the arenium ion (Wheland intermediate) is', 'An anion', 'A resonance-stabilized carbocation formed by addition of the electrophile to the ring', 'A free radical', 'A neutral species', 'option2', 'The electrophile adds to one carbon, disrupting aromaticity temporarily. The arenium ion is stabilized by resonance (3 structures). Loss of H$^+$ restores aromaticity.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Hammond postulate states that', 'All transition states are identical', 'The transition state of an exothermic step resembles the reactants; for an endothermic step, it resembles the products', 'Transition states cannot be studied', 'Products always resemble reactants', 'option2', 'Useful for predicting selectivity: in endothermic steps (e.g., H-abstraction by Cl$\cdot$), the TS resembles the radical product, so more stable radical $=$ lower barrier.', 'c_organic_reaction_types', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_hc_classification (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Classification, isomerism, IUPAC nomenclature, general properties
-- ============================================================
('Hydrocarbons are classified into', 'Saturated (alkanes) and unsaturated (alkenes, alkynes, aromatics)', 'Only alkanes', 'Only aromatics', 'Metals and non-metals', 'option1', 'Saturated: only C-C single bonds. Unsaturated: contain C=C, C$\equiv$C, or aromatic rings.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The general formula of alkanes is', 'C$_n$H$_{2n}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n-2}$', 'C$_n$H$_n$', 'option2', 'Alkanes (paraffins): C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$. Saturated, $sp^3$ hybridized, tetrahedral geometry.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The general formula of alkenes is', 'C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n-2}$', 'C$_n$H$_n$', 'option2', 'Alkenes (olefins): C$_n$H$_{2n}$. One C=C double bond. $sp^2$ hybridized at the double bond.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The general formula of alkynes is', 'C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n-2}$', 'C$_n$H$_n$', 'option3', 'Alkynes (acetylenes): C$_n$H$_{2n-2}$. One C$\equiv$C triple bond. $sp$ hybridized.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Cycloalkanes have the general formula', 'C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n-2}$', 'C$_n$H$_{2n+4}$', 'option2', 'Same as alkenes (C$_n$H$_{2n}$) but no double bond — the ring accounts for the "missing" 2H.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aromatic hydrocarbons are characterized by', 'Always 6 carbons', 'Only single bonds', 'No $\pi$ electrons', 'A planar cyclic structure with $(4n+2)$ $\pi$ electrons (Huckel''s rule)', 'option4', 'Aromaticity: planar, cyclic, conjugated, $(4n+2)$ $\pi$ electrons. Benzene ($n=1$, 6$\pi$e) is the prototype.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aliphatic hydrocarbons are', 'Only branched', 'Only aromatic', 'Only cyclic', 'Open-chain (acyclic) or non-aromatic cyclic hydrocarbons', 'option4', 'Aliphatic: includes alkanes, alkenes, alkynes, and cycloalkanes. Excludes aromatic compounds.', 'c_hc_classification', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of structural isomers of C$_4$H$_8$ that are acyclic alkenes is', '$2$', '$3$', '$4$', '$5$', 'option2', 'But-1-ene, but-2-ene, and 2-methylpropene (isobutylene). Three structural isomers. (cis/trans but-2-ene are stereoisomers, not structural.)', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wurtz reaction is used to prepare', 'Alkenes', 'Higher alkanes from alkyl halides using Na metal', 'Alkynes', 'Aromatic compounds', 'option2', '2R-X + 2Na $\to$ R-R + 2NaX. E.g., 2CH$_3$Br + 2Na $\to$ C$_2$H$_6$ + 2NaBr. Gives symmetrical alkanes.', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Kolbe''s electrolysis produces alkanes by', 'Electrolysis of sodium or potassium salts of carboxylic acids', 'Reduction of alkenes', 'Wurtz reaction', 'Grignard reaction', 'option1', '2RCOONa + 2H$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{electrolysis}}$ R-R + 2CO$_2$ + 2NaOH + H$_2$. At anode: 2RCOO$^- \to$ R-R + 2CO$_2$ + 2e$^-$.', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Decarboxylation of sodium salts of carboxylic acids with soda lime gives', 'Alkanes with one fewer carbon', 'Alkenes', 'Alkynes', 'Alcohols', 'option1', 'RCOONa + NaOH/CaO $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ R-H + Na$_2$CO$_3$. Loses one carbon as CO$_2$.', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sabatier-Senderens reaction involves', 'Catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes/alkynes using Ni, Pd, or Pt catalyst', 'Dehydration', 'Halogenation', 'Oxidation', 'option1', 'R-CH=CH-R + H$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{Ni/Pt/Pd}}$ R-CH$_2$-CH$_2$-R. Syn addition of H$_2$.', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Corey-House synthesis uses', 'Lithium dialkylcuprate (R$_2$CuLi) with alkyl halides to form alkanes', 'Grignard reagent', 'Wurtz reaction', 'Kolbe electrolysis', 'option1', 'R$_2$CuLi + R''X $\to$ R-R'' + RCu + LiX. Advantage: can make unsymmetrical alkanes (unlike Wurtz).', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Wurtz reaction gives a mixture of products when two different alkyl halides are used because', 'Three possible coupling products form: R-R, R''-R'', and R-R'' (cross-coupling)', 'Only one product forms', 'No reaction occurs', 'Only the cross product forms', 'option1', 'CH$_3$Br + C$_2$H$_5$Br + 2Na $\to$ C$_2$H$_6$ + C$_4$H$_{10}$ + C$_3$H$_8$ (mixture). Poor selectivity.', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Birch reduction of benzene with Na/liquid NH$_3$/alcohol gives', 'Cyclohexene', 'Cyclohexane', '1,4-Cyclohexadiene', 'No reaction', 'option3', 'Partial reduction: benzene $\to$ 1,4-cyclohexadiene (unconjugated diene). Not full reduction to cyclohexane.', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Clemmensen reduction converts', 'Alcohols to alkenes', 'Alkenes to alkanes', 'Carbonyl groups to CH$_2$ using Zn-Hg/conc. HCl', 'Amines to amides', 'option3', 'R-CO-R'' $\xrightarrow{\text{Zn-Hg/HCl}}$ R-CH$_2$-R''. Reduces C=O to CH$_2$. Used for acid-stable compounds.', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Wolff-Kishner reduction converts', 'Carbonyl groups to CH$_2$ using NH$_2$NH$_2$/KOH/ethylene glycol at high temperature', 'Alkenes to alkanes', 'Alcohols to alkenes', 'Amines to amides', 'option1', 'R-CO-R'' $\xrightarrow{\text{NH}_2\text{NH}_2/\text{KOH}}$ R-CH$_2$-R''. Complementary to Clemmensen (used for base-stable compounds).', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Frankland reaction involves', 'Wurtz coupling', 'Heating alkyl halides with zinc to form dialkylzinc compounds', 'Grignard reaction', 'Kolbe electrolysis', 'option2', '2RX + 2Zn $\to$ R$_2$Zn + ZnX$_2$. Dialkylzinc compounds are organometallic reagents.', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hydroboration-oxidation of alkenes gives', 'Anti-Markownikoff alcohols (syn addition)', 'Markownikoff alcohols', 'Ketones', 'Carboxylic acids', 'option1', 'R-CH=CH$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{BH}_3/\text{THF}}$ $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}_2/\text{NaOH}}$ R-CH$_2$-CH$_2$OH. Anti-Markownikoff, syn addition.', 'c_hc_classification', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Grignard reagents (RMgX) react with water to give', 'Alkenes', 'Alcohols', 'Alkanes (R-H)', 'Ethers', 'option3', 'RMgX + H$_2$O $\to$ R-H + Mg(OH)X. The carbanion (R$^-$) abstracts a proton from water.', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Catalytic hydrogenation of alkynes can be stopped at the alkene stage using', 'No catalyst can do this', 'Pt catalyst', 'Ni catalyst at high pressure', 'Lindlar''s catalyst (Pd/CaCO$_3$/quinoline) — gives cis-alkene', 'option4', 'Lindlar''s catalyst is "poisoned" (deactivated) to prevent further reduction. Gives cis (syn) addition product.', 'c_hc_classification', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_alkanes (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Conformations (sawhorse, Newman), mechanism of halogenation
-- ============================================================
('The most stable conformation of ethane is', 'Eclipsed', 'Staggered', 'Gauche', 'Skew', 'option2', 'Staggered: H atoms are maximally separated ($60°$ dihedral). Torsional strain is minimized. $\sim 12\;\text{kJ/mol}$ more stable than eclipsed.', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a Newman projection, the front carbon is represented by', 'A circle', 'A point (intersection of three bonds)', 'A square', 'A triangle', 'option2', 'Front carbon: point with 3 bonds radiating. Back carbon: circle with 3 bonds. Viewer looks along the C-C bond axis.', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Free radical halogenation of alkanes follows the order of reactivity', 'F$_2 >$ Cl$_2 >$ Br$_2 >$ I$_2$', 'I$_2 >$ Br$_2 >$ Cl$_2 >$ F$_2$', 'Cl$_2 >$ F$_2 >$ Br$_2 >$ I$_2$', 'All are equally reactive', 'option1', 'F$_2$: explosive (very exothermic). Cl$_2$: moderate. Br$_2$: selective. I$_2$: does not react (endothermic).', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The halogenation of alkanes is a', 'Nucleophilic substitution', 'Electrophilic addition', 'Free radical substitution', 'Elimination reaction', 'option3', 'Initiated by UV light or heat. Mechanism: initiation (homolysis of X$_2$), propagation (chain), termination (radical coupling).', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a sawhorse projection, the C-C bond is drawn as', 'A vertical line', 'A diagonal line viewed from an angle', 'A horizontal line', 'A circle', 'option2', 'Sawhorse: 3D perspective of the C-C bond. Front carbon is lower-left, back carbon is upper-right.', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Alkanes are relatively unreactive because', 'C-C and C-H bonds are strong and non-polar, with no functional group', 'They have weak bonds', 'They are ionic', 'They have double bonds', 'option1', 'C-C ($347\;\text{kJ}$) and C-H ($414\;\text{kJ}$) are strong $\sigma$ bonds. No polar functional group to attract reagents.', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The combustion of alkanes produces', 'No products', 'Only CO', 'Only H$_2$', 'CO$_2$ and H$_2$O (complete combustion)', 'option4', 'C$_n$H$_{2n+2}$ + $(3n+1)/2$ O$_2 \to$ $n$CO$_2$ + $(n+1)$H$_2$O. Highly exothermic. Basis of fossil fuel use.', 'c_alkanes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the chlorination of methane, the propagation steps are', 'Only Cl$_2 \to$ 2Cl$\cdot$', 'Cl$\cdot$ + CH$_4 \to$ CH$_3\cdot$ + HCl; CH$_3\cdot$ + Cl$_2 \to$ CH$_3$Cl + Cl$\cdot$', 'CH$_4 \to$ CH$_3^+$ + H$^-$', 'No propagation occurs', 'option2', 'Each propagation step regenerates a radical, sustaining the chain. The chain continues until termination.', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The selectivity of bromination over chlorination is because', 'Br$\cdot$ is more reactive', 'Br$\cdot$ is less reactive and more selective — preferentially abstracts tertiary H (more stable radical)', 'Cl$\cdot$ is more selective', 'Both are equally selective', 'option2', 'Reactivity-selectivity principle: less reactive Br$\cdot$ has a later (more product-like) transition state, so it discriminates between H types better.', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The relative rates of H-abstraction by Cl$\cdot$ are approximately: 1° : 2° : 3° $=$', '$1 : 1 : 1$', '$1 : 3.9 : 5.2$', '$1 : 82 : 1600$', '$5.2 : 3.9 : 1$', 'option2', 'Chlorination: low selectivity. 1° : 2° : 3° $\approx 1 : 3.9 : 5.2$. Bromination: high selectivity $\approx 1 : 82 : 1600$.', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gauche conformation of butane has', 'No strain', 'The methyl groups at $180°$', 'The methyl groups eclipsed', 'The two methyl groups at $60°$ dihedral angle (higher energy than anti)', 'option4', 'Gauche: $60°$ dihedral. Steric strain between CH$_3$ groups. $\sim 3.8\;\text{kJ/mol}$ less stable than anti.', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The anti conformation of butane is the most stable because', 'It has the most strain', 'The methyl groups are eclipsed', 'The two methyl groups are at $180°$ dihedral — maximum separation, minimum steric strain', 'It is the least stable', 'option3', 'Anti: $180°$ dihedral. No steric interaction between the bulky CH$_3$ groups. Global energy minimum.', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Pyrolysis (cracking) of alkanes involves', 'Electrolysis', 'Cooling', 'Dissolving in water', 'Heating to high temperatures ($700$-$1200°\text{C}$) to break C-C bonds, producing smaller alkanes, alkenes, and H$_2$', 'option4', 'Thermal cracking: homolytic C-C bond cleavage. Catalytic cracking: uses zeolite catalysts at lower temperatures.', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Isomerization of n-alkanes to branched alkanes is catalyzed by', 'No catalyst needed', 'NaOH', 'KMnO$_4$', 'AlCl$_3$/HCl or Pt catalyst', 'option4', 'n-Butane $\xrightarrow{\text{AlCl}_3/\text{HCl}}$ isobutane. Important in petroleum refining (increases octane number).', 'c_alkanes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy barrier to rotation in ethane ($\sim 12\;\text{kJ/mol}$) is due to', 'No barrier exists', 'Steric strain', 'Angle strain', 'Torsional strain from repulsion between bonding electrons in eclipsed C-H bonds', 'option4', 'Torsional strain: electron-electron repulsion between eclipsed $\sigma$ bonds. Small but measurable.', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Monochlorination of 2-methylpropane (isobutane) gives two products. The ratio of 1-chloro-2-methylpropane to 2-chloro-2-methylpropane is approximately', '$1:1$', '$9:1$ (statistical) but actually $\sim 2:1$ (accounting for reactivity)', '$1:9$', '$3:1$', 'option2', '9 equivalent 1° H vs 1 tertiary H. Statistical ratio $= 9:1$. Reactivity: 3° is $5.2\times$ faster. Actual $\approx 9:(1 \times 5.2) = 9:5.2 \approx 1.7:1 \approx 2:1$.', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The conformational energy diagram of butane shows _____ energy maxima and _____ energy minima between $0°$ and $360°$.', '2 maxima, 2 minima', '3 maxima, 3 minima', '4 maxima, 4 minima', '1 maximum, 1 minimum', 'option2', '$0°$-$360°$: 3 eclipsed conformations (maxima) and 3 staggered conformations (minima: 1 anti + 2 gauche).', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The highest energy conformation of butane is the', 'Anti', 'Gauche', 'Fully eclipsed (CH$_3$ groups eclipsed, $0°$ dihedral)', 'Partially eclipsed', 'option3', 'Fully eclipsed ($0°$): maximum steric + torsional strain. CH$_3$-CH$_3$ eclipsing. $\sim 19\;\text{kJ/mol}$ above anti.', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the chlorination of methane, the chain length (number of propagation cycles before termination) depends on', 'The ratio of Cl$_2$ to CH$_4$ and the intensity of light', 'Only temperature', 'Only pressure', 'Nothing — it is always the same', 'option1', 'Higher [Cl$_2$] and stronger light increase initiation rate. Chain length $=$ propagation rate / termination rate.', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Cyclopropane is more reactive than cyclohexane because', 'It is aromatic', 'It has significant angle strain ($60°$ vs ideal $109.5°$) and torsional strain (all eclipsed)', 'It has no strain', 'Cyclohexane is more strained', 'option2', 'Cyclopropane: $60°$ bond angles (far from $109.5°$). All C-H bonds are eclipsed. Ring strain $\sim 115\;\text{kJ/mol}$. Opens easily.', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The chair conformation of cyclohexane is the most stable because', 'It has no angle strain ($109.5°$) and no torsional strain (all bonds staggered)', 'It is planar', 'It has eclipsed bonds', 'It has maximum strain', 'option1', 'Chair: all C-C-C angles $\approx 111°$ (close to $109.5°$). All adjacent C-H bonds are staggered. Strain-free.', 'c_alkanes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_alkenes (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Geometrical isomerism, electrophilic addition, Markownikoff, ozonolysis
-- ============================================================
('Alkenes undergo electrophilic addition because', 'They undergo only substitution', 'They have no electrons', 'They are ionic', 'The $\pi$ bond is electron-rich and attracts electrophiles', 'option4', 'The $\pi$ electron cloud above and below the plane is exposed and nucleophilic, attracting electrophiles like H$^+$, Br$^+$, etc.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Markownikoff''s rule predicts that in HBr addition to propene, the major product is', '1-Bromopropane', '2-Bromopropane', 'Equal mixture', 'No reaction', 'option2', 'H$^+$ adds to the less substituted C (C1), Br$^-$ to the more substituted C (C2). More stable 2° carbocation intermediate.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ozonolysis of alkenes involves', 'Halogenation', 'Treatment with KMnO$_4$', 'Hydrogenation', 'Treatment with O$_3$ followed by Zn/H$_2$O to cleave the double bond into carbonyl compounds', 'option4', 'R$_2$C=CR$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{O}_3}$ ozonide $\xrightarrow{\text{Zn/H}_2\text{O}}$ R$_2$C=O + O=CR$_2$. Used to locate double bonds.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The addition of Br$_2$ to ethene is an example of', 'Elimination', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'Free radical addition', 'Electrophilic addition', 'option4', 'Br$_2$ is polarized by the $\pi$ cloud. Br$^+$ (electrophile) attacks first, forming a bromonium ion. Then Br$^-$ attacks anti.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Geometrical (cis-trans) isomerism in alkenes requires', 'Free rotation around the double bond', 'Restricted rotation around C=C and different groups on each doubly-bonded carbon', 'Only identical groups', 'A triple bond', 'option2', 'C=C prevents rotation. Each C must have 2 different substituents for cis/trans to exist.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Alkenes are prepared by', 'Oxidation of alkanes', 'Hydrogenation', 'Halogenation', 'Dehydration of alcohols (H$_2$SO$_4$/Al$_2$O$_3$) or dehydrohalogenation of alkyl halides (alc. KOH)', 'option4', 'Elimination reactions: remove H$_2$O or HX to form C=C. Zaitsev''s rule gives the more substituted alkene as major product.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Baeyer test for unsaturation uses', 'FeCl$_3$', 'Br$_2$/CCl$_4$ only', 'Cold dilute KMnO$_4$ (decolourized by alkenes)', 'NaOH', 'option3', 'Purple KMnO$_4$ is decolourized: alkene $\to$ diol (syn-dihydroxylation). Also, Br$_2$/CCl$_4$ decolourization tests for unsaturation.', 'c_alkenes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Anti-Markownikoff addition of HBr occurs in the presence of', 'No catalyst', 'AlCl$_3$', 'H$_2$SO$_4$', 'Peroxides (ROOR) — free radical mechanism', 'option4', 'Peroxide effect (Kharasch): ROOR generates Br$\cdot$. Br$\cdot$ adds to less substituted C (more stable radical). Only works for HBr.', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The addition of HBr to propene in the presence of peroxides gives', '2-Bromopropane (Markownikoff)', '1-Bromopropane (anti-Markownikoff)', 'No reaction', 'Propane', 'option2', 'Br$\cdot$ adds to C1 (less substituted, forming more stable 2° radical at C2). Then H$\cdot$ adds to C2. Product: 1-bromopropane.', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ozonolysis of 2-butene gives', 'Acetone and formaldehyde', 'Formaldehyde and propanal', 'Two molecules of acetaldehyde (CH$_3$CHO)', 'Butanal', 'option3', 'CH$_3$CH=CHCH$_3$ $\xrightarrow{\text{O}_3/\text{Zn}}$ 2 CH$_3$CHO. Each side of the double bond becomes an aldehyde.', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The addition of Br$_2$ to alkenes is anti (trans) addition because', 'The addition is syn', 'Br$_2$ adds from the same side', 'No intermediate forms', 'A cyclic bromonium ion intermediate forms, and Br$^-$ attacks from the opposite side', 'option4', 'Bromonium ion: 3-membered ring. Br$^-$ attacks the more substituted C from the back (anti). Product: anti-dibromo compound.', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hydration of alkenes (H$_2$O/H$^+$) follows', 'No selectivity', 'Anti-Markownikoff''s rule', 'Markownikoff''s rule (OH goes to more substituted C)', 'Only syn addition', 'option3', 'H$^+$ adds to less substituted C, forming more stable carbocation. H$_2$O attacks the carbocation. Product: Markownikoff alcohol.', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes is a', 'Anti (trans) addition', 'Syn (cis) addition of H$_2$', 'Free radical addition', 'Elimination', 'option2', 'Both H atoms add from the same face of the alkene (surface of the catalyst). Syn addition.', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The stability of alkenes increases with', 'Increasing strain', 'Decreasing substitution', 'No pattern', 'Increasing substitution (more alkyl groups on the double bond)', 'option4', 'Hyperconjugation stabilizes the double bond. Tetrasubstituted $>$ trisubstituted $>$ disubstituted $>$ monosubstituted. Trans $>$ cis (less steric strain).', 'c_alkenes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wacker process oxidizes ethene to', 'Acetaldehyde using PdCl$_2$/CuCl$_2$ catalyst', 'Ethanol', 'Acetic acid', 'Ethane', 'option1', 'CH$_2$=CH$_2$ + PdCl$_2$ + H$_2$O $\to$ CH$_3$CHO + Pd + 2HCl. CuCl$_2$/O$_2$ reoxidizes Pd. Industrial process.', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Epoxidation of alkenes with mCPBA (meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid) gives', 'Epoxides (oxiranes) via syn addition of oxygen', 'Diols', 'Ketones', 'Aldehydes', 'option1', 'mCPBA transfers one O atom to the alkene face. Syn addition. Epoxide can be opened by nucleophiles (anti in acidic, syn in basic conditions).', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dihydroxylation of alkenes with OsO$_4$ gives', 'Anti-diols', 'Syn-diols (cis-1,2-diol)', 'Epoxides', 'Ketones', 'option2', 'OsO$_4$ adds to the same face (syn). Produces cis-diol. Catalytic with NMO (N-methylmorpholine N-oxide) as co-oxidant.', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ozonolysis of 2-methylpropene gives', 'Two molecules of acetaldehyde', 'Acetone and formaldehyde', 'Propanal and formaldehyde', 'Butanal', 'option2', '(CH$_3$)$_2$C=CH$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{O}_3/\text{Zn}}$ (CH$_3$)$_2$C=O (acetone) + HCHO (formaldehyde).', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Polymerization of ethene under high pressure and temperature gives', 'Polyethylene (LDPE)', 'Polypropylene', 'PVC', 'Teflon', 'option1', '$n$CH$_2$=CH$_2$ $\xrightarrow{P, T, \text{initiator}}$ $(-$CH$_2$-CH$_2$$-)_n$. Free radical polymerization gives branched LDPE.', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Simmons-Smith reaction converts alkenes to', 'Cyclopropanes using CH$_2$I$_2$/Zn-Cu (carbenoid addition)', 'Epoxides', 'Diols', 'Aldehydes', 'option1', 'CH$_2$I$_2$ + Zn-Cu $\to$ ICH$_2$ZnI (carbenoid). Adds CH$_2$ across the double bond (syn). Stereospecific cyclopropanation.', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The regioselectivity of hydroboration (BH$_3$ adds to the less substituted carbon) is due to', 'Steric factors — BH$_3$ preferentially approaches the less hindered end of the double bond', 'Electronic factors only', 'No selectivity exists', 'BH$_3$ adds to the more substituted carbon', 'option1', 'BH$_3$ is bulky. It adds to the less substituted C (anti-Markownikoff). After oxidation: anti-Markownikoff alcohol with syn stereochemistry.', 'c_alkenes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_alkynes (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Acidic character, addition reactions, polymerization
-- ============================================================
('Terminal alkynes (RC$\equiv$CH) are weakly acidic because', 'They have no hydrogen', 'The $sp$ carbon has $50\%$ $s$ character, holding the bonding electrons closer and stabilizing the carbanion', 'They are strong acids', 'The triple bond is non-polar', 'option2', '$sp$ C-H: most acidic among C-H bonds. $pK_a \approx 25$ (vs $\sim 44$ for alkanes, $\sim 36$ for alkenes).', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Terminal alkynes react with sodium metal to form', 'Sodium hydride', 'Sodium alkoxides', 'No reaction', 'Sodium acetylides (RC$\equiv$CNa) with evolution of H$_2$', 'option4', 'RC$\equiv$CH + Na $\to$ RC$\equiv$CNa + $\frac{1}{2}$H$_2$. Acetylides are strong nucleophiles/bases.', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The addition of HBr to propyne follows Markownikoff''s rule to give', '1-Bromopropene', '2-Bromopropene', '1,2-Dibromopropane', '2-Bromopropane', 'option2', 'CH$_3$C$\equiv$CH + HBr $\to$ CH$_3$CBr=CH$_2$ (2-bromopropene). H adds to terminal C, Br to internal C.', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Alkynes can be prepared by', 'Dehydrohalogenation of vicinal dihalides with strong base (NaNH$_2$)', 'Hydrogenation of alkenes', 'Halogenation of alkanes', 'Oxidation of alcohols', 'option1', 'RCHBrCHBrR'' $\xrightarrow{2\text{NaNH}_2}$ RC$\equiv$CR'' + 2NaBr + 2NH$_3$. Two eliminations.', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetylene (ethyne) is prepared industrially by', 'Action of water on calcium carbide (CaC$_2$)', 'Dehydration of ethanol', 'Cracking of methane', 'Both option1 and option3', 'option4', 'CaC$_2$ + 2H$_2$O $\to$ C$_2$H$_2$ + Ca(OH)$_2$. Also: 2CH$_4$ $\xrightarrow{1500°\text{C}}$ C$_2$H$_2$ + 3H$_2$.', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The addition of 2 moles of Br$_2$ to 1 mole of ethyne gives', '1,2-Dibromoethene', '1,1,2,2-Tetrabromoethane', '1,2-Dibromoethane', 'Bromoethane', 'option2', 'HC$\equiv$CH + Br$_2 \to$ CHBr=CHBr (trans). + Br$_2 \to$ CHBr$_2$CHBr$_2$ (1,1,2,2-tetrabromoethane).', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Lindlar''s catalyst reduces alkynes to', 'Trans-alkenes', 'Cis-alkenes (syn addition of H$_2$)', 'Alkanes', 'Aldehydes', 'option2', 'Pd/CaCO$_3$/quinoline (poisoned catalyst). Stops at alkene stage. Syn addition gives cis product.', 'c_alkynes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Birch reduction of alkynes (Na/liquid NH$_3$) gives', 'Cis-alkenes', 'Trans-alkenes', 'Alkanes', 'No reaction', 'option2', 'Dissolving metal reduction: anti addition of H atoms. Gives trans-alkene. Complementary to Lindlar (cis).', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetylide ions (RC$\equiv$C$^-$) are useful in synthesis because they are', 'Acids', 'Electrophiles', 'Unreactive', 'Strong nucleophiles that undergo S$_N$2 reactions with primary alkyl halides', 'option4', 'RC$\equiv$C$^-$ + R''CH$_2$X $\to$ RC$\equiv$CCH$_2$R'' + X$^-$. Extends the carbon chain. Only works with 1° halides (2°/3° give elimination).', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hydration of alkynes with H$_2$SO$_4$/HgSO$_4$ gives', 'Alcohols', 'Ketones (or acetaldehyde from acetylene) via enol intermediate', 'Carboxylic acids', 'Ethers', 'option2', 'RC$\equiv$CH + H$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{H}^+/\text{Hg}^{2+}}$ [enol] $\to$ RCOCH$_3$ (Markownikoff). Acetylene gives acetaldehyde.', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The tautomerization of the enol form to the keto form in alkyne hydration is called', 'Resonance', 'Geometric isomerism', 'Optical isomerism', 'Keto-enol tautomerism', 'option4', 'Enol (C=C-OH) $\rightleftharpoons$ keto (C-C=O). Keto form is usually more stable. Rapid equilibrium.', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ozonolysis of alkynes gives', 'Carboxylic acids (or CO$_2$ from terminal alkynes)', 'Aldehydes', 'Ketones', 'Alcohols', 'option1', 'RC$\equiv$CR'' $\xrightarrow{\text{O}_3/\text{H}_2\text{O}}$ RCOOH + R''COOH. Terminal C gives CO$_2$ + H$_2$O.', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetylene reacts with HCl in the presence of HgCl$_2$ to give', 'Acetaldehyde', 'Ethyl chloride', '1,2-Dichloroethane', 'Vinyl chloride (CH$_2$=CHCl)', 'option4', 'HC$\equiv$CH + HCl $\xrightarrow{\text{HgCl}_2}$ CH$_2$=CHCl (vinyl chloride, monomer for PVC). Markownikoff addition.', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Polymerization of acetylene gives', 'Polyethylene', 'Polyacetylene (conducting polymer)', 'PVC', 'Teflon', 'option2', '$n$HC$\equiv$CH $\xrightarrow{\text{catalyst}}$ $(-$CH=CH$-)_n$. Polyacetylene: conjugated polymer, conducts electricity when doped (Nobel Prize 2000).', 'c_alkynes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Cyclic trimerization of acetylene gives', 'Cyclohexane', 'Benzene (Reppe synthesis)', 'Cyclopropane', 'Naphthalene', 'option2', '3HC$\equiv$CH $\xrightarrow{\text{red hot Fe tube, }600°\text{C}}$ C$_6$H$_6$ (benzene). Berthelot synthesis.', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hydroboration-oxidation of terminal alkynes (using disiamylborane) gives', 'Ketones (Markownikoff)', 'Aldehydes (anti-Markownikoff)', 'Carboxylic acids', 'Alcohols', 'option2', 'RC$\equiv$CH $\xrightarrow{\text{(sia)}_2\text{BH}}$ vinyl borane $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}_2/\text{NaOH}}$ enol $\to$ RCHO. Anti-Markownikoff aldehyde.', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acidity order of C-H bonds is', 'RC$\equiv$CH $>$ R$_2$C=CH$_2$ $>$ R$_3$CH ($sp > sp^2 > sp^3$)', 'R$_3$CH $>$ R$_2$C=CH$_2$ $>$ RC$\equiv$CH', 'All are equally acidic', 'R$_2$C=CH$_2$ $>$ RC$\equiv$CH $>$ R$_3$CH', 'option1', 'More $s$ character $\to$ electrons held closer $\to$ more stable conjugate base $\to$ more acidic. $sp$ ($50\%$ s) $>$ $sp^2$ ($33\%$) $>$ $sp^3$ ($25\%$).', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Rosenmund reduction of acid chlorides gives aldehydes. Similarly, Stephen reduction of nitriles gives aldehydes using', 'NaBH$_4$', 'LiAlH$_4$', 'SnCl$_2$/HCl followed by hydrolysis', 'H$_2$/Pd', 'option3', 'RCN $\xrightarrow{\text{SnCl}_2/\text{HCl}}$ RCH=NH$\cdot$HCl $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}}$ RCHO. Selective reduction to aldehyde.', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Kolbe electrolysis of potassium salt of a dicarboxylic acid (e.g., potassium succinate) gives', 'A ketone', 'An alkane', 'A diol', 'An alkene (ethene from succinate)', 'option4', '$^-$OOC-CH$_2$-CH$_2$-COO$^- \xrightarrow{\text{electrolysis}}$ CH$_2$=CH$_2$ + 2CO$_2$ + 2e$^-$. Decarboxylation at both ends.', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Internal alkynes can be converted to terminal alkynes by', 'No conversion is possible', 'Hydrogenation', 'Oxidation', 'Treatment with strong base (NaNH$_2$) in liquid NH$_3$ — isomerization via acetylide equilibrium', 'option4', 'RC$\equiv$CR'' $\xrightarrow{\text{NaNH}_2/\text{NH}_3}$ RC$\equiv$CH (thermodynamic product, terminal alkyne). Base-catalyzed isomerization.', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_aromatic_hc (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Benzene structure, aromaticity, EAS, directive influence
-- ============================================================
('Benzene has the molecular formula C$_6$H$_6$ and is', 'A saturated hydrocarbon', 'An unsaturated hydrocarbon that is unusually stable due to aromaticity', 'An alkane', 'An alkyne', 'option2', 'Benzene: 3 C=C equivalents but does not behave like a typical alkene. Resonance stabilization ($\sim 150\;\text{kJ/mol}$) makes it aromatic.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Huckel''s rule for aromaticity requires', 'Any number of $\pi$ electrons', '$(4n)$ $\pi$ electrons', '$(4n+2)$ $\pi$ electrons in a planar, cyclic, fully conjugated system', 'No $\pi$ electrons', 'option3', '$n = 0$: 2$\pi$e (cyclopropenyl cation). $n = 1$: 6$\pi$e (benzene). $n = 2$: 10$\pi$e (naphthalene).', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Benzene undergoes electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS) rather than addition because', 'Addition is faster', 'Benzene has no $\pi$ electrons', 'Substitution preserves the aromatic $\pi$ system; addition would destroy it', 'Substitution is always preferred', 'option3', 'Aromaticity provides $\sim 150\;\text{kJ/mol}$ stabilization. The system "prefers" to retain the aromatic ring.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Friedel-Crafts alkylation of benzene uses', 'RCl/AlCl$_3$ to introduce an alkyl group', 'NaOH', 'KMnO$_4$', 'H$_2$/Ni', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_6$ + RCl $\xrightarrow{\text{AlCl}_3}$ C$_6$H$_5$R + HCl. AlCl$_3$ generates the electrophile R$^+$ (or R$^+$-like species).', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nitration of benzene uses', 'Conc. HNO$_3$/conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ (nitrating mixture)', 'Dilute HNO$_3$', 'NaNO$_2$', 'NH$_3$', 'option1', 'Electrophile: NO$_2^+$ (nitronium ion) generated from HNO$_3$ + H$_2$SO$_4$. C$_6$H$_6$ + NO$_2^+ \to$ C$_6$H$_5$NO$_2$ + H$^+$.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ortho/para directors are groups that', 'Direct to meta position', 'Deactivate the ring always', 'Activate the ring and direct incoming electrophiles to ortho and para positions', 'Have no effect', 'option3', 'EDGs ($-$OH, $-$NH$_2$, $-$OR, $-$R, $-$NHCOR): activate and direct ortho/para. Exception: halogens deactivate but direct ortho/para.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Meta directors are groups that', 'Activate the ring', 'Deactivate the ring and direct incoming electrophiles to the meta position', 'Direct to ortho position', 'Have no effect', 'option2', 'EWGs ($-$NO$_2$, $-$CN, $-$COOH, $-$CHO, $-$COR, $-$SO$_3$H): deactivate and direct meta.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Friedel-Crafts acylation of benzene gives', 'Alkylbenzene', 'Aryl ketone (acylbenzene)', 'Carboxylic acid', 'Aldehyde', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_6$ + RCOCl $\xrightarrow{\text{AlCl}_3}$ C$_6$H$_5$COR + HCl. Advantage over alkylation: no rearrangement, no polyalkylation.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Toluene (methylbenzene) is more reactive than benzene in EAS because', 'The methyl group is electron-donating ($+$I, hyperconjugation), activating the ring', 'Methyl is electron-withdrawing', 'Toluene is not aromatic', 'Benzene is more reactive', 'option1', 'CH$_3$ donates electron density to the ring, increasing $\pi$ electron density and making it more nucleophilic.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nitrobenzene is less reactive than benzene in EAS because', 'Nitrobenzene is not aromatic', '$-$NO$_2$ is electron-donating', '$-$NO$_2$ is a strong electron-withdrawing group ($-$I, $-$M), deactivating the ring', 'Benzene is less reactive', 'option3', '$-$NO$_2$ withdraws electron density from the ring, making it less nucleophilic and harder to attack by electrophiles.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sulphonation of benzene is reversible. This is useful because', 'The $-$SO$_3$H group can be introduced as a blocking group and later removed', 'It is irreversible', 'It has no use', 'It always fails', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_6$ + H$_2$SO$_4$ $\rightleftharpoons$ C$_6$H$_5$SO$_3$H. Dilute H$_2$SO$_4$/steam removes $-$SO$_3$H. Used in directing synthesis.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Birch reduction of benzene gives', 'Cyclohexane', '1,4-Cyclohexadiene', 'Cyclohexene', 'No reaction', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_6$ + Na/NH$_3$(l)/ROH $\to$ 1,4-cyclohexadiene (unconjugated). Partial reduction.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Side-chain oxidation of toluene with KMnO$_4$/H$^+$ gives', 'Benzaldehyde', 'Benzoic acid', 'Benzyl alcohol', 'Phenol', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$CH$_3$ $\xrightarrow{\text{KMnO}_4/\text{H}^+}$ C$_6$H$_5$COOH. Any alkyl side chain with a benzylic H is oxidized to $-$COOH.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wurtz-Fittig reaction is used to prepare', 'Alkanes', 'Biphenyls', 'Alkylbenzenes from aryl halides and alkyl halides using Na', 'Alkenes', 'option3', 'C$_6$H$_5$Br + CH$_3$Br + 2Na $\to$ C$_6$H$_5$CH$_3$ + 2NaBr. Cross-coupling of aryl and alkyl halides.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the EAS mechanism, the rate-determining step is', 'The generation of the electrophile', 'The loss of H$^+$', 'The formation of the arenium ion (sigma complex)', 'No step is rate-determining', 'option3', 'Electrophile attacks the ring (slow, endothermic) $\to$ arenium ion. Then H$^+$ is lost (fast) to restore aromaticity.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ortho/para ratio in EAS depends on', 'Only temperature', 'Only electronic effects', 'Steric effects (bulky electrophiles favour para) and electronic effects', 'Nothing — it is always 2:1', 'option3', 'Statistically: ortho:para $= 2:1$ (2 ortho positions vs 1 para). Steric hindrance reduces ortho product. Bulky groups give more para.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Friedel-Crafts reactions do not work with', 'Benzene', 'Strongly deactivated rings (nitrobenzene, benzoic acid) or with vinyl/aryl halides', 'Toluene', 'Activated rings', 'option2', 'Deactivated rings: too electron-poor. Also, AlCl$_3$ complexes with $-$NH$_2$ (deactivates catalyst). Vinyl/aryl halides don''t form stable carbocations.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Haworth synthesis of naphthalene involves', 'Wurtz reaction', 'Direct coupling of two benzene rings', 'Diels-Alder reaction', 'Friedel-Crafts acylation of benzene with succinic anhydride, followed by reduction, cyclization, and aromatization', 'option4', 'Multi-step synthesis building the second ring onto benzene. Classic example of retrosynthetic planning.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Benzene reacts with ozone to give', 'Three molecules of glyoxal (OHCCHO)', 'Phenol', 'Benzoic acid', 'No reaction', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_6$ + 3O$_3 \to$ 3OHCCHO (glyoxal). Confirms the Kekule structure (3 "double bonds"). Rarely asked but tests understanding.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Gattermann-Koch reaction converts benzene to', 'Toluene', 'Benzoic acid', 'Benzaldehyde using CO/HCl/AlCl$_3$ (formylation)', 'Phenol', 'option3', 'C$_6$H$_6$ + CO + HCl $\xrightarrow{\text{AlCl}_3/\text{CuCl}}$ C$_6$H$_5$CHO. Electrophile: [CHO]$^+$ (formyl cation equivalent).', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Clemmensen reduction of acetophenone (C$_6$H$_5$COCH$_3$) gives', 'Styrene', 'Ethylbenzene', 'Benzoic acid', 'Benzaldehyde', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$COCH$_3$ $\xrightarrow{\text{Zn-Hg/HCl}}$ C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2$CH$_3$ (ethylbenzene). Reduces C=O to CH$_2$.', 'c_aromatic_hc', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetylene reacts with dilute H$_2$SO$_4$ in the presence of HgSO$_4$ to give', 'Ethanol', 'Acetaldehyde (via enol tautomerization)', 'Acetic acid', 'Ethane', 'option2', 'HC$\equiv$CH + H$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4/\text{HgSO}_4}$ [CH$_2$=CHOH] (vinyl alcohol, enol) $\to$ CH$_3$CHO (acetaldehyde, keto form).', 'c_alkynes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_haloalkane_prep_reactions (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Preparation, properties, reactions, C-X bond, substitution mechanisms
-- ============================================================
('Haloalkanes (alkyl halides) have the general formula', 'R-OH', 'R-X (where X = F, Cl, Br, I)', 'R-NH$_2$', 'R-COOH', 'option2', 'R-X: X is a halogen bonded to an $sp^3$ carbon. Classified as 1°, 2°, 3° based on the carbon bearing X.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The C-X bond in haloalkanes is polar because', 'Carbon is more electronegative', 'Halogens are more electronegative than carbon', 'The bond is non-polar', 'Halogens are metals', 'option2', 'C$^{\delta+}$-X$^{\delta-}$: the carbon is electrophilic, making it susceptible to nucleophilic attack.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Haloalkanes can be prepared from alcohols by', 'Oxidation', 'Reaction with HX, PX$_3$, PX$_5$, or SOCl$_2$', 'Reduction', 'Dehydration only', 'option2', 'R-OH + HX $\to$ R-X + H$_2$O. SOCl$_2$ (thionyl chloride) is preferred: gaseous by-products (SO$_2$, HCl) leave.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The S$_N$2 mechanism involves', 'Backside attack by the nucleophile in a single concerted step with inversion of configuration', 'Formation of a carbocation intermediate', 'Free radical mechanism', 'No nucleophile', 'option1', 'Rate $= k[\text{substrate}][\text{Nu}]$. Transition state: pentacoordinate carbon. Walden inversion.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The S$_N$1 mechanism involves', 'A concerted one-step process', 'Formation of a carbocation intermediate followed by nucleophilic attack', 'Free radical mechanism', 'Elimination only', 'option2', 'Rate $= k[\text{substrate}]$. Step 1: ionization (slow). Step 2: nucleophilic attack (fast). Racemization.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reactivity order of haloalkanes in S$_N$2 is', '3° $>$ 2° $>$ 1° $>$ CH$_3$X', 'CH$_3$X $>$ 1° $>$ 2° $>$ 3°', 'All are equally reactive', '2° $>$ 1° $>$ 3°', 'option2', 'S$_N$2: backside attack. Steric hindrance increases from CH$_3$ to 3°. Less hindered $=$ faster S$_N$2.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reactivity order of haloalkanes in S$_N$1 is', 'CH$_3$X $>$ 1° $>$ 2° $>$ 3°', '3° $>$ 2° $>$ 1° $>$ CH$_3$X', 'All are equally reactive', '1° $>$ 2° $>$ 3°', 'option2', 'S$_N$1: via carbocation. More stable carbocation $=$ faster ionization. 3° $>$ 2° $>$ 1°.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Finkelstein reaction converts', 'Alkyl chlorides/bromides to alkyl iodides using NaI in acetone', 'Alkyl iodides to chlorides', 'Alcohols to halides', 'Halides to alcohols', 'option1', 'R-Cl + NaI $\xrightarrow{\text{acetone}}$ R-I + NaCl$\downarrow$. NaCl precipitates (insoluble in acetone), driving the reaction forward.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Swarts reaction converts', 'Fluorides to iodides', 'Alkyl fluorides to chlorides', 'Alcohols to fluorides', 'Alkyl chlorides/bromides to alkyl fluorides using AgF or Hg$_2$F$_2$', 'option4', 'R-Cl + AgF $\to$ R-F + AgCl. Used to prepare fluoroalkanes.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Grignard reagents (RMgX) are prepared by', 'Reacting alkyl halides with Mg in dry ether', 'Reacting alcohols with Mg', 'Heating alkyl halides', 'Electrolysis', 'option1', 'R-X + Mg $\xrightarrow{\text{dry ether}}$ RMgX. Extremely versatile organometallic reagent. Must be anhydrous.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Williamson ether synthesis uses', 'Friedel-Crafts reaction', 'Dehydration of alcohols', 'Grignard reagent', 'Sodium alkoxide + alkyl halide (S$_N$2) to form ethers', 'option4', 'R-ONa + R''-X $\to$ R-O-R'' + NaX. Best with 1° alkyl halides (2°/3° give elimination).', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The bond strength order of C-X bonds is', 'C-F $>$ C-Cl $>$ C-Br $>$ C-I', 'C-I $>$ C-Br $>$ C-Cl $>$ C-F', 'All are equal', 'C-Cl $>$ C-F $>$ C-Br $>$ C-I', 'option1', 'C-F ($485\;\text{kJ}$) $>$ C-Cl ($339$) $>$ C-Br ($276$) $>$ C-I ($238$). Shorter bond $=$ stronger.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Despite C-F being the strongest C-X bond, the reactivity order in S$_N$ reactions is', 'R-I $>$ R-Br $>$ R-Cl $>$ R-F', 'R-F $>$ R-Cl $>$ R-Br $>$ R-I', 'All are equally reactive', 'R-Cl $>$ R-I $>$ R-Br $>$ R-F', 'option1', 'Reactivity depends on leaving group ability: I$^-$ (best, most stable, least basic) $>$ Br$^-$ $>$ Cl$^-$ $>$ F$^-$ (worst).', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Allyl and benzyl halides are more reactive in S$_N$1 because', 'They have weaker C-X bonds', 'Their carbocations are resonance-stabilized', 'They are primary', 'They are unreactive', 'option2', 'Allyl: CH$_2$=CH-CH$_2^+ \leftrightarrow ^+$CH$_2$-CH=CH$_2$. Benzyl: C$_6$H$_5$CH$_2^+$ (resonance with ring). Stable carbocations form easily.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vinyl halides (CH$_2$=CHX) and aryl halides (C$_6$H$_5$X) are unreactive in S$_N$ reactions because', 'The C-X bond has partial double bond character (resonance) and the carbon is $sp^2$ (harder to attack)', 'They have weak C-X bonds', 'They are very reactive', 'They have no halogen', 'option1', 'Resonance: lone pair on X delocalizes into the $\pi$ system, strengthening C-X. $sp^2$ carbon is harder to approach (no backside attack for S$_N$2).', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aryl halides can undergo nucleophilic substitution via', 'Addition-elimination (S$_N$Ar) with strong EWGs, or elimination-addition (benzyne mechanism) with strong bases', 'S$_N$2 directly', 'S$_N$1 directly', 'No mechanism works', 'option1', 'S$_N$Ar: requires activating groups (NO$_2$) ortho/para to X. Benzyne: NaNH$_2$/NH$_3$ generates benzyne intermediate.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Wurtz reaction, Grignard reaction, and Corey-House synthesis all use haloalkanes to', 'Form new C-C bonds', 'Form C-O bonds', 'Form C-N bonds', 'Break C-C bonds', 'option1', 'C-C bond formation is central to organic synthesis. Haloalkanes are versatile starting materials for this.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Neopentyl bromide ((CH$_3$)$_3$CCH$_2$Br) is very slow in both S$_N$1 and S$_N$2 because', 'Both mechanisms are fast', 'It is very reactive', 'It has no leaving group', 'S$_N$2: severe steric hindrance from 3 methyl groups. S$_N$1: primary carbocation is very unstable', 'option4', 'Neopentyl: 1° halide (bad for S$_N$1) but extremely hindered (bad for S$_N$2). Reacts mainly via E2 or rearrangement.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reaction of 2-bromobutane with NaOH in water gives mainly', 'Butan-2-ol (S$_N$ product)', 'But-2-ene (E product)', 'Butane', 'No reaction', 'option1', 'Aqueous NaOH: moderate base/nucleophile. 2° substrate: S$_N$1/S$_N$2 compete. In water (protic), substitution dominates at moderate temperature.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reaction of 2-bromobutane with $t$-BuOK in $t$-BuOH gives mainly', 'Butan-2-ol', 'But-2-ene (E2 product, Zaitsev)', 'But-1-ene', 'Butane', 'option2', 'Strong bulky base + 2° substrate: E2 dominates. Zaitsev product (more substituted alkene) is major.', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Saytzeff (Zaitsev) product from dehydrohalogenation of 2-bromobutane is', 'But-1-ene', 'But-2-ene (more substituted, more stable)', 'Butane', '1,3-Butadiene', 'option2', 'E2 with non-bulky base: Zaitsev rule. But-2-ene (disubstituted) is more stable than but-1-ene (monosubstituted).', 'c_haloalkane_prep_reactions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_haloalkane_environmental (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Uses and environmental effects of CHCl3, CHI3, freons, DDT
-- ============================================================
('Chloroform (CHCl$_3$) is used as', 'A solvent and was historically used as an anaesthetic', 'A fuel', 'A food additive', 'A fertilizer', 'option1', 'CHCl$_3$: good solvent for fats, oils, resins. No longer used as anaesthetic (toxic to liver). Stored in dark bottles with ethanol (prevents phosgene formation).', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Iodoform (CHI$_3$) was historically used as', 'A food preservative', 'A fuel', 'A fertilizer', 'An antiseptic', 'option4', 'CHI$_3$: yellow solid with characteristic smell. Antiseptic properties due to liberation of free iodine. Now replaced by better antiseptics.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Freons (CFCs like CCl$_2$F$_2$) were used as', 'Refrigerants and aerosol propellants', 'Fuels', 'Fertilizers', 'Food additives', 'option1', 'Freons: non-toxic, non-flammable, chemically inert. Ideal refrigerants. But they deplete the ozone layer.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('DDT (dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane) is', 'An insecticide that is now banned in many countries due to environmental persistence', 'A medicine', 'A fertilizer', 'A food additive', 'option1', 'DDT: effective insecticide but bioaccumulates in food chains (biomagnification). Banned by Stockholm Convention.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('CFCs deplete the ozone layer by', 'Producing CO$_2$', 'Absorbing UV light', 'Reacting with N$_2$', 'Releasing Cl$\cdot$ radicals in the stratosphere that catalytically destroy O$_3$', 'option4', 'CFCl$_3$ $\xrightarrow{UV}$ CFCl$_2\cdot$ + Cl$\cdot$. Cl$\cdot$ + O$_3 \to$ ClO$\cdot$ + O$_2$. ClO$\cdot$ + O $\to$ Cl$\cdot$ + O$_2$. One Cl$\cdot$ destroys $\sim 100{,}000$ O$_3$ molecules.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Montreal Protocol (1987) aimed to', 'Reduce CO$_2$ emissions', 'Phase out the production of ozone-depleting substances (CFCs, halons)', 'Ban nuclear weapons', 'Promote fossil fuels', 'option2', 'Most successful environmental treaty. CFCs replaced by HCFCs (less harmful) and HFCs (no Cl, no ozone depletion but greenhouse effect).', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Carbon tetrachloride (CCl$_4$) was used as', 'A fire extinguisher and dry-cleaning solvent, but is now restricted due to toxicity', 'A food additive', 'A fuel', 'A medicine', 'option1', 'CCl$_4$: causes liver and kidney damage. Ozone-depleting. Replaced by safer alternatives.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chloroform slowly oxidizes in air and sunlight to form', 'HCl only', 'CO$_2$', 'Phosgene (COCl$_2$), a poisonous gas', 'No product', 'option3', '2CHCl$_3$ + O$_2 \xrightarrow{light}$ 2COCl$_2$ + 2HCl. Phosgene is extremely toxic. CHCl$_3$ is stored in dark bottles with $1\%$ ethanol.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The iodoform test is positive for compounds containing', 'Any alcohol', 'CH$_3$CO$-$ group (methyl ketones) or CH$_3$CH(OH)$-$ group (secondary alcohols with methyl)', 'Any aldehyde', 'Any halide', 'option2', 'CH$_3$COR + 3I$_2$ + 4NaOH $\to$ CHI$_3$ (yellow ppt) + RCOONa. Also positive for ethanol and acetaldehyde.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('DDT is non-biodegradable because', 'It is a gas', 'It is water-soluble', 'It decomposes quickly', 'Its multiple C-Cl bonds and aromatic rings resist enzymatic breakdown', 'option4', 'DDT persists in soil for decades. Accumulates in fatty tissues of organisms. Biomagnification through food chains.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Teflon (PTFE, polytetrafluoroethylene) is', 'A pesticide', 'A CFC', 'A polymer of tetrafluoroethene (CF$_2$=CF$_2$) with exceptional chemical resistance', 'A medicine', 'option3', '$(-$CF$_2$-CF$_2$$-)_n$: non-stick, chemically inert, high mp. Used in cookware, gaskets, electrical insulation.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('PVC (polyvinyl chloride) is made from', 'Ethylene', 'Vinyl chloride (CH$_2$=CHCl)', 'Propylene', 'Styrene', 'option2', '$n$CH$_2$=CHCl $\to$ $(-$CH$_2$-CHCl$-)_n$. Used in pipes, flooring, packaging. Plasticizers (phthalates) make it flexible.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Halothane (CF$_3$CHClBr) is used as', 'A modern inhalation anaesthetic', 'A pesticide', 'A refrigerant', 'A solvent', 'option1', 'Halothane: non-flammable, potent anaesthetic. Replaced older agents like chloroform and ether.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The greenhouse warming potential of HFCs (replacements for CFCs) is a concern because', 'HFCs have no environmental impact', 'HFCs deplete ozone', 'HFCs are non-toxic', 'HFCs do not deplete ozone but are potent greenhouse gases (high GWP)', 'option4', 'HFCs: no Cl (no ozone depletion) but strong IR absorption. GWP of some HFCs is $>1000\times$ CO$_2$. Being phased down by Kigali Amendment.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ozone hole is most prominent over', 'The tropics', 'The equator', 'The North Pole', 'Antarctica', 'option4', 'Polar stratospheric clouds (PSCs) in Antarctic winter provide surfaces for CFC-derived Cl reactions. Spring sunlight triggers rapid O$_3$ destruction.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Biomagnification of DDT means', 'DDT concentration increases at each trophic level of the food chain', 'DDT breaks down at each level', 'DDT concentration decreases', 'DDT is not absorbed', 'option1', 'Water ($0.000003\;\text{ppm}$) $\to$ plankton ($0.04$) $\to$ fish ($0.5$) $\to$ birds ($25\;\text{ppm}$). Concentration increases $\sim 10\times$ per level.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reaction CHCl$_3$ + 4Cl$_2 \to$ CCl$_4$ + 3HCl is an example of', 'Addition', 'Further chlorination (substitution of remaining H atoms)', 'Elimination', 'Rearrangement', 'option2', 'Excess Cl$_2$ replaces all H atoms. CHCl$_3 \to$ CCl$_4$ (carbon tetrachloride). Free radical mechanism.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Methyl bromide (CH$_3$Br) is used as a soil fumigant but is being phased out because', 'It has no environmental impact', 'It is non-toxic', 'It depletes the ozone layer (Br is even more effective than Cl at destroying O$_3$)', 'It is too expensive', 'option3', 'Br$\cdot$ is $\sim 60\times$ more efficient than Cl$\cdot$ at destroying ozone. CH$_3$Br is a significant ODS.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Carbylamine reaction (isocyanide test) uses chloroform to detect', 'Tertiary amines', 'Secondary amines', 'Primary amines (R-NH$_2$)', 'Alcohols', 'option3', 'R-NH$_2$ + CHCl$_3$ + 3KOH $\to$ R-NC (isocyanide, foul smell) + 3KCl + 3H$_2$O. Specific for 1° amines.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Reimer-Tiemann reaction uses chloroform to introduce', 'A $-$COOH group', 'A $-$NO$_2$ group', 'A $-$Cl group', 'A $-$CHO group on the benzene ring of phenol (formylation)', 'option4', 'C$_6$H$_5$OH + CHCl$_3$ + 3NaOH $\to$ o-HOC$_6$H$_4$CHO (salicylaldehyde) + 3NaCl + 2H$_2$O. Via dichlorocarbene (:CCl$_2$) intermediate.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chlorofluorocarbons are chemically inert in the troposphere but reactive in the stratosphere because', 'The stratosphere has no UV', 'They react with water in the troposphere', 'They are not inert', 'UV-C radiation in the stratosphere has enough energy to break the C-Cl bond, releasing Cl$\cdot$ radicals', 'option4', 'C-Cl bond energy $\sim 339\;\text{kJ/mol}$. UV-C ($< 240\;\text{nm}$) in the stratosphere provides sufficient energy for homolysis.', 'c_haloalkane_environmental', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_alcohols (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Identification of 1°/2°/3°, mechanism of dehydration
-- ============================================================
('A primary alcohol has the $-$OH group attached to a carbon bonded to', 'Three other carbons', 'Two other carbons', 'One other carbon (or none, as in methanol)', 'No hydrogens', 'option3', '1° alcohol: R-CH$_2$OH. The carbon bearing $-$OH has at most one other C attached. E.g., ethanol, 1-propanol.', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Lucas test distinguishes 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols using', 'Conc. HCl/ZnCl$_2$: 3° reacts immediately (turbidity), 2° in 5 min, 1° very slowly', 'NaOH', 'KMnO$_4$', 'FeCl$_3$', 'option1', '3° alcohol: SN1 with ZnCl$_2$/HCl gives immediate turbidity (alkyl chloride). 2°: 5-10 min. 1°: no reaction at RT.', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dehydration of alcohols to alkenes requires', 'Br$_2$', 'NaOH', 'KMnO$_4$', 'Conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ or Al$_2$O$_3$ at high temperature', 'option4', 'E1 mechanism (for 2°/3°): protonation of OH $\to$ loss of H$_2$O $\to$ carbocation $\to$ loss of H$^+$ $\to$ alkene. Zaitsev product.', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Alcohols are prepared by', 'Oxidation of alkanes directly', 'Dehydration only', 'Halogenation', 'Hydration of alkenes (H$_2$O/H$^+$) or reduction of aldehydes/ketones', 'option4', 'Hydration: Markownikoff (acid-catalyzed) or anti-Markownikoff (hydroboration-oxidation). Reduction: NaBH$_4$ or LiAlH$_4$.', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Oxidation of a primary alcohol with PCC gives', 'A carboxylic acid', 'An aldehyde', 'A ketone', 'No reaction', 'option2', 'PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate): mild oxidant. Stops at aldehyde. CrO$_3$/H$_2$SO$_4$ (Jones) goes all the way to carboxylic acid.', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Oxidation of a secondary alcohol gives', 'An aldehyde', 'A ketone', 'A carboxylic acid', 'No reaction', 'option2', '2° alcohol $\xrightarrow{\text{CrO}_3 \text{ or PCC or KMnO}_4}$ ketone. Cannot be further oxidized without breaking C-C bonds.', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Tertiary alcohols resist oxidation because', 'They are too stable', 'There is no H on the carbon bearing $-$OH to be removed', 'They are gases', 'They react with water', 'option2', '3° alcohol: no $\alpha$-H on the carbinol carbon. Oxidation would require C-C bond cleavage (harsh conditions).', 'c_alcohols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ease of dehydration of alcohols follows the order', '1° $>$ 2° $>$ 3°', '3° $>$ 2° $>$ 1°', 'All are equal', '2° $>$ 3° $>$ 1°', 'option2', '3° alcohols dehydrate most easily (most stable carbocation intermediate). Lower temperature needed.', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Victor Meyer test distinguishes 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols by converting them to nitroalkanes. The colours are', '1°: red, 2°: blue, 3°: colourless', '1°: blue, 2°: red, 3°: colourless', '1°: colourless, 2°: blue, 3°: red', 'All give the same colour', 'option1', '1° nitroalkane + HNO$_2$ + NaOH: blood red. 2°: blue. 3°: colourless (no reaction with HNO$_2$).', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ethanol reacts with sodium to give', 'No reaction', 'Sodium hydroxide', 'Ethane', 'Sodium ethoxide + H$_2$', 'option4', '2C$_2$H$_5$OH + 2Na $\to$ 2C$_2$H$_5$ONa + H$_2\uparrow$. Alcohols are weaker acids than water.', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acidity order of alcohols is', '3° $>$ 2° $>$ 1° $>$ CH$_3$OH', 'CH$_3$OH $>$ 1° $>$ 2° $>$ 3° (in gas phase)', 'All are equally acidic', '2° $>$ 1° $>$ 3°', 'option2', 'Gas phase: +I effect of alkyl groups destabilizes alkoxide. CH$_3$O$^-$ is most stable. In solution: solvation effects complicate the order.', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Esterification of an alcohol with a carboxylic acid gives', 'An ester + water', 'An ether', 'An aldehyde', 'A ketone', 'option1', 'RCOOH + R''OH $\xrightleftharpoons[\text{H}^+]{}$ RCOOR'' + H$_2$O. Fischer esterification. Reversible, acid-catalyzed.', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dehydration of ethanol at $140°\text{C}$ with conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ gives', 'Ethene', 'Diethyl ether', 'Acetaldehyde', 'Acetic acid', 'option2', '$140°\text{C}$: intermolecular dehydration $\to$ ether (2C$_2$H$_5$OH $\to$ C$_2$H$_5$OC$_2$H$_5$ + H$_2$O). $170°\text{C}$: intramolecular $\to$ ethene.', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Swern oxidation uses', 'DMSO/(COCl)$_2$/Et$_3$N to oxidize alcohols to aldehydes/ketones under mild conditions', 'KMnO$_4$', 'CrO$_3$', 'O$_3$', 'option1', 'Swern: no heavy metals, mild conditions, no over-oxidation. 1° $\to$ aldehyde, 2° $\to$ ketone.', 'c_alcohols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Bouveault-Blanc reduction uses Na/ethanol to reduce', 'Esters to primary alcohols', 'Ketones to secondary alcohols', 'Acids to aldehydes', 'Alkenes to alkanes', 'option1', 'RCOOR'' + 4Na + 2C$_2$H$_5$OH $\to$ RCH$_2$OH + R''OH + 2C$_2$H$_5$ONa. Now largely replaced by LiAlH$_4$.', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Pinacol-pinacolone rearrangement involves', 'Reduction of a ketone', 'Oxidation of a diol', '1,2-Diol losing water and undergoing 1,2-methyl shift to form a ketone', 'No rearrangement', 'option3', '(CH$_3$)$_2$C(OH)C(OH)(CH$_3$)$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{H}^+}$ (CH$_3$)$_3$CCOCH$_3$ (pinacolone). 1,2-methyl shift to more stable carbocation.', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Oppenauer oxidation uses', 'PCC', 'CrO$_3$', 'KMnO$_4$', 'Aluminium isopropoxide and acetone to oxidize 2° alcohols to ketones', 'option4', 'Transfer hydrogenation: Al(OiPr)$_3$ + acetone (H-acceptor). Mild, selective for 2° alcohols. Reverse of Meerwein-Ponndorf-Verley reduction.', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Retro-pinacol rearrangement and Wagner-Meerwein rearrangement both involve', '1,2-Shifts of alkyl or aryl groups to adjacent carbocation centres', 'Elimination', 'Addition', 'No rearrangement', 'option1', 'Carbocation rearrangements: 1,2-H shift or 1,2-alkyl shift to form a more stable carbocation. Common in dehydration and SN1 reactions.', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Grignard reaction of formaldehyde with RMgX gives', 'A ketone', 'A secondary alcohol', 'A tertiary alcohol', 'A primary alcohol (RCH$_2$OH)', 'option4', 'HCHO + RMgX $\to$ RCH$_2$OMgX $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_3\text{O}^+}$ RCH$_2$OH. Formaldehyde gives 1° alcohol.', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Grignard reaction of a ketone (R$_2$CO) with R''MgX gives', 'A primary alcohol', 'A secondary alcohol', 'A tertiary alcohol', 'An aldehyde', 'option3', 'R$_2$CO + R''MgX $\to$ R$_2$R''COMgX $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_3\text{O}^+}$ R$_2$R''COH (3° alcohol).', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Catalytic dehydrogenation of ethanol over Cu at $300°\text{C}$ gives', 'Ethene', 'Acetaldehyde', 'Diethyl ether', 'Acetic acid', 'option2', 'CH$_3$CH$_2$OH $\xrightarrow{\text{Cu}, 300°\text{C}}$ CH$_3$CHO + H$_2$. Dehydrogenation (not dehydration). 1° alcohol $\to$ aldehyde.', 'c_alcohols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_phenols (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Acidic nature, EAS, halogenation, nitration, Reimer-Tiemann
-- ============================================================
('Phenol is more acidic than ethanol because', 'The phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilized (charge delocalized into the ring)', 'Phenol has a weaker O-H bond', 'Ethanol is aromatic', 'Phenol is a base', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$O$^-$: 5 resonance structures. C$_2$H$_5$O$^-$: no resonance. Phenol $pK_a \approx 10$ vs ethanol $\approx 16$.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol gives a violet colour with', 'NaOH', 'Neutral FeCl$_3$ solution', 'H$_2$SO$_4$', 'KMnO$_4$', 'option2', 'Phenol + FeCl$_3 \to$ [Fe(OC$_6$H$_5$)$_6$]$^{3-}$ (violet/purple complex). Characteristic test for enols and phenols.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol undergoes electrophilic substitution more easily than benzene because', '$-$OH is a strong activating group ($+$M effect donates electron density to the ring)', '$-$OH is deactivating', 'Phenol is not aromatic', 'Benzene is more reactive', 'option1', '$-$OH: strong $+$M (lone pair donation into ring). Activates ortho/para positions strongly.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Bromination of phenol with Br$_2$/H$_2$O gives', 'Monobromophenol', '2,4,6-Tribromophenol (white precipitate)', 'Bromobenzene', 'No reaction', 'option2', 'Phenol is so activated that aqueous Br$_2$ gives trisubstitution directly. For mono: use Br$_2$/CS$_2$ at low temperature.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Kolbe reaction (Kolbe-Schmitt) converts phenol to', 'Benzoic acid', 'Benzaldehyde', 'Salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid)', 'Aniline', 'option3', 'C$_6$H$_5$ONa + CO$_2$ $\xrightarrow{125°\text{C}, 4-7\;\text{atm}}$ o-HOC$_6$H$_4$COONa (sodium salicylate). Electrophilic carboxylation.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Reimer-Tiemann reaction converts phenol to', 'Salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde)', 'Salicylic acid', 'Benzaldehyde', 'Aniline', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$OH + CHCl$_3$ + 3NaOH $\to$ o-HOC$_6$H$_4$CHO. Via dichlorocarbene (:CCl$_2$) electrophile.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol dissolves in NaOH but not in NaHCO$_3$ because', 'Phenol is neutral', 'Phenol is a strong acid', 'NaHCO$_3$ is too strong', 'Phenol ($pK_a \approx 10$) is acidic enough to react with NaOH but too weak to decompose NaHCO$_3$', 'option4', 'Only acids with $pK_a < 6.4$ (stronger than H$_2$CO$_3$) react with NaHCO$_3$. Phenol ($pK_a \approx 10$) cannot.', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electron-withdrawing groups on the ring make phenol', 'Less acidic', 'More acidic (stabilize phenoxide ion further)', 'Neutral', 'Basic', 'option2', 'EWGs ($-$NO$_2$, $-$CN) at ortho/para stabilize the phenoxide anion by dispersing the negative charge. p-Nitrophenol $>$ phenol in acidity.', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acidity order of phenols is', 'Phenol $>$ p-nitrophenol', 'p-Cresol $>$ phenol $>$ p-nitrophenol', 'All are equally acidic', 'p-Nitrophenol $>$ phenol $>$ p-methylphenol (p-cresol)', 'option4', '$-$NO$_2$ ($-$I, $-$M): stabilizes phenoxide $\to$ more acidic. $-$CH$_3$ ($+$I): destabilizes phenoxide $\to$ less acidic.', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Williamson synthesis of phenyl ethers uses', 'Sodium phenoxide + alkyl halide (not aryl halide)', 'Phenol + aryl halide', 'Phenol + alcohol', 'Phenol + acid', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$ONa + CH$_3$I $\to$ C$_6$H$_5$OCH$_3$ (anisole) + NaI. Aryl halides do not work (no S$_N$2 on $sp^2$ C).', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol on treatment with Zn dust gives', 'Benzene', 'Toluene', 'Cyclohexane', 'Aniline', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$OH + Zn $\to$ C$_6$H$_6$ + ZnO. Reduction (removal of $-$OH).', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is prepared by acetylation of', 'Salicylic acid with acetic anhydride', 'Phenol with acetic acid', 'Benzoic acid with ethanol', 'Aniline with acetyl chloride', 'option1', 'o-HOC$_6$H$_4$COOH + (CH$_3$CO)$_2$O $\to$ o-CH$_3$COOC$_6$H$_4$COOH (aspirin) + CH$_3$COOH.', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Bakelite is a polymer of', 'Styrene', 'Ethylene', 'Phenol and formaldehyde', 'Vinyl chloride', 'option3', 'Phenol-formaldehyde resin: thermosetting polymer. Cross-linked 3D network. Used in electrical fittings, handles.', 'c_phenols', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol) is a strong acid because', 'It has no $-$OH group', 'Three $-$NO$_2$ groups strongly stabilize the phenoxide ion by $-$I and $-$M effects', 'It is not a phenol', 'It is a base', 'option2', '$pK_a \approx 0.4$. Three EWGs massively stabilize the anion. Stronger than many carboxylic acids.', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Fries rearrangement converts', 'Phenol to aniline', 'Phenyl esters to hydroxyaryl ketones (o- and p-) using AlCl$_3$', 'Benzene to phenol', 'Ester to acid', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$OCOR $\xrightarrow{\text{AlCl}_3}$ o-/p-HOC$_6$H$_4$COR. Intramolecular acyl migration. Low T: para. High T: ortho.', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Claisen rearrangement of allyl phenyl ether gives', 'No reaction', 'p-Allylphenol', 'Phenol + propene', 'o-Allylphenol', 'option4', 'C$_6$H$_5$OCH$_2$CH=CH$_2$ $\xrightarrow{200°\text{C}}$ o-CH$_2$CH=CH$_2$C$_6$H$_4$OH. [3,3]-Sigmatropic rearrangement.', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dienone-phenol rearrangement involves', 'Reduction of quinone', 'Oxidation of phenol', 'Acid-catalyzed rearrangement of cyclohexadienones to phenols', 'No rearrangement', 'option3', '4,4-Disubstituted cyclohexadienone $\xrightarrow{\text{H}^+}$ 3,4-disubstituted phenol. 1,2-Alkyl shift restores aromaticity.', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Coupling reaction of phenol with diazonium salt gives', 'An ether', 'An ester', 'An azo dye (coloured compound)', 'No reaction', 'option3', 'C$_6$H$_5$OH + ArN$_2^+$ $\to$ Ar-N=N-C$_6$H$_4$OH (p-hydroxyazobenzene). Electrophilic substitution at para position.', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Oxidation of phenol with CrO$_3$ gives', 'Benzoquinone', 'Benzoic acid', 'Catechol', 'Hydroquinone', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$OH $\xrightarrow{\text{CrO}_3}$ p-benzoquinone (O=C$_6$H$_4$=O). Yellow solid. Important in biochemistry (electron transport).', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol is manufactured industrially by the', 'Kolbe reaction', 'Dow process only', 'Direct oxidation of benzene', 'Cumene process (oxidation of cumene to cumene hydroperoxide, then acid cleavage to phenol + acetone)', 'option4', 'C$_6$H$_5$CH(CH$_3$)$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{O}_2}$ C$_6$H$_5$C(CH$_3$)$_2$OOH $\xrightarrow{\text{H}^+}$ C$_6$H$_5$OH + CH$_3$COCH$_3$. Co-product: acetone.', 'c_phenols', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenol is a weaker acid than carboxylic acids because', 'Phenol is a strong acid', 'The phenoxide ion is less stabilized by resonance than the carboxylate ion (charge on O vs delocalized over 2 O atoms)', 'Carboxylic acids are bases', 'They are equally acidic', 'option2', 'RCOO$^-$: charge equally shared between 2 equivalent O atoms. C$_6$H$_5$O$^-$: charge mainly on O with partial delocalization into ring. Carboxylate is more stable.', 'c_phenols', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_ethers (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Ethers: structure, preparation, reactions
-- ============================================================
('Ethers have the general formula', 'R-OH', 'R-O-R'' (two alkyl/aryl groups bonded to oxygen)', 'R-CHO', 'R-COOH', 'option2', 'Ethers: R-O-R''. Symmetric (R = R'') or asymmetric (R $\neq$ R''). E.g., diethyl ether (C$_2$H$_5$OC$_2$H$_5$).', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ethers are prepared by the Williamson synthesis using', 'Oxidation of alcohols', 'Dehydration of two different alcohols', 'Grignard reaction', 'Sodium alkoxide + alkyl halide (S$_N$2)', 'option4', 'R-ONa + R''-X $\to$ R-O-R'' + NaX. Best with 1° alkyl halides. 2°/3° give elimination.', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ethers are relatively unreactive because', 'They are ionic', 'They have double bonds', 'The C-O-C bond is stable and oxygen has no easily ionizable H', 'They are very reactive', 'option3', 'No acidic H (unlike alcohols). Weak Lewis base (lone pairs on O). Used as solvents (diethyl ether, THF).', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ethers are cleaved by', 'NaOH', 'Concentrated HI or HBr at high temperature', 'Water', 'Mild acids', 'option2', 'R-O-R'' + HI $\to$ R-I + R''-OH (or R''-I). Protonation of O makes it a good leaving group. HI is strongest.', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diethyl ether is commonly used as', 'A solvent and was historically used as an anaesthetic', 'A fuel', 'A food additive', 'A fertilizer', 'option1', 'Low bp ($35°\text{C}$), good solvent for Grignard reactions. Highly flammable. Forms explosive peroxides on standing.', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Anisole (methoxybenzene, C$_6$H$_5$OCH$_3$) undergoes EAS preferentially at', 'Meta position', 'Ortho and para positions', 'Only para', 'No reaction', 'option2', '$-$OCH$_3$ is a strong activating group ($+$M). Directs to ortho/para.', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Epoxides (oxiranes) are', 'Six-membered rings', 'Three-membered cyclic ethers that are highly reactive due to ring strain', 'Unreactive', 'Aromatic', 'option2', 'Epoxides: strained 3-membered ring ($\sim 60°$ angles). Opened by nucleophiles (H$_2$O, ROH, RNH$_2$, RMgX).', 'c_ethers', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When anisole is treated with HI, the products are', 'Iodobenzene + CH$_3$OH', 'Phenol + CH$_3$I', 'No reaction', 'Phenol + CH$_3$OH', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$OCH$_3$ + HI $\to$ C$_6$H$_5$OH + CH$_3$I. The C(aryl)-O bond is not broken (too strong). The C(alkyl)-O bond breaks.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acid-catalyzed ring opening of epoxides follows', 'No selectivity', 'Anti-Markownikoff', 'Markownikoff-like regioselectivity (nucleophile attacks the more substituted carbon)', 'Only syn addition', 'option3', 'Acid: protonation creates partial carbocation character at the more substituted C. Nucleophile attacks there. Anti stereochemistry.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Base-catalyzed ring opening of epoxides follows', 'Markownikoff', 'Anti-Markownikoff (nucleophile attacks the less substituted carbon)', 'No selectivity', 'Only syn addition', 'option2', 'Base: S$_N$2-like. Nucleophile attacks the less hindered (less substituted) carbon. Anti stereochemistry.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Crown ethers (e.g., 18-crown-6) are used to', 'Polymerize alkenes', 'Oxidize alcohols', 'Reduce ketones', 'Complex alkali metal cations, making their salts soluble in organic solvents', 'option4', '18-Crown-6 selectively binds K$^+$ (size match). KF/18-crown-6 in benzene: "naked" F$^-$ is a powerful nucleophile.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ethers form peroxides on exposure to air and light. This is dangerous because', 'Peroxides are stable', 'Peroxides are harmless', 'Ethers do not form peroxides', 'Ether peroxides are explosive and can detonate on heating or concentration', 'option4', 'R-O-O-H (hydroperoxide) and R-O-O-R (dialkyl peroxide) form by autoxidation. Always test old ether for peroxides before distilling.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('THF (tetrahydrofuran) is preferred over diethyl ether as a solvent for Grignard reactions because', 'THF is a stronger Lewis base (better coordination to Mg) and has a higher boiling point', 'THF is cheaper', 'Diethyl ether is a better solvent', 'THF is non-polar', 'option1', 'THF: cyclic ether, bp $66°\text{C}$ (vs $35°\text{C}$ for Et$_2$O). Better solvation of RMgX. Miscible with water.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Zeisel method for determination of methoxy ($-$OCH$_3$) groups uses', 'NaOH', 'HI to cleave the ether, then AgNO$_3$ to precipitate AgI', 'KMnO$_4$', 'Br$_2$', 'option2', 'R-OCH$_3$ + HI $\to$ R-OH + CH$_3$I. CH$_3$I + AgNO$_3 \to$ AgI (yellow). Weigh AgI to determine number of $-$OCH$_3$ groups.', 'c_ethers', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Sharpless asymmetric epoxidation uses', 'KMnO$_4$', 'mCPBA', 'OsO$_4$', 'Ti(OiPr)$_4$/TBHP/diethyl tartrate to epoxidize allylic alcohols enantioselectively', 'option4', 'Nobel Prize 2001 (Sharpless). Chiral catalyst gives $>95\%$ ee. Predicts which face of the alkene is epoxidized based on the tartrate enantiomer used.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Claisen rearrangement of allyl vinyl ether gives', 'An alcohol', 'A $\gamma,\delta$-unsaturated carbonyl compound', 'An ester', 'No reaction', 'option2', 'CH$_2$=CHOCH$_2$CH=CH$_2$ $\xrightarrow{200°\text{C}}$ CH$_2$=CHCH$_2$CH$_2$CHO. [3,3]-Sigmatropic rearrangement. Concerted, suprafacial.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Grignard reagents react with epoxides to give', 'Primary alcohols with 2 extra carbons', 'Secondary alcohols', 'Ketones', 'Ethers', 'option1', 'RMgX + ethylene oxide $\to$ RCH$_2$CH$_2$OMgX $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_3\text{O}^+}$ RCH$_2$CH$_2$OH. Extends chain by 2C. S$_N$2 at less substituted C.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dioxane (1,4-dioxane) is a cyclic diether used as', 'A medicine', 'A fuel', 'A food additive', 'A solvent miscible with both water and organic solvents', 'option4', 'Dioxane: 6-membered ring with 2 O atoms. Good solvent. Suspected carcinogen — handle with care.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The boiling point of ethers is much lower than alcohols of similar molecular weight because', 'Ethers have stronger H-bonds', 'Ethers cannot form hydrogen bonds with themselves (no O-H)', 'Ethers are ionic', 'Alcohols have weaker bonds', 'option2', 'Diethyl ether (bp $35°\text{C}$) vs 1-butanol (bp $117°\text{C}$). Both $M \approx 74$. Ethers: only dipole-dipole forces. Alcohols: H-bonding.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is a polymer of', 'Ethylene', 'Ethylene oxide (epoxide ring-opening polymerization)', 'Propylene', 'Styrene', 'option2', '$n$ ethylene oxide $\to$ HO$(-$CH$_2$CH$_2$O$-)_n$H. Water-soluble, non-toxic. Used in pharmaceuticals, cosmetics, and as a phase-transfer catalyst.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Williamson synthesis fails for preparing diaryl ethers (Ar-O-Ar) because', 'Sodium phenoxide is unreactive', 'Aryl halides are too reactive', 'Aryl halides do not undergo S$_N$2 reactions (C-X bond has partial double bond character)', 'Ethers cannot contain aryl groups', 'option3', 'Ar-X: $sp^2$ carbon, resonance-strengthened C-X bond, no backside attack possible. Use Ullmann coupling (Cu catalyst) instead.', 'c_ethers', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_aldehydes_ketones (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Carbonyl group, nucleophilic addition, named reactions
-- ============================================================
('The carbonyl group (C=O) in aldehydes and ketones undergoes', 'Electrophilic addition', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'Electrophilic substitution', 'Free radical substitution', 'option2', 'C=O is polar ($\delta^+$C, $\delta^-$O). Nucleophiles attack the electrophilic carbon. E.g., HCN, NaHSO$_3$, RMgX, NH$_2$OH.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aldehydes are more reactive than ketones towards nucleophilic addition because', 'Aldehydes have less steric hindrance and less electron donation to C=O', 'Ketones have weaker C=O bonds', 'Aldehydes are less polar', 'Ketones are more electrophilic', 'option1', 'Steric: 1 R group vs 2. Electronic: 2 alkyl groups donate more electron density to C=O, reducing electrophilicity.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Tollen''s test (silver mirror test) is positive for', 'Ketones', 'Aldehydes (reduces Ag$^+$ to Ag mirror)', 'Ethers', 'Alkanes', 'option2', 'RCHO + 2[Ag(NH$_3$)$_2$]$^+$ + 2OH$^- \to$ RCOO$^-$ + 2Ag$\downarrow$ + 4NH$_3$ + H$_2$O. Aldehydes are reducing agents.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fehling''s test is positive for', 'Aromatic aldehydes', 'Aliphatic aldehydes (reduces Cu$^{2+}$ to Cu$_2$O red precipitate)', 'Ketones', 'Ethers', 'option2', 'RCHO + 2Cu$^{2+}$ + 5OH$^- \to$ RCOO$^-$ + Cu$_2$O$\downarrow$ (red) + 3H$_2$O. Aromatic aldehydes do not reduce Fehling''s.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The aldol condensation involves', 'Two molecules of an aldehyde (or ketone) with $\alpha$-H combining in the presence of dilute base', 'Oxidation', 'Reduction', 'Halogenation', 'option1', 'CH$_3$CHO + CH$_3$CHO $\xrightarrow{\text{NaOH}}$ CH$_3$CH(OH)CH$_2$CHO (aldol). Dehydration gives $\alpha,\beta$-unsaturated aldehyde.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Cannizzaro reaction occurs with aldehydes that have', '$\alpha$-Hydrogen atoms', 'No $\alpha$-hydrogen atoms (e.g., HCHO, C$_6$H$_5$CHO)', 'Ketone groups', 'Ester groups', 'option2', '2HCHO + NaOH $\to$ HCOONa + CH$_3$OH. One molecule is oxidized (to acid), the other reduced (to alcohol). Disproportionation.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Reduction of aldehydes with NaBH$_4$ gives', 'Carboxylic acids', 'Secondary alcohols', 'Primary alcohols', 'Ketones', 'option3', 'RCHO $\xrightarrow{\text{NaBH}_4}$ RCH$_2$OH. NaBH$_4$: mild, selective (reduces aldehydes/ketones, not esters/acids).', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Grignard reaction of an aldehyde (RCHO) with R''MgX gives', 'A primary alcohol', 'A secondary alcohol (RR''CHOH)', 'A tertiary alcohol', 'A ketone', 'option2', 'RCHO + R''MgX $\to$ RR''CHOMgX $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_3\text{O}^+}$ RR''CHOH (2° alcohol).', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Wittig reaction converts', 'Acids to esters', 'Alkenes to aldehydes', 'Alcohols to ethers', 'Aldehydes/ketones to alkenes using phosphorus ylides (Ph$_3$P=CHR)', 'option4', 'R$_2$C=O + Ph$_3$P=CHR'' $\to$ R$_2$C=CHR'' + Ph$_3$P=O. Powerful C=C forming reaction. Regiospecific.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The haloform reaction occurs with', 'Methyl ketones (RCOCH$_3$) and acetaldehyde, giving CHX$_3$ (haloform)', 'All ketones', 'All aldehydes', 'Ethers', 'option1', 'RCOCH$_3$ + 3X$_2$ + 4NaOH $\to$ RCOONa + CHX$_3$ + 3NaX + 3H$_2$O. Iodoform (CHI$_3$, yellow) is the diagnostic test.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Beckmann rearrangement converts', 'Amines to amides', 'Aldehydes to acids', 'Alcohols to ethers', 'Ketoximes to substituted amides using acid catalyst', 'option4', 'R-C(=NOH)-R'' $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4}$ R''-NH-CO-R (amide). Anti group migrates. Used industrially: cyclohexanone oxime $\to$ caprolactam $\to$ nylon-6.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wolff-Kishner reduction converts C=O to CH$_2$ using', 'Zn-Hg/HCl', 'NH$_2$NH$_2$/KOH/high boiling solvent (ethylene glycol)', 'NaBH$_4$', 'H$_2$/Pd', 'option2', 'Complementary to Clemmensen (which uses acidic conditions). Wolff-Kishner: basic conditions. Both give the same product.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aldehydes react with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP) to give', 'A yellow/orange/red precipitate (2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone)', 'A colourless solution', 'No reaction', 'A blue colour', 'option1', 'RCHO + 2,4-DNP-NH-NH$_2 \to$ RCH=N-NH-DNP + H$_2$O. Used to detect and characterize carbonyl compounds.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Schiff bases are formed by reaction of aldehydes with', 'Tertiary amines', 'Secondary amines', 'Primary amines (RNH$_2$) to give R''CH=NR (imine)', 'Carboxylic acids', 'option3', 'RCHO + R''NH$_2 \to$ RCH=NR'' + H$_2$O. Schiff base (imine). Acid-catalyzed. Important in biochemistry (enzyme mechanisms).', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Baeyer-Villiger oxidation converts ketones to', 'Esters (or lactones from cyclic ketones) using mCPBA or peracids', 'Alcohols', 'Aldehydes', 'Carboxylic acids', 'option1', 'RCOR'' + RCO$_3$H $\to$ RCOOR'' (ester). Migration aptitude: aryl $>$ 3° $>$ 2° $>$ 1° $>$ methyl.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The crossed aldol condensation between two different aldehydes (both with $\alpha$-H) gives', 'A mixture of 4 products (2 self-aldols + 2 crossed aldols)', 'Only one product', 'No reaction', 'Only self-aldol products', 'option1', 'Uncontrolled crossed aldol: statistical mixture. Useful only when one aldehyde has no $\alpha$-H (e.g., HCHO + CH$_3$CHO).', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Claisen-Schmidt condensation is a crossed aldol between', 'An ester and a ketone', 'Two identical aldehydes', 'Two ketones', 'An aromatic aldehyde (no $\alpha$-H) and a ketone with $\alpha$-H', 'option4', 'C$_6$H$_5$CHO + CH$_3$COCH$_3$ $\xrightarrow{\text{NaOH}}$ C$_6$H$_5$CH=CHCOCH$_3$ (chalcone). Only one direction possible.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Perkin reaction gives', 'Alcohols', '$\alpha,\beta$-Unsaturated acids from aromatic aldehydes + acid anhydrides + base', 'Ethers', 'Amines', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$CHO + (CH$_3$CO)$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{CH}_3\text{COONa}}$ C$_6$H$_5$CH=CHCOOH (cinnamic acid). Aldol-type + dehydration.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Knoevenagel condensation involves', 'Two aldehydes', 'An aldehyde + active methylene compound (e.g., malonic ester) + weak base', 'An aldehyde + Grignard reagent', 'A ketone + NaBH$_4$', 'option2', 'RCHO + CH$_2$(COOC$_2$H$_5$)$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{piperidine}}$ RCH=C(COOC$_2$H$_5$)$_2$ + H$_2$O. Useful C=C forming reaction.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Reformatsky reaction uses', 'Zinc + $\alpha$-bromoester + aldehyde/ketone to give $\beta$-hydroxy esters', 'Grignard reagent', 'NaBH$_4$', 'LiAlH$_4$', 'option1', 'BrCH$_2$COOR + Zn $\to$ BrZnCH$_2$COOR (organozinc). Reacts with RCHO to give RCH(OH)CH$_2$COOR.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Tischenko reaction is a variant of the Cannizzaro reaction where', 'A ketone gives an acid', 'An aldehyde gives a ketone', 'An aldehyde disproportionates to give an ester (not acid + alcohol) using Al(OEt)$_3$ catalyst', 'No reaction occurs', 'option3', '2RCHO $\xrightarrow{\text{Al(OEt)}_3}$ RCOOCH$_2$R (ester). Both molecules of aldehyde end up in the ester.', 'c_aldehydes_ketones', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_carboxylic_acids (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Acidic strength and factors affecting it
-- ============================================================
('Carboxylic acids contain the functional group', '$-$OH', '$-$CHO', '$-$COOH (carboxyl group)', '$-$CO$-$', 'option3', 'RCOOH: carboxyl group ($-$C(=O)OH). Combination of carbonyl and hydroxyl.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Carboxylic acids are acidic because', 'They have no ionizable H', 'They have strong O-H bonds', 'They are bases', 'The carboxylate ion (RCOO$^-$) is resonance-stabilized (charge delocalized over two equivalent O atoms)', 'option4', 'RCOOH $\rightleftharpoons$ RCOO$^-$ + H$^+$. The anion has two equivalent resonance structures. $pK_a \approx 4$-$5$.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electron-withdrawing groups on the $\alpha$-carbon make carboxylic acids', 'Weaker', 'Stronger (more acidic)', 'Neutral', 'Basic', 'option2', 'EWGs ($-$Cl, $-$NO$_2$, $-$F) stabilize RCOO$^-$ by dispersing the negative charge ($-$I effect). More stable anion $=$ stronger acid.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electron-donating groups on the $\alpha$-carbon make carboxylic acids', 'Stronger', 'Weaker (less acidic)', 'Neutral', 'More basic', 'option2', 'EDGs ($-$CH$_3$, $-$C$_2$H$_5$) destabilize RCOO$^-$ by pushing electron density towards the negative charge. Weaker acid.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acid strength order is', 'C$_2$H$_5$COOH $>$ CH$_3$COOH $>$ HCOOH', 'HCOOH $>$ CH$_3$COOH $>$ C$_2$H$_5$COOH', 'All are equally acidic', 'CH$_3$COOH $>$ HCOOH', 'option2', 'More alkyl groups $=$ more +I effect $=$ weaker acid. HCOOH ($pK_a = 3.75$) $>$ CH$_3$COOH ($4.76$) $>$ C$_2$H$_5$COOH ($4.87$).', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Carboxylic acids react with NaHCO$_3$ to give', 'No reaction', 'Effervescence of CO$_2$ (sodium salt + CO$_2$ + H$_2$O)', 'A precipitate', 'H$_2$ gas', 'option2', 'RCOOH + NaHCO$_3 \to$ RCOONa + CO$_2\uparrow$ + H$_2$O. Distinguishes acids from phenols (phenols do not react with NaHCO$_3$).', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Carboxylic acids have high boiling points because', 'They are gases', 'They are ionic', 'They have weak bonds', 'They form strong intermolecular hydrogen bonds (dimers)', 'option4', 'RCOOH forms cyclic dimers via two H-bonds. Acetic acid bp $= 118°\text{C}$ (much higher than acetaldehyde $21°\text{C}$, similar MW).', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acid strength order among ClCH$_2$COOH, Cl$_2$CHCOOH, Cl$_3$CCOOH, and CH$_3$COOH is', 'CH$_3$COOH $>$ ClCH$_2$COOH $>$ Cl$_2$CHCOOH $>$ Cl$_3$CCOOH', 'Cl$_3$CCOOH $>$ Cl$_2$CHCOOH $>$ ClCH$_2$COOH $>$ CH$_3$COOH', 'All are equal', 'CH$_3$COOH is strongest', 'option2', 'More Cl $=$ stronger $-$I effect $=$ more stable anion $=$ stronger acid. Cl$_3$CCOOH ($pK_a = 0.65$) is very strong.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Among o-chlorobenzoic acid, m-chlorobenzoic acid, and p-chlorobenzoic acid, the strongest acid is', 'para', 'meta', 'ortho', 'All are equal', 'option3', 'Ortho: $-$I effect is strongest (closest to COOH) + possible intramolecular H-bonding stabilizing the anion. Ortho $>$ para $>$ meta.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Benzoic acid is a weaker acid than formic acid because', 'Benzoic acid has no COOH', 'The phenyl group is electron-donating by resonance ($+$M), destabilizing the carboxylate', 'Formic acid is a base', 'They are equally acidic', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$ group: $-$I (weak) + $+$M (moderate). Net: slightly electron-donating. Destabilizes C$_6$H$_5$COO$^-$ relative to HCOO$^-$.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky (HVZ) reaction halogenates carboxylic acids at the', 'Carbonyl carbon', '$\beta$-Carbon', '$\alpha$-Carbon using X$_2$/P (red phosphorus)', 'Methyl group', 'option3', 'RCHCOOH $\xrightarrow{\text{Br}_2/\text{P}}$ RCBrHCOOH. P converts acid to acid bromide (more reactive). Br$_2$ then substitutes $\alpha$-H.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Decarboxylation of sodium salts of carboxylic acids with soda lime gives', 'Alcohols', 'Alkenes', 'Alkanes (one fewer carbon)', 'Ethers', 'option3', 'RCOONa + NaOH/CaO $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ R-H + Na$_2$CO$_3$. Removes one carbon as CO$_2$.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Esterification of carboxylic acids with alcohols is catalyzed by', 'NaOH', 'Conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ (Fischer esterification)', 'KMnO$_4$', 'Br$_2$', 'option2', 'RCOOH + R''OH $\xrightleftharpoons[\text{H}^+]{}$ RCOOR'' + H$_2$O. Reversible. Excess alcohol or removal of water drives forward.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Reduction of carboxylic acids to primary alcohols requires', 'LiAlH$_4$ (strong reducing agent)', 'NaBH$_4$ (too mild)', 'H$_2$/Pd', 'FeCl$_3$', 'option1', 'RCOOH $\xrightarrow{\text{LiAlH}_4}$ RCH$_2$OH. NaBH$_4$ does not reduce carboxylic acids (only aldehydes/ketones).', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Hunsdiecker reaction converts', 'Acids to alcohols', 'Acids to esters', 'Acids to amides', 'Silver salts of carboxylic acids to alkyl halides (RCOOAg + Br$_2 \to$ RBr + CO$_2$ + AgBr)', 'option4', 'Decarboxylative bromination. RCOOAg + Br$_2 \to$ RBr + CO$_2$ + AgBr. Free radical mechanism.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Arndt-Eistert synthesis converts', 'Carboxylic acids to their next higher homologue (RCOOH $\to$ RCH$_2$COOH) via diazoketone', 'Acids to alcohols', 'Acids to amines', 'Acids to esters', 'option1', 'RCOOH $\to$ RCOCl $\xrightarrow{\text{CH}_2\text{N}_2}$ RCOCHN$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{Ag}_2\text{O/H}_2\text{O}}$ RCH$_2$COOH. Wolff rearrangement of the ketene intermediate.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Kolbe electrolysis of potassium acetate gives', 'Acetic acid', 'Methane', 'Ethanol', 'Ethane', 'option4', '2CH$_3$COOK $\xrightarrow{\text{electrolysis}}$ CH$_3$-CH$_3$ + 2CO$_2$ + 2KOH + H$_2$. Decarboxylative coupling at the anode.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Schmidt reaction converts carboxylic acids to', 'Esters', 'Amines (one fewer carbon) using HN$_3$/H$_2$SO$_4$', 'Alcohols', 'Ketones', 'option2', 'RCOOH + HN$_3$ $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4}$ RNH$_2$ + CO$_2$. Curtius-like rearrangement. Loses one carbon.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetic acid is manufactured industrially by', 'Monsanto process (carbonylation of methanol with CO using Rh/I catalyst)', 'Kolbe electrolysis', 'Oxidation of ethane', 'Fermentation only', 'option1', 'CH$_3$OH + CO $\xrightarrow{\text{Rh/I}^-}$ CH$_3$COOH. Highly selective, mild conditions. Also: Cativa process (Ir catalyst).', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $pK_a$ of trifluoroacetic acid (CF$_3$COOH) is $\sim 0.2$, making it', 'A base', 'A weak acid', 'A very strong acid (comparable to mineral acids)', 'Neutral', 'option3', 'Three F atoms: very strong $-$I effect. CF$_3$COO$^-$ is extremely well-stabilized. Much stronger than CH$_3$COOH ($pK_a = 4.76$).', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Oxalic acid (HOOC-COOH) is a stronger acid than acetic acid because', 'The second COOH group acts as an EWG ($-$I effect) on the first, stabilizing the anion', 'It has more H atoms', 'It is aromatic', 'It has weaker bonds', 'option1', 'Oxalic acid $pK_{a1} = 1.25$ (very strong for an organic acid). The adjacent COOH withdraws electron density powerfully.', 'c_carboxylic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_amines (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Nomenclature, classification, structure, basic character, identification of 1°/2°/3°
-- ============================================================
('Amines are classified as primary, secondary, or tertiary based on', 'The number of carbon groups attached to the nitrogen atom', 'The number of hydrogen atoms in the molecule', 'The molecular weight', 'The boiling point', 'option1', 'Primary (1°): R-NH$_2$ (one C group on N). Secondary (2°): R$_2$NH (two). Tertiary (3°): R$_3$N (three). Classification depends on substitution at N.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The IUPAC name of CH$_3$NH$_2$ is', 'Methylamine', 'Methanamine', 'Aminomethane', 'Methyl azide', 'option2', 'IUPAC: replace -e of parent alkane with -amine. CH$_4$ $\to$ methane $\to$ methanamine. Common name: methylamine.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Which of the following is a secondary amine?', 'C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$', 'CH$_3$NH$_2$', '(CH$_3$)$_3$N', '(CH$_3$)$_2$NH', 'option4', '(CH$_3$)$_2$NH has two methyl groups on N $\to$ secondary amine. CH$_3$NH$_2$ is 1°, (CH$_3$)$_3$N is 3°, aniline is 1° aromatic.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aniline (C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$) is an example of', 'A primary aromatic amine', 'A secondary amine', 'A tertiary amine', 'An amide', 'option1', 'One aryl group (C$_6$H$_5$) directly bonded to NH$_2$. Only one C-group on N $\to$ primary amine. Aromatic because the C-group is an aryl ring.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The geometry around the nitrogen atom in amines is', 'Pyramidal (sp$^3$ hybridized with one lone pair)', 'Planar', 'Linear', 'Tetrahedral with no lone pair', 'option1', 'N in amines: 3 bond pairs + 1 lone pair $\to$ sp$^3$ hybridized $\to$ pyramidal shape. Bond angle $\approx 107°$.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Amines are basic because', 'The lone pair on nitrogen can accept a proton', 'They contain OH groups', 'They are acidic', 'They have no lone pairs', 'option1', 'R-NH$_2$ + H$^+$ $\to$ R-NH$_3^+$. The lone pair on N acts as a Lewis base / Brønsted base by accepting H$^+$.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Hinsberg test is used to distinguish between', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Aldehydes and ketones', 'Primary, secondary, and tertiary amines', 'Alkanes and alkenes', 'option3', 'Hinsberg reagent: benzenesulfonyl chloride (C$_6$H$_5$SO$_2$Cl). 1° amine $\to$ soluble in NaOH. 2° amine $\to$ insoluble product. 3° amine $\to$ no reaction.', 'c_amines', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Among aliphatic amines in aqueous solution, the correct basicity order is', 'NH$_3$ $>$ CH$_3$NH$_2$ $>$ (CH$_3$)$_2$NH $>$ (CH$_3$)$_3$N', '(CH$_3$)$_2$NH $>$ CH$_3$NH$_2$ $>$ (CH$_3$)$_3$N $>$ NH$_3$', '(CH$_3$)$_3$N $>$ (CH$_3$)$_2$NH $>$ CH$_3$NH$_2$ $>$ NH$_3$', 'All are equally basic', 'option2', 'In water: 2° $>$ 1° $>$ 3° $>$ NH$_3$. Interplay of +I effect (increases basicity) and solvation/steric effects (3° poorly solvated $\to$ lower basicity in water).', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aniline is a weaker base than cyclohexylamine because', 'Aniline has no lone pair', 'Aniline has more H atoms', 'Cyclohexylamine is aromatic', 'The lone pair on N in aniline is delocalized into the benzene ring (+M effect)', 'option4', 'In aniline, the N lone pair participates in resonance with the ring, making it less available for protonation. $pK_b$ aniline $\approx 9.4$ vs cyclohexylamine $\approx 3.3$.', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The carbylamine (isocyanide) test is specific for', 'Primary amines only', 'Secondary amines', 'Tertiary amines', 'All amines', 'option1', 'R-NH$_2$ + CHCl$_3$ + 3KOH $\to$ R-NC (isocyanide, foul smell) + 3KCl + 3H$_2$O. Only 1° amines give this test.', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When a primary amine reacts with nitrous acid (NaNO$_2$/HCl) at 0-5°C, the product depends on whether the amine is aliphatic or aromatic. An aliphatic 1° amine gives', 'A diazonium salt', 'An alcohol with evolution of N$_2$ gas', 'No reaction', 'An amide', 'option2', 'R-NH$_2$ + HNO$_2$ $\to$ R-OH + N$_2\uparrow$ + H$_2$O. Aliphatic diazonium ions are unstable and decompose immediately to give alcohol + N$_2$.', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is used to prepare', 'Pure primary amines', 'Secondary amines', 'Tertiary amines', 'Quaternary ammonium salts', 'option1', 'Potassium phthalimide + R-X $\to$ N-alkylphthalimide $\xrightarrow{\text{N}_2\text{H}_4}$ R-NH$_2$ + phthalhydrazide. Gives only 1° amines (no over-alkylation).', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hofmann bromamide degradation converts', 'A primary amide to a primary amine with one fewer carbon (RCONH$_2$ $\to$ RNH$_2$)', 'An amine to an amide', 'A ketone to an amine', 'An ester to an amine', 'option1', 'RCONH$_2$ + Br$_2$ + 4NaOH $\to$ RNH$_2$ + Na$_2$CO$_3$ + 2NaBr + 2H$_2$O. Involves nitrene intermediate and 1,2-shift.', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electron-withdrawing groups on the ring of aniline make it', 'A stronger base', 'A weaker base (less basic)', 'Neutral', 'An acid', 'option2', 'EWGs ($-$NO$_2$, $-$CN, $-$COOH) reduce electron density on N, making the lone pair less available. p-Nitroaniline ($pK_b \approx 13$) is much weaker than aniline ($pK_b \approx 9.4$).', 'c_amines', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The correct order of basic strength among p-toluidine, aniline, and p-nitroaniline is', 'p-Nitroaniline $>$ aniline $>$ p-toluidine', 'p-Toluidine $>$ aniline $>$ p-nitroaniline', 'All are equally basic', 'Aniline $>$ p-toluidine $>$ p-nitroaniline', 'option2', 'p-CH$_3$ is EDG (+I, weak +M) $\to$ increases basicity. p-NO$_2$ is EWG ($-$I, $-$M) $\to$ decreases basicity. Order: p-toluidine $>$ aniline $>$ p-nitroaniline.', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Hofmann elimination, a quaternary ammonium hydroxide on heating gives', 'The less substituted (Hofmann) alkene + tertiary amine', 'The more substituted (Zaitsev) alkene', 'An alcohol', 'A carboxylic acid', 'option1', 'R$_4$N$^+$OH$^-$ $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ less substituted alkene + R$_3$N + H$_2$O. Anti-Zaitsev due to bulky leaving group (NR$_3$). E2 mechanism with steric control.', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Coupling of aniline with benzenediazonium chloride occurs at', 'The nitrogen of aniline', 'The meta position', 'The para position of aniline (forming p-aminoazobenzene)', 'The ortho position only', 'option3', 'Diazonium ion is a weak electrophile. Aniline (activated ring) undergoes electrophilic substitution preferentially at para position. Product: p-aminoazobenzene (yellow dye).', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetylation of aniline with acetic anhydride is done before nitration because', 'It converts aniline to a phenol', 'Aniline cannot be nitrated', 'Acetylation increases the yield of meta product', 'The $-$NHCOCH$_3$ group protects N from oxidation and is a moderate activator giving mainly the para product', 'option4', 'Free $-$NH$_2$ is strongly activating and gets oxidized by HNO$_3$. Acetylation reduces activation, prevents oxidation, and gives cleaner p-nitroacetanilide. Hydrolysis then gives p-nitroaniline.', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The reaction of a secondary amine with nitrous acid gives', 'No reaction', 'A diazonium salt', 'An alcohol', 'An N-nitrosoamine (R$_2$N-NO), a yellow oily substance', 'option4', 'R$_2$NH + HNO$_2$ $\to$ R$_2$N-NO + H$_2$O. N-Nitrosoamines are carcinogenic. This is a characteristic test for 2° amines.', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Arrange the following in decreasing order of basicity: (i) C$_2$H$_5$NH$_2$, (ii) C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$, (iii) (C$_2$H$_5$)$_2$NH, (iv) C$_6$H$_5$NHCH$_3$', '(i) $>$ (iii) $>$ (ii) $>$ (iv)', '(ii) $>$ (iv) $>$ (i) $>$ (iii)', '(iii) $>$ (i) $>$ (iv) $>$ (ii)', 'All are equal', 'option3', '(iii) diethylamine (2° aliphatic, strong +I) $>$ (i) ethylamine (1° aliphatic) $>$ (iv) N-methylaniline (N lone pair still delocalized but CH$_3$ adds some +I) $>$ (ii) aniline (lone pair most delocalized).', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Mustard oil reaction (R-NCS formation) is given by', 'Tertiary amines', 'Secondary amines', 'Primary amines with CS$_2$ and HgCl$_2$', 'Quaternary salts', 'option3', 'R-NH$_2$ + CS$_2$ $\to$ R-NHCS$_2$H $\xrightarrow{\text{HgCl}_2}$ R-NCS (alkyl isothiocyanate, pungent smell) + HgS + 2HCl. Specific for 1° amines.', 'c_amines', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_diazonium_salts (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Importance in synthetic organic chemistry
-- ============================================================
('Diazonium salts have the general formula', 'ArNH$_2$', 'ArN$_2^+$X$^-$', 'ArNO$_2$', 'ArCOOH', 'option2', 'Formed by treating aromatic primary amines with NaNO$_2$ + HCl at 0-5°C (diazotization). Ar-NH$_2$ $\to$ Ar-N$_2^+$Cl$^-$.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diazotization is carried out at 0-5°C because', 'HCl evaporates at room temperature', 'The reaction does not occur at low temperature', 'Diazonium salts decompose at higher temperatures', 'NaNO$_2$ is unstable at all temperatures', 'option3', 'Ar-N$_2^+$ is unstable and decomposes above 5°C releasing N$_2$ gas. Low temperature preserves the diazonium ion.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Sandmeyer reaction replaces the diazonium group with', 'Cl, Br, or CN using Cu$_2$Cl$_2$, Cu$_2$Br$_2$, or CuCN respectively', 'Only hydrogen', 'Only hydroxyl', 'Only fluorine', 'option1', 'ArN$_2^+$Cl$^-$ + CuCl $\to$ ArCl + N$_2$. Similarly for Br and CN. Cu(I) salts catalyze the substitution.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When a diazonium salt is treated with water on warming, the product is', 'A nitro compound', 'An amine', 'A phenol (ArOH) with loss of N$_2$', 'An ether', 'option3', 'ArN$_2^+$ + H$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ ArOH + N$_2\uparrow$ + H$^+$. Hydrolysis replaces $-$N$_2^+$ with $-$OH.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diazonium salts are important in synthesis because', 'They are stable indefinitely', 'They allow introduction of many different groups ($-$OH, $-$X, $-$CN, $-$NO$_2$, $-$H) onto an aromatic ring via the $-$N$_2^+$ group', 'They are naturally occurring', 'They are used only as dyes', 'option2', 'The $-$N$_2^+$ group is an excellent leaving group (N$_2$ is very stable). This makes diazonium salts versatile synthetic intermediates.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Balz-Schiemann reaction converts a diazonium salt to', 'A phenol', 'An aryl chloride', 'An aryl bromide', 'An aryl fluoride (ArF) using HBF$_4$', 'option4', 'ArN$_2^+$Cl$^-$ + HBF$_4$ $\to$ ArN$_2^+$BF$_4^-$ $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ ArF + BF$_3$ + N$_2$. Best method for introducing F onto an aromatic ring.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Azo dyes are formed when diazonium salts react with', 'Mineral acids', 'Alkanes', 'Activated aromatic compounds (phenols or amines) in a coupling reaction', 'Alkyl halides', 'option3', 'ArN$_2^+$ + Ar''OH $\to$ Ar-N=N-Ar''OH (azo compound). The $-$N=N$-$ chromophore gives intense colour. Used in dye industry.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Gattermann reaction, the diazonium group is replaced by', 'F only', 'Cl or Br using Cu powder (instead of Cu$_2$X$_2$ in Sandmeyer)', 'OH only', 'NH$_2$', 'option2', 'Similar to Sandmeyer but uses Cu powder as catalyst instead of cuprous salts. ArN$_2^+$Cl$^-$ $\xrightarrow{\text{Cu}}$ ArCl + N$_2$.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Replacement of the diazonium group by $-$H (deamination) is achieved using', 'Hypophosphorous acid (H$_3$PO$_2$) or ethanol', 'Conc. H$_2$SO$_4$', 'NaOH', 'KMnO$_4$', 'option1', 'ArN$_2^+$ + H$_3$PO$_2$ $\to$ ArH + N$_2$ + H$_3$PO$_3$. Useful for removing the NH$_2$ group after it has served as a directing group.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To synthesize 1,3,5-tribromobenzene from aniline, the correct sequence is', 'Diazotize, then brominate, then reduce diazonium', 'Brominate aniline to 2,4,6-tribromoaniline, then diazotize, then replace $-$N$_2^+$ with $-$H using H$_3$PO$_2$', 'Directly brominate benzene', 'Nitrate first, then reduce', 'option2', 'Aniline + 3Br$_2$ $\to$ 2,4,6-tribromoaniline. Diazotize $\to$ diazonium salt. H$_3$PO$_2$ replaces $-$N$_2^+$ with $-$H $\to$ 1,3,5-tribromobenzene.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The coupling reaction of diazonium salts with phenol occurs preferentially at', 'The para position of phenol in weakly alkaline medium', 'The meta position', 'The ortho position only', 'The nitrogen of phenol', 'option1', 'Phenol is activated by $-$OH group. In alkaline medium, phenoxide ion (more activated) couples at para position. If para is blocked, ortho coupling occurs.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Methyl orange indicator is synthesized by coupling benzenediazonium chloride with', 'Dimethylaniline (N,N-dimethylaniline) in acidic medium', 'Phenol', 'Toluene', 'Benzene', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$N$_2^+$Cl$^-$ + (CH$_3$)$_2$NC$_6$H$_5$ $\to$ p-(CH$_3$)$_2$NC$_6$H$_4$N=NC$_6$H$_5$ (methyl orange). Coupling at para to $-$N(CH$_3$)$_2$.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aryl diazonium salts are more stable than alkyl diazonium salts because', 'The positive charge on N is stabilized by resonance with the aromatic ring', 'They have higher molecular weight', 'They are ionic', 'Alkyl groups donate electrons', 'option1', 'The $-$N$_2^+$ group is conjugated with the $\pi$-system of the ring, delocalizing the positive charge. Aliphatic diazonium ions lack this stabilization and decompose instantly.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The diazonium group can be replaced by $-$NO$_2$ using', 'HNO$_3$ directly', 'NaNO$_2$ in the presence of Cu (diazo replacement)', 'NaOH', 'H$_2$SO$_4$', 'option2', 'ArN$_2^+$ + NaNO$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{Cu}}$ ArNO$_2$ + N$_2$ + NaCl. Useful for placing $-$NO$_2$ at positions not accessible by direct nitration.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To prepare m-bromochlorobenzene from aniline, the correct sequence is', 'Diazotize aniline, Sandmeyer with CuCl to get chlorobenzene, then brominate (meta director absent, gives mixture)', 'Acetylate aniline, brominate (para to NHCOCH$_3$), hydrolyze, diazotize, Sandmeyer with CuCl', 'Directly brominate aniline', 'Nitrate aniline first', 'option2', 'Acetylation protects NH$_2$ and directs Br to para. After hydrolysis: p-bromoaniline. Diazotize, then Sandmeyer (CuCl) replaces $-$N$_2^+$ with Cl at the position where NH$_2$ was (meta to Br).', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Schiemann reaction is preferred for introducing fluorine because', 'Fluorine is cheap', 'Direct fluorination of aromatics is too violent and non-selective; ArN$_2^+$BF$_4^-$ decomposes cleanly to ArF', 'It gives meta products', 'HF is a strong acid', 'option2', 'F$_2$ reacts explosively with aromatics. The Schiemann route (via stable ArN$_2^+$BF$_4^-$ salt) provides controlled, regioselective fluorination.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To synthesize p-cresol (4-methylphenol) from toluene, a viable route using diazonium chemistry is', 'Friedel-Crafts acylation', 'Directly oxidize toluene', 'Brominate toluene', 'Nitrate toluene (para), reduce $-$NO$_2$ to $-$NH$_2$, diazotize, hydrolyze with water', 'option4', 'Toluene $\xrightarrow{\text{HNO}_3/\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4}$ p-nitrotoluene $\xrightarrow{\text{Sn/HCl}}$ p-toluidine $\xrightarrow{\text{NaNO}_2/\text{HCl}}$ diazonium salt $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{O}/\Delta}$ p-cresol.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the coupling reaction, the diazonium ion acts as', 'A nucleophile', 'A weak electrophile that attacks electron-rich aromatic rings', 'A free radical', 'A reducing agent', 'option2', 'ArN$_2^+$ is a weak electrophile (positive charge on N). It can only attack highly activated rings (phenols, amines). Does not couple with deactivated rings.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The diazonium salt of p-nitroaniline is more stable than that of aniline because', '$-$NO$_2$ is an EDG', 'It has higher molecular weight', 'The $-$NO$_2$ group (EWG) disperses the positive charge on the diazonium group by $-$M and $-$I effects', 'p-Nitroaniline is more basic', 'option3', 'EWGs stabilize the positive charge on $-$N$_2^+$ by withdrawing electron density. More stable diazonium $=$ slower decomposition.', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To convert aniline to iodobenzene, the best method is', 'Diazotize aniline, then treat with KI (ArN$_2^+$ + KI $\to$ ArI + N$_2$ + KCl)', 'Direct iodination with I$_2$', 'Sandmeyer reaction with CuI', 'Treat with HI', 'option1', 'ArN$_2^+$ + KI $\to$ ArI + N$_2$ + KCl. No Cu catalyst needed for iodine replacement (unlike Cl, Br, CN which need Sandmeyer conditions).', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Congo red indicator is an example of', 'An anthraquinone dye', 'A monoacid dye', 'A triphenylmethane dye', 'A bisazo dye containing two $-$N=N$-$ linkages', 'option4', 'Congo red is made by coupling tetrazotized benzidine with two molecules of naphthionic acid. Contains two azo ($-$N=N$-$) groups. Changes colour from red (pH $>$ 5) to blue (pH $<$ 3).', 'c_diazonium_salts', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_carbohydrates (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Classification, aldoses/ketoses, monosaccharides (glucose, fructose), oligosaccharides
-- ============================================================
('Carbohydrates are chemically defined as', 'Polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones, or substances that yield them on hydrolysis', 'Polymers of amino acids', 'Esters of fatty acids', 'Nucleotide chains', 'option1', 'General formula: C$_x$(H$_2$O)$_y$. Contain multiple $-$OH groups and either an aldehyde (aldose) or ketone (ketose) functional group.', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Glucose is classified as an aldohexose because it', 'Contains an aldehyde group and six carbon atoms', 'Contains a ketone group and five carbons', 'Is a disaccharide', 'Has no functional group', 'option1', 'Aldo = aldehyde functional group. Hexose = 6 carbons. Glucose: CH$_2$OH(CHOH)$_4$CHO. Molecular formula: C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$.', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fructose is classified as a', 'Ketohexose', 'Aldohexose', 'Aldopentose', 'Disaccharide', 'option1', 'Fructose has a ketone group at C-2 and six carbon atoms. Keto = ketone. Hexose = 6C. Also C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$ (isomer of glucose).', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of', 'Two fructose units', 'Two glucose units', 'Glucose and fructose linked by a glycosidic bond', 'Glucose and galactose', 'option3', 'Sucrose = $\alpha$-D-glucopyranose + $\beta$-D-fructofuranose joined by $\alpha$-1,2 glycosidic bond. Non-reducing sugar (both anomeric carbons involved in bond).', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Monosaccharides that cannot be hydrolyzed further into simpler sugars include', 'Sucrose and maltose', 'Glucose, fructose, and galactose', 'Starch and cellulose', 'Lactose only', 'option2', 'Monosaccharides are the simplest carbohydrates. They cannot be broken down by hydrolysis. Examples: glucose, fructose, galactose, ribose.', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Starch is a polysaccharide composed of', 'Amylose (linear $\alpha$-1,4 linked glucose) and amylopectin (branched $\alpha$-1,4 and $\alpha$-1,6 linked glucose)', 'Fructose units only', 'Amino acids', 'Nucleotides', 'option1', 'Starch: 20% amylose (linear, helical) + 80% amylopectin (branched). Both made of $\alpha$-D-glucose. Gives blue colour with iodine (amylose).', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Cellulose differs from starch in that', 'Starch is a protein', 'Cellulose is made of fructose', 'Cellulose has $\beta$-1,4 glycosidic linkages while starch has $\alpha$-1,4 linkages', 'They are identical', 'option3', '$\beta$-linkages in cellulose create straight chains with extensive H-bonding $\to$ rigid fibrous structure. Humans lack enzymes to digest $\beta$-linkages.', 'c_carbohydrates', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Glucose gives a silver mirror with Tollens'' reagent because', 'It has no reducing group', 'It is a ketone', 'It is an acid', 'It contains a free (or hemiacetal) aldehyde group that can be oxidized', 'option4', 'The open-chain form of glucose has a free $-$CHO group. CHO $\to$ COO$^-$ (oxidized). Ag$^+$ $\to$ Ag (reduced, silver mirror). Glucose is a reducing sugar.', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Mutarotation in glucose refers to', 'Polymerization', 'The loss of optical activity', 'Racemization', 'The change in specific optical rotation when $\alpha$ or $\beta$ anomers equilibrate in solution', 'option4', '$\alpha$-D-glucose ($[\alpha] = +112°$) and $\beta$-D-glucose ($[\alpha] = +18.7°$) equilibrate in water to give $[\alpha] = +52.7°$. Ring opening/closing interconverts anomers.', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar because', 'Both anomeric carbons (C-1 of glucose and C-2 of fructose) are involved in the glycosidic bond', 'It has no OH groups', 'It is a monosaccharide', 'It gives a positive Tollens'' test', 'option1', 'No free anomeric carbon $\to$ cannot open to give a free aldehyde or ketone $\to$ cannot reduce Tollens'' or Fehling''s reagent.', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Maltose on hydrolysis gives', 'Glucose and fructose', 'Two molecules of glucose', 'Glucose and galactose', 'Two molecules of fructose', 'option2', 'Maltose = two $\alpha$-D-glucose units joined by $\alpha$-1,4 glycosidic bond. Hydrolysis: maltose + H$_2$O $\to$ 2 glucose. It is a reducing sugar (free anomeric C on second glucose).', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Lactose is composed of', 'Galactose and glucose linked by a $\beta$-1,4 glycosidic bond', 'Two glucose units', 'Glucose and fructose', 'Two galactose units', 'option1', 'Lactose (milk sugar) = $\beta$-D-galactose + $\alpha/\beta$-D-glucose. $\beta$-1,4 linkage from galactose C-1 to glucose C-4. Reducing sugar (free anomeric C on glucose).', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The osazone test with phenylhydrazine is used to identify sugars. Glucose and fructose give the same osazone because', 'Phenylhydrazine does not react with sugars', 'They are identical', 'They have different molecular formulae', 'They differ only at C-1 and C-2, and osazone formation involves C-1 and C-2', 'option4', 'Osazone reacts at C-1 and C-2 (converting both to phenylhydrazones). Since glucose and fructose differ only at C-1 and C-2, the rest of the chain is identical $\to$ same osazone.', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Glucose and mannose give different osazones because they differ at C-2 only. However, glucose and fructose give the same osazone. This means glucose and mannose are', 'Enantiomers', 'C-2 epimers', 'Identical', 'Anomers', 'option2', 'Epimers differ at only one chiral centre. Glucose and mannose differ only at C-2. Since osazone destroys C-1 and C-2 configurations, glucose-mannose osazones differ (different C-3, C-4, C-5 not affected but C-2 configuration matters in the osazone).', 'c_carbohydrates', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Haworth projection of $\alpha$-D-glucopyranose, the $-$OH on the anomeric carbon (C-1) is', 'Below the ring plane (axial, trans to the CH$_2$OH group at C-5)', 'Above the ring plane', 'In the plane of the ring', 'Absent', 'option1', 'In $\alpha$-anomer: the $-$OH at C-1 is on the opposite side (trans) to the $-$CH$_2$OH at C-5 in Haworth projection $\to$ below the ring. In $\beta$-anomer, it is on the same side (cis) $\to$ above.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Inversion of sucrose refers to', 'Polymerization of sucrose', 'Hydrolysis of sucrose to glucose and fructose, with a change in sign of optical rotation from dextro to levo', 'Oxidation of sucrose', 'Dehydration of sucrose', 'option2', 'Sucrose ($[\alpha] = +66.5°$) $\to$ glucose ($+52.7°$) + fructose ($-92°$). Net rotation becomes negative (levo). The sign inverts, hence "inversion." The mixture is called invert sugar.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('D and L configuration of sugars is determined by', 'The molecular weight', 'The sign of optical rotation', 'The configuration at the highest-numbered chiral centre (penultimate carbon) compared to D- or L-glyceraldehyde', 'The number of OH groups', 'option3', 'If the $-$OH on the penultimate C (C-5 in hexoses) is on the right in Fischer projection $\to$ D-sugar. On the left $\to$ L-sugar. D/L is NOT related to (+)/($-$) rotation.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Glucose does not give Schiff''s test (or gives a very faint reaction) despite having an aldehyde group because', 'In solution, glucose exists predominantly in the cyclic hemiacetal form with very little free aldehyde', 'Glucose has no aldehyde group', 'Schiff''s reagent is too weak', 'Glucose is a ketone', 'option1', 'Only $\sim 0.003\%$ of glucose is in the open-chain aldehyde form in solution. The cyclic pyranose form dominates. Tollens'' and Fehling''s work because they shift the equilibrium.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of stereoisomers possible for aldohexoses is', '8', '16', '4', '32', 'option2', 'Aldohexoses have 4 chiral centres (C-2, C-3, C-4, C-5). Number of stereoisomers $= 2^4 = 16$ (8 D-forms + 8 L-forms). Includes glucose, mannose, galactose, etc.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Glycogen is the storage polysaccharide in animals and differs from amylopectin in that', 'They are identical', 'Glycogen is made of fructose', 'Amylopectin is unbranched', 'Glycogen is more highly branched ($\alpha$-1,6 branches every 8-12 glucose units vs 24-30 in amylopectin)', 'option4', 'More branching in glycogen $\to$ more non-reducing ends $\to$ faster mobilization of glucose when energy is needed. Stored mainly in liver and muscle.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Glucose reacts with acetic anhydride to form glucose pentaacetate, confirming the presence of', 'An aldehyde group only', 'Three OH groups', 'Five free hydroxyl groups in glucose', 'No functional groups', 'option3', 'C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$ + 5(CH$_3$CO)$_2$O $\to$ glucose pentaacetate + 5CH$_3$COOH. Five $-$OH groups are acetylated, confirming five hydroxyl groups in glucose.', 'c_carbohydrates', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_proteins (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Amino acids, peptide bond, polypeptides, protein structure, denaturation, enzymes
-- ============================================================
('Amino acids contain both', 'An amino group ($-$NH$_2$) and a carboxyl group ($-$COOH)', 'Only an amino group', 'Only a carboxyl group', 'A nitro group and a hydroxyl group', 'option1', '$\alpha$-Amino acids: NH$_2$-CHR-COOH. The amino and carboxyl groups are on the same carbon ($\alpha$-carbon). R is the side chain that varies.', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A peptide bond is formed between two amino acids by', 'A substitution reaction', 'An addition reaction', 'A condensation reaction between the $-$COOH of one and the $-$NH$_2$ of another, releasing H$_2$O', 'An oxidation reaction', 'option3', '$-$CO-NH$-$ is the peptide bond (amide linkage). R$_1$CH(NH$_2$)COOH + H$_2$NCHR$_2$COOH $\to$ R$_1$CH(NH$_2$)CO-NHCHR$_2$COOH + H$_2$O.', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Proteins are polymers of', 'Nucleotides', 'Monosaccharides', '$\alpha$-Amino acids linked by peptide bonds', 'Fatty acids', 'option3', 'Proteins contain 20 different naturally occurring $\alpha$-amino acids. The sequence of amino acids determines the protein''s structure and function.', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The primary structure of a protein refers to', 'The linear sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain', 'The helical structure', 'The 3D folding', 'The association of multiple subunits', 'option1', 'Primary structure: the order of amino acids from N-terminus to C-terminus. Determined by the gene encoding the protein. Any change can alter function.', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Denaturation of a protein involves', 'Polymerization', 'Breaking of peptide bonds', 'Hydrolysis to amino acids', 'Loss of secondary, tertiary, and quaternary structure without breaking peptide bonds', 'option4', 'Denaturation disrupts H-bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges, and hydrophobic interactions. The protein unfolds and loses biological activity. Caused by heat, pH change, heavy metals.', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Enzymes are biological catalysts that are chemically', 'Nucleic acids only', 'Carbohydrates', 'Lipids', 'Proteins (with some exceptions like ribozymes)', 'option4', 'Enzymes are globular proteins with specific active sites. They lower activation energy and are highly specific. Named with suffix $-$ase (e.g., lipase, amylase).', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At the isoelectric point (pI), an amino acid exists as', 'A neutral molecule with no charges', 'A cation', 'An anion', 'A zwitterion with no net charge', 'option4', 'At pI: NH$_3^+$-CHR-COO$^-$ (zwitterion). Equal positive and negative charges $\to$ net charge = 0. Does not migrate in an electric field at pI.', 'c_proteins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The secondary structure of proteins includes', '$\alpha$-Helix and $\beta$-pleated sheet, stabilized by hydrogen bonds between C=O and N-H of the peptide backbone', 'Only the amino acid sequence', 'Disulfide bonds between distant residues', 'Subunit association', 'option1', '$\alpha$-Helix: right-handed coil, H-bonds between C=O of residue $i$ and N-H of residue $i+4$. $\beta$-Sheet: extended chains with inter-strand H-bonds.', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The tertiary structure of a protein is stabilized by', 'Van der Waals forces only', 'Only peptide bonds', 'Only H-bonds in the backbone', 'Hydrophobic interactions, disulfide bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrogen bonds between R-groups', 'option4', 'Tertiary structure: overall 3D folding of a single polypeptide. Hydrophobic core, disulfide bridges ($-$S$-$S$-$) between cysteine residues, salt bridges, H-bonds between side chains.', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Quaternary structure of proteins refers to', 'The $\alpha$-helix', 'The amino acid sequence', 'The arrangement of two or more polypeptide subunits into a functional protein complex', 'The peptide bond', 'option3', 'Example: hemoglobin has 4 subunits ($2\alpha + 2\beta$). Quaternary structure is stabilized by the same forces as tertiary structure (non-covalent + sometimes disulfide).', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ninhydrin test gives a purple colour with', 'All $\alpha$-amino acids (except proline, which gives yellow)', 'Only glucose', 'Only lipids', 'Only nucleic acids', 'option1', 'Ninhydrin reacts with the free $-$NH$_2$ group of amino acids to give Ruhemann''s purple (diketohydrindylidene-diketohydrindamine). Used to detect amino acids in chromatography.', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The biuret test detects', 'Proteins and peptides containing two or more peptide bonds (violet colour with Cu$^{2+}$/NaOH)', 'Monosaccharides', 'Lipids', 'Single amino acids', 'option1', 'Cu$^{2+}$ coordinates with the nitrogen atoms of peptide bonds in alkaline solution $\to$ violet/purple complex. Requires $\geq 2$ peptide bonds. Single amino acids give negative result.', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fibrous proteins differ from globular proteins in that', 'Globular proteins are insoluble', 'Fibrous proteins are spherical', 'Fibrous proteins have elongated shapes, are insoluble in water, and serve structural roles (e.g., keratin, collagen)', 'They have the same shape', 'option3', 'Fibrous: long, thread-like, insoluble, structural (keratin in hair, collagen in connective tissue). Globular: spherical, soluble, functional (enzymes, antibodies, hemoglobin).', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Essential amino acids are those that', 'Are not found in proteins', 'Are synthesized by the body', 'Cannot be synthesized by the human body and must be obtained from diet', 'Have no amino group', 'option3', 'Humans cannot synthesize 8-10 amino acids (e.g., valine, leucine, isoleucine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, methionine, threonine, lysine). Must come from food.', 'c_proteins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In gel electrophoresis with SDS-PAGE, proteins are separated based on', 'Solubility', 'Isoelectric point', 'Colour', 'Molecular weight (SDS denatures proteins and gives uniform charge-to-mass ratio)', 'option4', 'SDS (sodium dodecyl sulfate) coats proteins with negative charge proportional to mass. In the gel, smaller proteins migrate faster. Used to estimate molecular weight.', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The xanthoproteic test gives a yellow colour with proteins containing', 'Sulfur-containing amino acids', 'Aromatic amino acids (tyrosine, tryptophan, phenylalanine) due to nitration of the aromatic ring', 'Basic amino acids', 'All amino acids equally', 'option2', 'Conc. HNO$_3$ nitrates the aromatic ring $\to$ yellow colour. On adding NaOH, the colour deepens to orange (salt formation of the nitro-phenol).', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Millon''s test is specific for proteins containing', 'Glycine', 'Cysteine', 'Tyrosine (phenolic amino acid) — gives a red precipitate with Hg(NO$_3$)$_2$/HNO$_3$', 'Proline', 'option3', 'Hg$^{2+}$ reacts with the phenol ring of tyrosine to form a red mercury-phenol complex. Positive for any protein containing tyrosine residues.', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The lead acetate test (black precipitate of PbS) is given by proteins containing', 'Sulfur-containing amino acids (cysteine, methionine)', 'Aromatic amino acids', 'Basic amino acids', 'Acidic amino acids', 'option2', 'Heating protein with NaOH releases S$^{2-}$ from cysteine/methionine. Pb(CH$_3$COO)$_2$ + S$^{2-}$ $\to$ PbS (black precipitate). Confirms presence of sulfur in the protein.', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Amino acids in solution at pH below their pI will', 'Be uncharged', 'Exist predominantly as cations (NH$_3^+$-CHR-COOH) and migrate towards the cathode', 'Exist as anions', 'Not dissolve', 'option2', 'At pH $<$ pI: excess H$^+$ protonates the COO$^-$ group. Net positive charge $\to$ migrates to cathode (negative electrode) in electrophoresis.', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sanger''s reagent (1-fluoro-2,4-dinitrobenzene, FDNB) is used to identify', 'Disulfide bonds', 'The C-terminal amino acid', 'The N-terminal amino acid of a polypeptide', 'The molecular weight', 'option3', 'FDNB reacts with the free $-$NH$_2$ at the N-terminus $\to$ DNP-amino acid (yellow). After hydrolysis, the DNP-labelled amino acid is identified by chromatography.', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Edman degradation sequentially removes amino acids from', 'Random positions', 'The C-terminus', 'The middle of the chain', 'The N-terminus using phenyl isothiocyanate (PITC), one residue at a time', 'option4', 'PITC reacts with N-terminal $-$NH$_2$ $\to$ phenylthiocarbamoyl derivative $\to$ mild acid cleaves the first residue as a PTH-amino acid. Repeat to read the sequence.', 'c_proteins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_vitamins (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Classification and functions
-- ============================================================
('Vitamins are classified into two groups based on their solubility:', 'Fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) and water-soluble (B-complex, C)', 'Organic and inorganic', 'Acidic and basic', 'Metallic and non-metallic', 'option1', 'Fat-soluble vitamins dissolve in fats/oils and are stored in the body (liver, adipose tissue). Water-soluble vitamins dissolve in water and are excreted if in excess.', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin C is chemically known as', 'Retinol', 'Ascorbic acid', 'Calciferol', 'Tocopherol', 'option2', 'Ascorbic acid (C$_6$H$_8$O$_6$) is a water-soluble antioxidant. Found in citrus fruits. Deficiency causes scurvy (bleeding gums, poor wound healing).', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Deficiency of vitamin D causes', 'Scurvy', 'Rickets in children (soft, deformed bones)', 'Night blindness', 'Beriberi', 'option2', 'Vitamin D (calciferol) regulates calcium and phosphorus absorption. Deficiency $\to$ poor bone mineralization $\to$ rickets (children) or osteomalacia (adults). Synthesized in skin by UV light.', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Night blindness is caused by deficiency of', 'Vitamin K', 'Vitamin B$_1$', 'Vitamin C', 'Vitamin A (retinol)', 'option4', 'Vitamin A is needed for synthesis of rhodopsin (visual pigment in rod cells). Deficiency $\to$ night blindness (nyctalopia). Severe deficiency $\to$ xerophthalmia (dry eyes).', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin B$_1$ (thiamine) deficiency causes', 'Pellagra', 'Scurvy', 'Rickets', 'Beriberi (nerve and heart damage)', 'option4', 'Thiamine is a coenzyme in carbohydrate metabolism (pyruvate dehydrogenase). Deficiency $\to$ beriberi: peripheral neuropathy, muscle wasting, cardiac failure.', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin K is essential for', 'Blood clotting (synthesis of prothrombin)', 'Vision', 'Bone growth', 'Energy metabolism', 'option1', 'Vitamin K (phylloquinone) is a cofactor for carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X). Deficiency $\to$ excessive bleeding.', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin E (tocopherol) functions primarily as', 'An antioxidant protecting cell membranes from oxidative damage', 'A coenzyme in glycolysis', 'A hormone', 'A structural protein', 'option1', 'Tocopherol scavenges free radicals, preventing lipid peroxidation of cell membranes. Deficiency is rare but can cause hemolytic anemia and neurological problems.', 'c_vitamins', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia) is caused by deficiency of', 'Vitamin C', 'Vitamin A', 'Vitamin B$_3$ (niacin/nicotinic acid)', 'Vitamin D', 'option3', 'Niacin is a precursor of NAD$^+$ and NADP$^+$ (coenzymes in redox reactions). Deficiency $\to$ pellagra (3 D''s: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia). Found in meat, legumes.', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin B$_{12}$ (cyanocobalamin) is unique among vitamins because', 'It is fat-soluble', 'It contains a metal ion (cobalt) in its structure', 'It has no biological function', 'It is a carbohydrate', 'option2', 'B$_{12}$ has a corrin ring with Co$^{3+}$ at the centre. Required for red blood cell formation and neurological function. Deficiency $\to$ pernicious anemia.', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin B$_2$ (riboflavin) is a component of', 'FAD and FMN (coenzymes in oxidation-reduction reactions)', 'NAD$^+$', 'Coenzyme A', 'ATP', 'option2', 'Riboflavin $\to$ FMN (flavin mononucleotide) and FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide). These are prosthetic groups of flavoproteins involved in electron transport.', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin B$_6$ (pyridoxine) is important as a coenzyme in', 'Fat digestion', 'Amino acid metabolism (transamination, decarboxylation)', 'Blood clotting', 'Calcium absorption', 'option2', 'Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) is the active form. Coenzyme for transaminases and amino acid decarboxylases. Deficiency $\to$ anemia, dermatitis, convulsions.', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Biotin (vitamin B$_7$) serves as a coenzyme for', 'Dehydrogenation reactions', 'Carboxylation reactions (e.g., pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase)', 'Hydrolysis reactions', 'Isomerization reactions', 'option2', 'Biotin is a CO$_2$ carrier. Covalently attached to carboxylase enzymes. Deficiency is rare (produced by gut bacteria) but can be caused by eating raw egg whites (avidin binds biotin).', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Folic acid (vitamin B$_9$) is essential for', 'Antioxidant protection', 'Vision', 'Blood clotting', 'DNA synthesis and cell division (one-carbon transfer reactions)', 'option4', 'Tetrahydrofolate (THF) carries one-carbon units needed for purine and thymidylate synthesis. Deficiency $\to$ megaloblastic anemia. Critical during pregnancy (prevents neural tube defects).', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Excess intake of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) can be harmful because', 'They are converted to water-soluble forms', 'They are excreted immediately', 'They have no biological effect', 'They accumulate in body fat and liver, leading to toxicity (hypervitaminosis)', 'option4', 'Unlike water-soluble vitamins (excreted in urine), fat-soluble vitamins are stored. Excess vitamin A $\to$ liver damage, headache. Excess vitamin D $\to$ hypercalcemia.', 'c_vitamins', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin A (retinol) is derived from', 'Glucose', 'Cholesterol', '$\beta$-Carotene (provitamin A), which is cleaved in the intestine to yield two molecules of retinal', 'Amino acids', 'option3', '$\beta$-Carotene (orange pigment in carrots) is a dimer of retinal. Enzyme $\beta$-carotene 15,15''-dioxygenase cleaves it. Retinal $\to$ retinol (vitamin A) $\to$ retinoic acid.', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin D$_3$ (cholecalciferol) is synthesized in the skin from', '7-Dehydrocholesterol upon exposure to UV-B radiation', 'Glucose', 'Amino acids', 'Fatty acids', 'option1', 'UV-B (290-315 nm) photolytically opens the B-ring of 7-dehydrocholesterol $\to$ previtamin D$_3$ $\to$ vitamin D$_3$. Then hydroxylated in liver (25-OH) and kidney (1,25-(OH)$_2$D$_3$, active form).', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Scurvy due to vitamin C deficiency is characterized by defective collagen synthesis because', 'Vitamin C breaks down collagen', 'Vitamin C is a structural part of collagen', 'Ascorbic acid is required as a cofactor for prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase, which hydroxylate proline and lysine in collagen', 'Collagen does not require any cofactors', 'option3', 'Hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine are essential for collagen triple helix stability. Without vitamin C, these hydroxylation reactions fail $\to$ weak collagen $\to$ bleeding gums, poor wound healing.', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Vitamin K acts as a cofactor for the enzyme that catalyzes', '$\gamma$-Carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors, enabling them to bind Ca$^{2+}$', 'Phosphorylation of serine', 'Methylation of DNA', 'Acetylation of histones', 'option1', 'Vitamin K-dependent carboxylase converts Glu $\to$ $\gamma$-carboxyglutamate (Gla). Gla residues chelate Ca$^{2+}$, which is essential for clotting factor binding to phospholipid membranes.', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is associated with severe deficiency of', 'Vitamin D', 'Vitamin C', 'Vitamin B$_1$ (thiamine), commonly seen in chronic alcoholism', 'Vitamin A', 'option3', 'Alcohol impairs thiamine absorption and utilization. Wernicke''s encephalopathy: confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia. Korsakoff''s psychosis: memory loss, confabulation. Medical emergency.', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Niacin (vitamin B$_3$) can be synthesized in the body from', 'Cholesterol', 'Glucose', 'Fatty acids', 'Tryptophan (an essential amino acid), though inefficiently (60 mg tryptophan $\approx$ 1 mg niacin)', 'option4', 'Tryptophan $\to$ kynurenine pathway $\to$ NAD$^+$. Requires vitamins B$_2$, B$_6$, and iron. Corn-based diets (low tryptophan) $\to$ pellagra in populations without niacin supplementation.', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The antioxidant mechanism of vitamin E involves', 'Chelating metal ions', 'Donating a hydrogen atom from its phenolic $-$OH to lipid peroxyl radicals, terminating the chain reaction', 'Absorbing UV light', 'Enzymatic catalysis', 'option2', '$\alpha$-Tocopherol + LOO$\cdot$ $\to$ $\alpha$-tocopheroxyl radical + LOOH. The tocopheroxyl radical is stable (resonance-stabilized). Vitamin C regenerates vitamin E.', 'c_vitamins', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_nucleic_acids (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- DNA and RNA constitution, biological functions, hormones
-- ============================================================
('The monomeric units of nucleic acids are called', 'Nucleotides', 'Amino acids', 'Monosaccharides', 'Fatty acids', 'option1', 'A nucleotide = nitrogenous base + pentose sugar + phosphate group. Nucleotides polymerize via phosphodiester bonds to form nucleic acids (DNA and RNA).', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('DNA contains the sugar', 'Deoxyribose (2-deoxyribose)', 'Ribose', 'Glucose', 'Fructose', 'option1', 'Deoxyribose lacks the $-$OH at C-2'' (has $-$H instead). This makes DNA more chemically stable than RNA. RNA contains ribose (with $-$OH at C-2'').', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The four nitrogenous bases in DNA are', 'Adenine, guanine, cytosine, and thymine', 'Adenine, guanine, cytosine, and uracil', 'Only purines', 'Only pyrimidines', 'option1', 'Purines: adenine (A) and guanine (G) — two-ring structures. Pyrimidines: cytosine (C) and thymine (T) — single-ring structures. In RNA, uracil (U) replaces thymine.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Watson-Crick model of DNA, the two strands are held together by', 'Van der Waals forces only', 'Covalent bonds between bases', 'Ionic bonds', 'Hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs (A=T and G≡C)', 'option4', 'A pairs with T via 2 H-bonds. G pairs with C via 3 H-bonds. The strands are antiparallel (5''$\to$3'' and 3''$\to$5'').', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('RNA differs from DNA in that RNA contains', 'No nitrogenous bases', 'Deoxyribose and thymine', 'No phosphate groups', 'Ribose sugar and uracil instead of deoxyribose and thymine', 'option4', 'RNA: ribose + A, G, C, U. DNA: deoxyribose + A, G, C, T. RNA is usually single-stranded. DNA is usually double-stranded.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The three main types of RNA are', 'DNA polymerase, RNA polymerase, and ligase', 'Only mRNA', 'Only tRNA', 'mRNA (messenger), tRNA (transfer), and rRNA (ribosomal)', 'option4', 'mRNA carries genetic information from DNA to ribosomes. tRNA brings amino acids to the ribosome. rRNA is a structural and catalytic component of ribosomes.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The biological function of DNA is', 'Energy production', 'Storage and transmission of genetic information', 'Structural support only', 'Catalysis of reactions', 'option2', 'DNA stores the genetic blueprint in the sequence of bases. It replicates during cell division and is transcribed into RNA for protein synthesis.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Chargaff''s rules state that in DNA', 'The amount of adenine equals thymine, and guanine equals cytosine (A=T, G=C)', 'A=G and C=T', 'All four bases are present in equal amounts', 'There are no rules for base composition', 'option1', 'Chargaff (1950): %A = %T and %G = %C in any DNA. This supported the Watson-Crick base-pairing model. The ratio A+T/G+C varies between species.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A nucleoside differs from a nucleotide in that a nucleoside lacks', 'The base', 'The sugar', 'The phosphate group (nucleoside = base + sugar only)', 'Both sugar and phosphate', 'option3', 'Nucleoside: base + sugar (e.g., adenosine = adenine + ribose). Nucleotide: base + sugar + phosphate (e.g., AMP = adenosine monophosphate). Nucleotide = nucleoside + phosphate.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The phosphodiester bond in nucleic acids links', 'Two bases directly', 'The 3''-OH of one nucleotide to the 5''-phosphate of the next', 'Two sugars without phosphate', 'The 1'' carbon to the phosphate', 'option2', 'The backbone of DNA/RNA: $\cdots$sugar-phosphate-sugar-phosphate$\cdots$. The bond is between 3''-OH and 5''-phosphate via a phosphodiester linkage. Bases project inward.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The central dogma of molecular biology describes the flow of genetic information as', 'DNA $\to$ RNA $\to$ Protein (replication, transcription, translation)', 'Protein $\to$ RNA $\to$ DNA', 'RNA $\to$ DNA $\to$ Protein', 'Protein $\to$ DNA $\to$ RNA', 'option1', 'DNA replicates itself. DNA is transcribed to mRNA. mRNA is translated to protein at ribosomes. Exceptions: reverse transcription (RNA $\to$ DNA by retroviruses).', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hormones are chemical messengers. Insulin is a', 'Steroid hormone', 'Peptide hormone secreted by the $\beta$-cells of the pancreas that lowers blood glucose', 'Amino acid derivative', 'Fatty acid derivative', 'option2', 'Insulin: 51 amino acids, two polypeptide chains (A and B) linked by disulfide bonds. Promotes glucose uptake by cells. Deficiency $\to$ diabetes mellitus.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Steroid hormones such as testosterone and estrogen are derived from', 'Cholesterol', 'Amino acids', 'Nucleotides', 'Carbohydrates', 'option1', 'Cholesterol $\to$ pregnenolone $\to$ various steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, testosterone, estradiol, progesterone). They are lipid-soluble and cross cell membranes.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Adrenaline (epinephrine) is a hormone derived from', 'Nucleotides', 'Cholesterol', 'Glucose', 'The amino acid tyrosine', 'option4', 'Tyrosine $\to$ DOPA $\to$ dopamine $\to$ noradrenaline $\to$ adrenaline. Secreted by adrenal medulla. "Fight or flight" response: increases heart rate, blood pressure, glucose release.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('DNA with a higher G-C content has a higher melting temperature ($T_m$) because', 'G-C base pairs have 3 hydrogen bonds compared to 2 in A-T pairs, requiring more energy to separate', 'G-C pairs are lighter', 'A-T pairs are stronger', 'G-C content does not affect $T_m$', 'option1', 'More H-bonds per G-C pair $\to$ stronger inter-strand interaction $\to$ higher $T_m$. $T_m$ increases linearly with %G+C. Used to characterize DNA from different organisms.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a double-stranded DNA molecule with 1000 base pairs, if 200 are A-T pairs, the number of hydrogen bonds is', '2600', '2400', '3000', '2800', 'option4', 'A-T pairs: 200 $\times$ 2 = 400 H-bonds. G-C pairs: (1000 $-$ 200) = 800 $\times$ 3 = 2400 H-bonds. Total = 400 + 2400 = 2800 H-bonds.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If one strand of DNA has the sequence 5''-ATGCCTA-3'', the complementary strand in the 3''$\to$5'' direction is', '5''-TACGGAT-3''', '3''-ATGCCTA-5''', '3''-TACGGAT-5''', '3''-UACGGAU-5''', 'option3', 'Complementary base pairing: A$\leftrightarrow$T, G$\leftrightarrow$C. Reading 5''$\to$3'': ATGCCTA. Complement (3''$\to$5''): TACGGAT. Strands are antiparallel.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The genetic code is degenerate, meaning', 'Each codon codes for multiple amino acids', 'Most amino acids are coded by more than one codon (triplet)', 'The code varies between species', 'Only 20 codons exist', 'option2', '64 codons (4$^3$) code for 20 amino acids + 3 stop codons. Multiple codons per amino acid (e.g., leucine has 6 codons). Degeneracy is mainly at the 3rd position (wobble).', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Thyroid hormones (T$_3$ and T$_4$) are unique because they contain', 'Iodine atoms in their structure', 'Sulfur atoms', 'Iron atoms', 'Zinc atoms', 'option2', 'T$_3$ (triiodothyronine): 3 iodine atoms. T$_4$ (thyroxine): 4 iodine atoms. Derived from tyrosine. Regulate basal metabolic rate. Deficiency $\to$ hypothyroidism, goiter.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a nucleotide that functions as', 'A vitamin', 'A structural component of membranes', 'The primary energy currency of the cell', 'A hormone', 'option3', 'ATP = adenine + ribose + 3 phosphate groups. Hydrolysis of the terminal phosphoanhydride bond releases $\sim 30.5$ kJ/mol. ATP $\to$ ADP + P$_i$ drives endergonic reactions.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Reverse transcriptase is an enzyme found in retroviruses that', 'Degrades proteins', 'Synthesizes RNA from DNA', 'Synthesizes DNA from an RNA template (RNA $\to$ DNA)', 'Replicates DNA', 'option3', 'Retroviruses (e.g., HIV) carry RNA genomes. Reverse transcriptase converts viral RNA $\to$ cDNA $\to$ dsDNA, which integrates into the host genome. Violates the simple central dogma.', 'c_nucleic_acids', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_prac_detection (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Detection of extra elements (N, S, halogens) and functional groups
-- ============================================================
('In the Lassaigne''s test, the organic compound is fused with', 'Copper metal', 'Sodium metal to convert organic elements (N, S, halogens) into ionic form', 'Silver metal', 'Zinc metal', 'option2', 'Organic compound + Na $\xrightarrow{\text{fusion}}$ NaCN (if N present), Na$_2$S (if S present), NaX (if halogen present). The sodium extract is then tested.', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nitrogen in an organic compound is detected by the Lassaigne''s test by forming', 'A red colour', 'A white precipitate', 'Prussian blue colour (sodium cyanide reacts with FeSO$_4$ and FeCl$_3$)', 'Effervescence', 'option3', 'Na + C + N $\to$ NaCN. NaCN + FeSO$_4$ $\to$ Na$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$]. Then + FeCl$_3$ $\to$ Fe$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$]$_3$ (Prussian blue).', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sulfur in an organic compound is detected by the Lassaigne''s test by', 'Adding BaCl$_2$', 'Adding sodium nitroprusside to the sodium extract — a violet colour indicates S', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'Heating with conc. H$_2$SO$_4$', 'option2', 'Na + S $\to$ Na$_2$S. Na$_2$S + Na$_2$[Fe(CN)$_5$NO] $\to$ Na$_4$[Fe(CN)$_5$NOS] (violet/purple colour). Alternatively, lead acetate test gives black PbS.', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Halogens in an organic compound are detected by the Lassaigne''s test by', 'Adding NaOH', 'Adding AgNO$_3$ to the sodium extract acidified with HNO$_3$ — a precipitate of AgX forms', 'Adding FeCl$_3$', 'Heating with Na$_2$CO$_3$', 'option2', 'Na + X $\to$ NaX. NaX + AgNO$_3$ $\to$ AgX$\downarrow$ + NaNO$_3$. AgCl (white), AgBr (pale yellow), AgI (yellow). Confirm by solubility in NH$_3$.', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The functional group test for an aldehyde is', 'Lucas test', 'Ferric chloride test', 'Hinsberg test', 'Tollens'' test (silver mirror) or Fehling''s test (red precipitate of Cu$_2$O)', 'option4', 'Aldehydes reduce Tollens'' reagent (Ag$^+$ $\to$ Ag mirror) and Fehling''s reagent (Cu$^{2+}$ $\to$ Cu$_2$O red ppt). Ketones do not give these tests (except $\alpha$-hydroxy ketones).', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ferric chloride test gives a violet/green colour with', 'Aldehydes', 'Phenols (formation of coloured iron-phenol complex)', 'Alkanes', 'Amines', 'option2', 'Phenol + FeCl$_3$ $\to$ [Fe(OC$_6$H$_5$)$_6$]$^{3-}$ (violet complex). Enols also give positive test. Used to distinguish phenols from alcohols.', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The 2,4-DNP test (Brady''s test) gives an orange/yellow precipitate with', 'Aldehydes and ketones (formation of 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone)', 'Alcohols', 'Amines', 'Carboxylic acids', 'option1', 'RCHO or RCOR'' + 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine $\to$ 2,4-DNP derivative (orange-yellow precipitate). Confirms presence of carbonyl group (C=O).', 'c_prac_detection', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When both N and S are present in an organic compound, the Lassaigne''s extract may contain NaSCN instead of NaCN and Na$_2$S. To test for N in this case', 'Boil the extract with conc. HCl to decompose NaSCN, then test for N with FeSO$_4$/FeCl$_3$', 'Add AgNO$_3$ directly', 'Add lead acetate', 'No modification is needed', 'option1', 'NaSCN + FeCl$_3$ $\to$ Fe(SCN)$_3$ (blood red, not Prussian blue). To avoid this: boil with HCl to destroy SCN$^-$, then proceed with the Prussian blue test for CN$^-$.', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To distinguish between a primary, secondary, and tertiary alcohol, the Lucas test uses', 'FeCl$_3$', 'Anhydrous ZnCl$_2$/conc. HCl — 3° reacts immediately (turbidity), 2° reacts in 5-10 min, 1° does not react at room temperature', 'Tollens'' reagent', 'NaHCO$_3$', 'option2', 'ROH + HCl $\xrightarrow{\text{ZnCl}_2}$ RCl + H$_2$O. 3° carbocation forms fastest (most stable). Turbidity = formation of insoluble alkyl chloride.', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The iodoform test is positive for', 'All alcohols', 'All aldehydes', 'Compounds with CH$_3$CO$-$ or CH$_3$CH(OH)$-$ groups (yellow precipitate of CHI$_3$)', 'All carboxylic acids', 'option3', 'CH$_3$COR + 3I$_2$ + 4NaOH $\to$ CHI$_3\downarrow$ (yellow) + RCOONa. Positive for acetaldehyde, methyl ketones, ethanol, and secondary alcohols with CH$_3$CH(OH)$-$.', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Beilstein test detects', 'Phosphorus', 'Nitrogen', 'Sulfur', 'Halogens in organic compounds (green flame when Cu wire coated with compound is heated)', 'option4', 'Cu wire + organic halide $\xrightarrow{\Delta}$ volatile CuX$_2$ $\to$ green/blue-green flame. Quick qualitative test but not specific (some N-compounds also give green flame).', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To distinguish between formic acid and acetic acid, the best test is', 'Ferric chloride test', 'Litmus test', 'NaHCO$_3$ test', 'Tollens'' test — formic acid gives a silver mirror (it has a reducible $-$CHO-like group) while acetic acid does not', 'option4', 'HCOOH has the structure HCO-OH (aldehyde + acid). It reduces Tollens'' and Fehling''s reagents. CH$_3$COOH cannot reduce these reagents.', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Schiff''s test distinguishes aldehydes from ketones because', 'Both give the same result', 'Ketones give a pink colour', 'Aldehydes restore the pink colour of Schiff''s reagent (decolorized fuchsin) while ketones do not', 'Neither reacts', 'option3', 'Schiff''s reagent (fuchsin + SO$_2$): colourless. Aldehydes restore the magenta/pink colour. Ketones do not react (or react very slowly). Exception: acetone may give a faint colour.', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The hydroxamic acid test is used to detect', 'Ethers', 'Amines', 'Alkenes', 'Esters (RCOOR'' + NH$_2$OH $\to$ RCONHOH, which gives a violet colour with FeCl$_3$)', 'option4', 'Ester + hydroxylamine (NH$_2$OH) in alkaline medium $\to$ hydroxamic acid (RCONHOH). FeCl$_3$ gives a violet/burgundy complex. Confirms ester functional group.', 'c_prac_detection', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the Lassaigne''s test, if both N and a halogen are present, the extract is first boiled with', 'Conc. HNO$_3$ to decompose NaCN (which would otherwise precipitate AgCN with AgNO$_3$, interfering with halogen detection)', 'NaOH', 'H$_2$SO$_4$', 'KMnO$_4$', 'option1', 'NaCN + AgNO$_3$ $\to$ AgCN (white ppt, interferes). Boiling with HNO$_3$ decomposes NaCN: NaCN + HNO$_3$ $\to$ NaNO$_3$ + HCN$\uparrow$. Then AgNO$_3$ detects only halide.', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Liebermann''s nitroso reaction is used to detect', 'Phenols — a blue/green colour that changes to red on dilution with water', 'Aldehydes', 'Amines', 'Carboxylic acids', 'option2', 'Phenol + NaNO$_2$ + conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ $\to$ p-nitrosophenol (green). On adding NaOH $\to$ blue/green (indophenol). On dilution with water $\to$ red. Characteristic colour changes.', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To detect a primary aromatic amine, the most reliable test is', 'Iodoform test', 'Tollens'' test', 'Lucas test', 'Diazotization followed by coupling with $\beta$-naphthol to give a bright orange-red azo dye', 'option4', 'ArNH$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{NaNO}_2/\text{HCl, 0-5°C}}$ ArN$_2^+$Cl$^-$ $\xrightarrow{\beta\text{-naphthol/NaOH}}$ orange-red azo dye. Specific for 1° aromatic amines.', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The sodium bicarbonate test distinguishes carboxylic acids from phenols because', 'Neither reacts', 'Phenols react with NaHCO$_3$', 'Both react equally', 'Carboxylic acids react with NaHCO$_3$ (effervescence of CO$_2$) while phenols do not (phenols are too weakly acidic)', 'option4', 'RCOOH ($pK_a \approx 4$-$5$) is strong enough to decompose NaHCO$_3$. Phenol ($pK_a \approx 10$) is too weak. RCOOH + NaHCO$_3$ $\to$ RCOONa + CO$_2\uparrow$ + H$_2$O.', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Victor Meyer test distinguishes 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols by converting them to nitroalkanes. The colour observed for a primary alcohol is', 'Blue', 'Blood red (with HNO$_2$ and NaOH, the nitronic acid gives a red colour with FeCl$_3$ or turns red with NaOH)', 'No colour (colourless)', 'Green', 'option2', '1° ROH $\to$ RX $\to$ RNO$_2$ $\xrightarrow{\text{HNO}_2}$ nitrolic acid $\xrightarrow{\text{NaOH}}$ blood red. 2° $\to$ pseudonitrole $\to$ blue. 3° $\to$ no reaction (colourless).', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ceric ammonium nitrate test gives a red colour with', 'Ethers', 'Alkanes', 'Alcohols (formation of a red complex with Ce$^{4+}$)', 'Amines', 'option3', 'ROH + (NH$_4$)$_2$Ce(NO$_3$)$_6$ $\to$ red complex [Ce(OR)(NO$_3$)$_5$]$^{2-}$. Ce$^{4+}$ (yellow) $\to$ Ce$^{4+}$-alkoxide complex (red). Confirms $-$OH group in alcohols.', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Molisch test is a general test for', 'Carbohydrates — a purple ring at the junction of two layers when $\alpha$-naphthol and conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ are used', 'Proteins', 'Lipids', 'Nucleic acids', 'option1', 'Conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ dehydrates carbohydrate $\to$ furfural (pentoses) or hydroxymethylfurfural (hexoses). These condense with $\alpha$-naphthol $\to$ purple/violet ring.', 'c_prac_detection', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_prac_preparation (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Preparation of inorganic/organic compounds
-- ============================================================
('Mohr''s salt (ferrous ammonium sulfate) is prepared by', 'Heating iron with ammonia', 'Dissolving equimolar FeSO$_4$ and (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$ in water acidified with H$_2$SO$_4$ and crystallizing', 'Mixing FeCl$_3$ with NaCl', 'Electrolysis of iron', 'option2', 'FeSO$_4$ + (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$ + 6H$_2$O $\to$ FeSO$_4$·(NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$·6H$_2$O. Double salt. H$_2$SO$_4$ prevents hydrolysis of Fe$^{2+}$.', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Potash alum is prepared by crystallizing a solution containing', 'KCl and AlCl$_3$', 'K$_2$SO$_4$ and Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ in equimolar ratio', 'KNO$_3$ and Al(NO$_3$)$_3$', 'KOH and Al(OH)$_3$', 'option2', 'K$_2$SO$_4$·Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$·24H$_2$O (potash alum). Dissolve both salts in water, concentrate, and crystallize. Octahedral crystals.', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acetanilide is prepared by', 'Oxidation of toluene', 'Reduction of nitrobenzene', 'Acetylation of aniline with acetic anhydride or glacial acetic acid', 'Hydrolysis of benzamide', 'option3', 'C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$ + (CH$_3$CO)$_2$O $\to$ C$_6$H$_5$NHCOCH$_3$ + CH$_3$COOH. The reaction is carried out by refluxing. Product is purified by recrystallization from water.', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is prepared by', 'Acetylation of salicylic acid with acetic anhydride in the presence of conc. H$_2$SO$_4$', 'Reduction of benzoic acid', 'Oxidation of phenol', 'Hydrolysis of acetanilide', 'option1', 'Salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid) + (CH$_3$CO)$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4}$ aspirin + CH$_3$COOH. The phenolic $-$OH is acetylated.', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('p-Nitroacetanilide is prepared by', 'Reduction of p-nitroaniline', 'Nitration of acetanilide with a mixture of conc. HNO$_3$ and conc. H$_2$SO$_4$', 'Oxidation of acetanilide', 'Bromination of acetanilide', 'option2', 'Acetanilide + HNO$_3$/H$_2$SO$_4$ $\to$ p-nitroacetanilide (major) + o-nitroacetanilide (minor). The $-$NHCOCH$_3$ group directs to ortho/para. Para is major due to steric reasons.', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dibenzalacetone is prepared by', 'Cannizzaro reaction', 'Friedel-Crafts acylation', 'Grignard reaction', 'Aldol condensation of benzaldehyde with acetone in the presence of NaOH', 'option4', '2C$_6$H$_5$CHO + CH$_3$COCH$_3$ $\xrightarrow{\text{NaOH}}$ C$_6$H$_5$CH=CHCOCH=CHC$_6$H$_5$ + 2H$_2$O. Crossed aldol followed by dehydration.', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ferrous sulfate crystals (FeSO$_4$·7H$_2$O) are prepared by', 'Mixing Fe$_2$O$_3$ with NaOH', 'Heating iron in air', 'Dissolving iron filings/nails in dilute H$_2$SO$_4$ and crystallizing', 'Electrolysis of FeCl$_3$', 'option3', 'Fe + H$_2$SO$_4$ (dil.) $\to$ FeSO$_4$ + H$_2\uparrow$. Filter, concentrate, and cool to get green crystals of FeSO$_4$·7H$_2$O (green vitriol).', 'c_prac_preparation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('During the preparation of acetanilide, the reaction mixture is poured into cold water because', 'It dissolves better in cold water', 'Acetanilide is insoluble in cold water and precipitates out, allowing easy separation', 'Cold water acts as a catalyst', 'It prevents the formation of acetanilide', 'option2', 'Acetanilide is soluble in hot water but insoluble in cold water. Pouring into ice-cold water precipitates the product. Filtered and recrystallized for purity.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the preparation of Mohr''s salt, dilute H$_2$SO$_4$ is added to prevent', 'Hydrolysis of Fe$^{2+}$ ions (which would form Fe(OH)$_2$ precipitate)', 'Oxidation of Fe$^{2+}$ to Fe$^{3+}$', 'Decomposition of (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$', 'Evaporation of water', 'option1', 'Fe$^{2+}$ + 2H$_2$O $\rightleftharpoons$ Fe(OH)$_2$ + 2H$^+$. Adding H$_2$SO$_4$ suppresses hydrolysis by Le Chatelier''s principle. Also minimizes aerial oxidation.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The preparation of iodoform from ethanol involves', 'Treating ethanol with I$_2$ and NaOH (or NaOI) — the haloform reaction', 'Treating ethanol with HI', 'Oxidation with KMnO$_4$', 'Reduction with LiAlH$_4$', 'option1', 'CH$_3$CH$_2$OH $\xrightarrow{\text{I}_2/\text{NaOH}}$ CHI$_3$ (yellow precipitate) + HCOONa. Ethanol is first oxidized to acetaldehyde, then the methyl group is iodinated and cleaved.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Aniline is prepared in the laboratory by', 'Hydrolysis of chlorobenzene', 'Oxidation of benzene', 'Friedel-Crafts alkylation', 'Reduction of nitrobenzene with Sn/HCl followed by basification with NaOH', 'option4', 'C$_6$H$_5$NO$_2$ + 3Sn + 7HCl $\to$ C$_6$H$_5$NH$_3^+$Cl$^-$ + 3SnCl$_2$ + 2H$_2$O. Then NaOH liberates free aniline. Purified by steam distillation.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the preparation of potash alum, the solution is concentrated by', 'Evaporation on a water bath until a crystallization point is reached, then cooled slowly', 'Boiling vigorously', 'Adding a desiccant', 'Freeze-drying', 'option1', 'Slow evaporation and cooling gives well-formed octahedral crystals. Rapid cooling gives small, impure crystals. The crystallization point is tested by placing a drop on a watch glass.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Benzanilide is prepared by the reaction of', 'Aniline with acetic acid', 'Aniline with benzoyl chloride in the presence of NaOH (Schotten-Baumann reaction)', 'Benzene with aniline', 'Phenol with benzoic acid', 'option2', 'C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$ + C$_6$H$_5$COCl $\xrightarrow{\text{NaOH}}$ C$_6$H$_5$NHCOC$_6$H$_5$ + NaCl + H$_2$O. NaOH neutralizes HCl formed, driving the reaction forward.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('p-Nitroaniline is obtained from p-nitroacetanilide by', 'Bromination', 'Reduction with Sn/HCl', 'Oxidation with KMnO$_4$', 'Acid hydrolysis (refluxing with dilute HCl or H$_2$SO$_4$) to remove the acetyl group', 'option4', 'p-O$_2$NC$_6$H$_4$NHCOCH$_3$ + H$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{H}^+/\Delta}$ p-O$_2$NC$_6$H$_4$NH$_2$ + CH$_3$COOH. Hydrolysis of the amide bond regenerates the free amine.', 'c_prac_preparation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the preparation of aspirin, the purity of the product is tested by', 'Iodoform test', 'Tollens'' test', 'FeCl$_3$ test — pure aspirin gives no colour (no free phenolic $-$OH), while impure aspirin containing salicylic acid gives a violet colour', 'Biuret test', 'option3', 'Salicylic acid has a free $-$OH (gives violet with FeCl$_3$). In aspirin, the $-$OH is acetylated (no free phenol). Any violet colour indicates unreacted salicylic acid impurity.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The yield of acetanilide can be improved by', 'Using excess acetic anhydride and maintaining proper temperature control during reflux', 'Using excess water', 'Heating at very high temperature', 'Adding a strong acid catalyst', 'option1', 'Excess acetic anhydride drives the equilibrium forward. Reflux ensures complete reaction without loss of volatile reagents. Temperature control prevents side reactions.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('During the preparation of aniline, steam distillation is used because', 'Aniline is soluble in water', 'Aniline is steam-volatile, immiscible with water, and would decompose at its normal boiling point (184°C) if distilled directly with impurities', 'Steam acts as a reducing agent', 'It increases the yield of nitrobenzene', 'option2', 'Steam distillation allows aniline to distil below 100°C. The distillate separates into two layers (aniline + water). Aniline is extracted with a separating funnel.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the preparation of Mohr''s salt, if the solution turns yellow/brown, it indicates', 'Oxidation of Fe$^{2+}$ to Fe$^{3+}$ — the preparation must be restarted with fresh reagents and more acid', 'Successful crystallization', 'Presence of (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$', 'Correct pH', 'option1', 'Fe$^{2+}$ (green) $\to$ Fe$^{3+}$ (yellow/brown) by aerial oxidation. Mohr''s salt must contain Fe$^{2+}$. Prevention: use excess H$_2$SO$_4$, minimize exposure to air, work quickly.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Schotten-Baumann reaction for preparing amides uses aqueous NaOH because', 'NaOH acts as a catalyst for reduction', 'NaOH is the acylating agent', 'NaOH dissolves the product', 'NaOH neutralizes the HCl produced, preventing it from reacting with the amine and shifting equilibrium towards product', 'option4', 'RNH$_2$ + R''COCl $\to$ RNHCOR'' + HCl. Without NaOH, HCl would protonate the amine (RNH$_3^+$Cl$^-$), making it unreactive. NaOH removes HCl, driving the reaction forward.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('To prepare a pure sample of benzoic acid from benzamide, the method used is', 'Acid hydrolysis of benzamide (reflux with dil. HCl or H$_2$SO$_4$), then crystallization', 'Oxidation with KMnO$_4$', 'Reduction with NaBH$_4$', 'Friedel-Crafts reaction', 'option1', 'C$_6$H$_5$CONH$_2$ + H$_2$O $\xrightarrow{\text{H}^+/\Delta}$ C$_6$H$_5$COOH + NH$_4^+$. Benzoic acid precipitates on cooling (low solubility in cold water). Filter and recrystallize.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The recrystallization solvent for acetanilide is chosen as water because', 'Water reacts with acetanilide', 'Acetanilide is insoluble in all solvents', 'Acetanilide is highly soluble in hot water but sparingly soluble in cold water, giving good crystal recovery', 'Ethanol is a better solvent', 'option3', 'Ideal recrystallization solvent: compound is very soluble when hot, sparingly soluble when cold, and impurities remain in solution. Water fits these criteria for acetanilide.', 'c_prac_preparation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_prac_titrations (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Titrimetric exercises: acids, bases, indicators, KMnO4 titrations
-- ============================================================
('In an acid-base titration, the equivalence point is the point where', 'The indicator changes colour', 'Moles of acid equal moles of base (stoichiometrically)', 'The solution turns colourless', 'pH equals 7 always', 'option2', 'Equivalence point: stoichiometric completion. End point: indicator colour change (ideally close to equivalence point).', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Phenolphthalein is used as an indicator for titrations of', 'Strong acid vs strong base or weak acid vs strong base (pH range 8.2-10)', 'Strong acid vs weak base', 'Any titration', 'Redox titrations', 'option1', 'Phenolphthalein: colourless (acidic) $\to$ pink (basic). Transition range pH 8.2-10. Not suitable for weak base titrations (equivalence pH $< 7$).', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Methyl orange is used as an indicator for titrations of', 'Weak acid vs strong base', 'Strong acid vs weak base (pH range 3.1-4.4)', 'Only redox titrations', 'Only precipitation titrations', 'option2', 'Methyl orange: red (acidic, pH $< 3.1$) $\to$ yellow (basic, pH $> 4.4$). Suitable when equivalence pH is acidic.', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a KMnO$_4$ titration in acidic medium, the indicator is', 'Phenolphthalein', 'Methyl orange', 'No external indicator needed (KMnO$_4$ is self-indicating)', 'Starch', 'option3', 'KMnO$_4$ (purple) $\to$ Mn$^{2+}$ (colourless). At the endpoint, the first excess drop of KMnO$_4$ gives a permanent pink colour.', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A standard solution is one whose', 'Concentration is accurately known', 'Colour is known', 'Temperature is fixed', 'Volume is fixed', 'option1', 'Primary standard: can be weighed accurately (e.g., Na$_2$CO$_3$, K$_2$Cr$_2$O$_7$, oxalic acid). Secondary standard: standardized against a primary (e.g., NaOH, HCl, KMnO$_4$).', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The normality equation for a titration is', '$N_1V_1 = N_2V_2$ (at the equivalence point)', '$N_1 + N_2 = V_1 + V_2$', '$N_1/V_1 = N_2/V_2$', '$N_1V_2 = N_2V_1$', 'option1', 'Milliequivalents of acid $=$ milliequivalents of base at equivalence. $N_1V_1 = N_2V_2$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Oxalic acid is used as a primary standard because', 'It is coloured', 'It is a strong acid', 'It is stable, non-hygroscopic, has high molar mass, and can be obtained in high purity', 'It is volatile', 'option3', 'H$_2$C$_2$O$_4 \cdot 2$H$_2$O: $M = 126$. Stable, easily purified by crystallization. Used to standardize KMnO$_4$ and NaOH.', 'c_prac_titrations', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the titration of oxalic acid with KMnO$_4$, the solution must be heated to $\sim 60°\text{C}$ because', 'The indicator requires heat', 'KMnO$_4$ decomposes at room temperature', 'Oxalic acid is insoluble at room temperature', 'The reaction is slow at room temperature and Mn$^{2+}$ (autocatalyst) is needed to speed it up', 'option4', 'Initially slow (no Mn$^{2+}$ catalyst). Heating provides activation energy. Once Mn$^{2+}$ forms, it autocatalyzes and the reaction speeds up.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$25\;\text{mL}$ of $0.1\;\text{N}$ NaOH is required to neutralize $10\;\text{mL}$ of an acid. The normality of the acid is', '$0.5\;\text{N}$', '$0.1\;\text{N}$', '$0.25\;\text{N}$', '$1.0\;\text{N}$', 'option3', '$N_1V_1 = N_2V_2$. $N_{acid} \times 10 = 0.1 \times 25$. $N_{acid} = 2.5/10 = 0.25\;\text{N}$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the titration of Mohr''s salt (FeSO$_4 \cdot$(NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4 \cdot 6$H$_2$O) with KMnO$_4$, the $n$-factor of Mohr''s salt is', '$1$ (Fe$^{2+} \to$ Fe$^{3+}$)', '$2$', '$5$', '$3$', 'option1', 'Fe$^{2+}$ loses 1 electron to become Fe$^{3+}$. $n$-factor $= 1$. Equivalent mass $= M/1 = 392$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $n$-factor of oxalic acid (H$_2$C$_2$O$_4$) in its titration with KMnO$_4$ is', '$1$', '$2$', '$5$', '$10$', 'option2', 'C$_2$O$_4^{2-} \to$ 2CO$_2$. Each C goes from $+3$ to $+4$: 2 electrons lost per molecule. $n = 2$. Equivalent mass $= 126/2 = 63$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a back titration, the excess reagent is titrated with a standard solution. This is used when', 'The analyte reacts instantly', 'The analyte reacts slowly or is insoluble, or the endpoint is hard to detect directly', 'No indicator is available', 'The analyte is a gas', 'option2', 'E.g., to find CaCO$_3$ in impure sample: add excess HCl, then back-titrate unreacted HCl with NaOH.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The endpoint of a starch-iodine titration is indicated by', 'No colour change', 'A pink colour', 'A yellow colour', 'Disappearance of the blue-black colour (when all I$_2$ is consumed)', 'option4', 'Starch + I$_2 \to$ blue-black complex. In iodometric titrations: titrate I$_2$ with Na$_2$S$_2$O$_3$ until blue disappears.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Mohr''s salt is preferred over FeSO$_4$ as a primary standard because', 'It is more stable (the double salt resists aerial oxidation of Fe$^{2+}$ better)', 'It is cheaper', 'It has a lower molar mass', 'It is colourless', 'option1', 'FeSO$_4$ alone oxidizes easily in air (Fe$^{2+} \to$ Fe$^{3+}$). The double salt with (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$ is more resistant to oxidation.', 'c_prac_titrations', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the titration of KMnO$_4$ vs oxalic acid, the molar ratio is', '$1:1$', '$2:5$ (2 mol KMnO$_4$ : 5 mol H$_2$C$_2$O$_4$)', '$5:2$', '$1:5$', 'option2', '2KMnO$_4$ + 5H$_2$C$_2$O$_4$ + 3H$_2$SO$_4 \to$ K$_2$SO$_4$ + 2MnSO$_4$ + 10CO$_2$ + 8H$_2$O. Molar ratio $= 2:5$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$20\;\text{mL}$ of $0.02\;\text{M}$ KMnO$_4$ is required to titrate $25\;\text{mL}$ of Mohr''s salt in acidic medium. The molarity of Mohr''s salt is', '$0.04\;\text{M}$', '$0.02\;\text{M}$', '$0.08\;\text{M}$', '$0.01\;\text{M}$', 'option3', 'Meq KMnO$_4 = 20 \times 0.02 \times 5 = 2$. Meq Mohr''s salt $= 25 \times M \times 1$. $25M = 2$. $M = 0.08\;\text{M}$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In iodometric titrations, the liberated I$_2$ is titrated with', 'Na$_2$S$_2$O$_3$ (sodium thiosulfate) using starch indicator', 'KMnO$_4$', 'NaOH', 'HCl', 'option1', '2Na$_2$S$_2$O$_3$ + I$_2 \to$ Na$_2$S$_4$O$_6$ + 2NaI. Starch indicator: blue $\to$ colourless at endpoint.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The percentage purity of a sample of Na$_2$CO$_3$ can be determined by titrating with standard HCl using', 'Methyl orange indicator (endpoint pH $\approx 4$)', 'Phenolphthalein only', 'No indicator', 'Starch indicator', 'option1', 'Na$_2$CO$_3$ + 2HCl $\to$ 2NaCl + H$_2$O + CO$_2$. Equivalence pH is acidic ($\sim 4$). Methyl orange is appropriate.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A double indicator titration of Na$_2$CO$_3$ + NaOH mixture with HCl uses', 'Only phenolphthalein', 'Phenolphthalein first (to neutralize NaOH + half of Na$_2$CO$_3$), then methyl orange (to complete Na$_2$CO$_3$ neutralization)', 'Only methyl orange', 'No indicator', 'option2', 'Phenolphthalein endpoint: NaOH fully neutralized + Na$_2$CO$_3 \to$ NaHCO$_3$. Methyl orange endpoint: NaHCO$_3 \to$ NaCl + CO$_2$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The equivalent mass of Na$_2$CO$_3$ when titrated with HCl (complete neutralization) is', '$53$ ($M/2 = 106/2$)', '$106$', '$26.5$', '$212$', 'option1', 'Na$_2$CO$_3$ + 2HCl $\to$ 2NaCl + H$_2$O + CO$_2$. $n$-factor $= 2$ (2 replaceable Na$^+$). Eq. mass $= 106/2 = 53$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The strength of H$_2$O$_2$ is often expressed in "volume strength". $10\;\text{V}$ H$_2$O$_2$ means', 'The pH is $10$', '$10\;\text{mL}$ of H$_2$O$_2$ is present', 'The concentration is $10\;\text{M}$', '$1\;\text{mL}$ of the solution gives $10\;\text{mL}$ of O$_2$ at STP on decomposition', 'option4', '2H$_2$O$_2 \to$ 2H$_2$O + O$_2$. Volume strength $= 11.2 \times$ Normality $= 5.6 \times$ Molarity. $10\;\text{V} \approx 0.893\;\text{N} \approx 0.446\;\text{M}$.', 'c_prac_titrations', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_prac_salt_analysis (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Qualitative salt analysis for cations and anions
-- ============================================================
('In qualitative salt analysis, the preliminary tests include', 'Colour, smell, solubility, flame test, and dry heating', 'Only flame test', 'Only solubility', 'No preliminary tests are done', 'option1', 'Preliminary tests give initial clues. E.g., blue salt $\to$ Cu$^{2+}$, rotten egg smell $\to$ S$^{2-}$, green flame $\to$ Cu/Ba.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The flame test for sodium gives a', 'Green flame', 'Persistent golden yellow flame', 'Crimson red flame', 'Lilac flame', 'option2', 'Na: golden yellow (persistent, masks other colours). K: lilac/violet (seen through cobalt blue glass). Ca: brick red. Ba: apple green. Cu: blue-green.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Group I cations (Ag$^+$, Pb$^{2+}$, Hg$_2^{2+}$) are precipitated by', 'Dilute HCl (as insoluble chlorides)', 'H$_2$S in acidic medium', 'NH$_4$OH', '(NH$_4$)$_2$CO$_3$', 'option1', 'AgCl (white), PbCl$_2$ (white, soluble in hot water), Hg$_2$Cl$_2$ (white). Group reagent: dilute HCl.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Group II cations (Cu$^{2+}$, Pb$^{2+}$, Bi$^{3+}$, Cd$^{2+}$, As$^{3+}$, Sb$^{3+}$, Sn$^{2+}$) are precipitated by', 'Dilute HCl', 'H$_2$S in acidic medium (as insoluble sulfides)', 'NH$_4$OH', 'NaOH', 'option2', 'CuS (black), PbS (black), Bi$_2$S$_3$ (black), CdS (yellow), As$_2$S$_3$ (yellow), Sb$_2$S$_3$ (orange). Group reagent: H$_2$S in dilute HCl.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The carbonate anion (CO$_3^{2-}$) is detected by', 'Adding NaOH', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'Flame test', 'Adding dilute HCl — effervescence of CO$_2$ which turns lime water milky', 'option4', 'CO$_3^{2-}$ + 2H$^+ \to$ CO$_2\uparrow$ + H$_2$O. CO$_2$ + Ca(OH)$_2 \to$ CaCO$_3$ (white turbidity). Excess CO$_2$: CaCO$_3$ + CO$_2$ + H$_2$O $\to$ Ca(HCO$_3$)$_2$ (clear).', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The sulphate anion (SO$_4^{2-}$) is detected by', 'Flame test', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'Adding BaCl$_2$ solution — white precipitate of BaSO$_4$ insoluble in conc. HCl', 'Adding NaOH', 'option3', 'SO$_4^{2-}$ + Ba$^{2+} \to$ BaSO$_4\downarrow$ (white, insoluble in HCl). Distinguishes from CO$_3^{2-}$ and SO$_3^{2-}$ (which dissolve in HCl).', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The chloride anion (Cl$^-$) is detected by', 'Adding AgNO$_3$ — white precipitate of AgCl soluble in NH$_4$OH', 'Adding BaCl$_2$', 'Flame test', 'Adding NaOH', 'option1', 'Cl$^-$ + Ag$^+ \to$ AgCl$\downarrow$ (white, curdy). Soluble in dilute NH$_4$OH: AgCl + 2NH$_3 \to$ [Ag(NH$_3$)$_2$]$^+$ + Cl$^-$.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Group III cations (Fe$^{3+}$, Al$^{3+}$, Cr$^{3+}$) are precipitated by', 'H$_2$S', 'Dilute HCl', 'NH$_4$OH in the presence of NH$_4$Cl (as hydroxides)', '(NH$_4$)$_2$CO$_3$', 'option3', 'Fe(OH)$_3$ (reddish-brown), Al(OH)$_3$ (white gelatinous), Cr(OH)$_3$ (green). NH$_4$Cl prevents precipitation of Group IV.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Group IV cations (Co$^{2+}$, Ni$^{2+}$, Mn$^{2+}$, Zn$^{2+}$) are precipitated by', '(NH$_4$)$_2$CO$_3$', 'Dilute HCl', 'NH$_4$OH alone', 'H$_2$S in ammoniacal medium (as sulfides)', 'option4', 'CoS (black), NiS (black), MnS (buff/pink), ZnS (white). Higher pH (NH$_3$) needed because these sulfides have higher $K_{sp}$.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Group V cations (Ba$^{2+}$, Ca$^{2+}$, Sr$^{2+}$) are precipitated by', 'Dilute HCl', '(NH$_4$)$_2$CO$_3$ in ammoniacal medium (as carbonates)', 'H$_2$S', 'AgNO$_3$', 'option2', 'BaCO$_3$, CaCO$_3$, SrCO$_3$ (all white). Distinguished by flame test: Ba (green), Ca (brick red), Sr (crimson).', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The nitrate anion (NO$_3^-$) is detected by the brown ring test using', 'NaOH', 'AgNO$_3$', 'BaCl$_2$', 'FeSO$_4$ + conc. H$_2$SO$_4$', 'option4', 'NO$_3^-$ + 3Fe$^{2+}$ + 4H$^+ \to$ NO + 3Fe$^{3+}$ + 2H$_2$O. NO + FeSO$_4 \to$ [Fe(H$_2$O)$_5$NO]$^{2+}$ (brown ring at junction).', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Fe$^{3+}$ is confirmed by adding KSCN, which gives', 'A green colour', 'A blue precipitate', 'No colour', 'A blood-red colour (Fe(SCN)$_3$)', 'option4', 'Fe$^{3+}$ + 3SCN$^- \to$ Fe(SCN)$_3$ (blood red). Very sensitive test. Also: Prussian blue test with K$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$].', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Cu$^{2+}$ is confirmed by adding excess NH$_4$OH, which gives', 'A white precipitate', 'A deep blue solution ([Cu(NH$_3$)$_4$]$^{2+}$, tetraamminecopper(II))', 'No change', 'A red precipitate', 'option2', 'Cu$^{2+}$ + 4NH$_3 \to$ [Cu(NH$_3$)$_4$]$^{2+}$ (deep blue, Schweizer''s reagent). Characteristic test for Cu$^{2+}$.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Zn$^{2+}$ is confirmed by adding NaOH (white ppt, soluble in excess — amphoteric) and then', 'Passing H$_2$S to get white ZnS precipitate', 'Adding AgNO$_3$', 'Flame test', 'Adding HCl', 'option1', 'Zn(OH)$_2$ (white, amphoteric: dissolves in excess NaOH as Na$_2$[Zn(OH)$_4$]). ZnS: white precipitate in alkaline medium.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The phosphate anion (PO$_4^{3-}$) is detected by the ammonium molybdate test, which gives', 'A canary yellow precipitate of ammonium phosphomolybdate', 'A white precipitate', 'A blue solution', 'No reaction', 'option1', 'PO$_4^{3-}$ + 12(NH$_4$)$_2$MoO$_4$ + 24HNO$_3 \to$ (NH$_4$)$_3$PO$_4 \cdot 12$MoO$_3\downarrow$ (yellow) + ... Confirmatory test for phosphate.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Interfering anions (CO$_3^{2-}$, SO$_3^{2-}$, S$^{2-}$, NO$_2^-$) must be removed before testing for other anions because', 'They enhance other tests', 'They are harmless', 'They react with group reagents and give false positives', 'No removal is needed', 'option3', 'E.g., S$^{2-}$ interferes with Group II cation analysis (precipitates metal sulfides prematurely). Remove by boiling with dilute HCl.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The chromyl chloride test is specific for', 'Iodide ions', 'Bromide ions', 'Chloride ions (Cl$^-$)', 'Sulphate ions', 'option3', 'K$_2$Cr$_2$O$_7$ + 4NaCl + 6H$_2$SO$_4 \to$ 2CrO$_2$Cl$_2$ (red vapours) + ... CrO$_2$Cl$_2$ dissolved in NaOH gives yellow CrO$_4^{2-}$. Confirms Cl$^-$.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Al$^{3+}$ is confirmed by the lake test using', 'K$_4$[Fe(CN)$_6$]', 'KSCN', 'Alizarin (1,2-dihydroxyanthraquinone) which forms a red lake with Al(OH)$_3$', 'Dimethylglyoxime', 'option3', 'Al(OH)$_3$ + alizarin $\to$ red "lake" (adsorption complex). Specific for Al$^{3+}$ in the presence of other Group III cations.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The borax bead test for Co gives a', 'Blue bead (in both oxidizing and reducing flames)', 'Green bead', 'Red bead', 'Colourless bead', 'option1', 'Co: blue bead (characteristic). Cu: blue (oxidizing), red (reducing). Cr: green. Mn: violet (oxidizing). Fe: yellow-brown.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ring test for NO$_3^-$ fails in the presence of', 'Br$^-$, I$^-$, NO$_2^-$ (which interfere by reacting with FeSO$_4$ or H$_2$SO$_4$)', 'Na$^+$', 'K$^+$', 'Cl$^-$', 'option1', 'Br$^-$/I$^-$: oxidized by H$_2$SO$_4$, producing coloured products. NO$_2^-$: also gives brown ring. Must be removed first.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Nessler''s reagent (K$_2$[HgI$_4$] + KOH) is used to detect', 'Cl$^-$', 'Fe$^{3+}$', 'Cu$^{2+}$', 'NH$_4^+$ ions (gives brown precipitate)', 'option4', 'NH$_4^+$ + 2K$_2$[HgI$_4$] + 4KOH $\to$ Hg$_2$O$\cdot$NH$_2$I$\downarrow$ (brown) + 7KI + 3H$_2$O. Very sensitive test for ammonia/ammonium.', 'c_prac_salt_analysis', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- c_prac_experiments (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Enthalpy experiments, sols, kinetic study of iodide with H2O2
-- ============================================================
('The enthalpy of neutralization of a strong acid with a strong base is measured using', 'A thermometer alone', 'A calorimeter (measuring temperature change of the solution)', 'A voltmeter', 'A spectrometer', 'option2', 'Mix known volumes of acid and base in an insulated calorimeter. $\Delta H = mc\Delta T/n$. For strong acid + strong base: $\approx -57.1\;\text{kJ/mol}$.', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The enthalpy of solution is measured by', 'Electrolysis', 'Heating the solution', 'Dissolving a known mass of solute in water in a calorimeter and measuring temperature change', 'Distillation', 'option3', 'Endothermic dissolution (e.g., NH$_4$NO$_3$): temperature drops. Exothermic (e.g., NaOH): temperature rises.', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A sol (colloidal solution) can be prepared by', 'Only distillation', 'Only filtration', 'Dispersion methods (mechanical, electrical, peptization) or condensation methods (chemical reaction)', 'Only crystallization', 'option3', 'Dispersion: break large particles into colloidal size. Condensation: aggregate molecules/ions into colloidal particles.', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Tyndall effect in a colloidal solution is', 'The absorption of light', 'The scattering of light by colloidal particles, making the beam visible', 'The reflection of light', 'No interaction with light', 'option2', 'Colloidal particles ($1$-$100\;\text{nm}$) scatter light. A beam of light passing through a sol is visible from the side (Tyndall cone).', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The kinetic study of the reaction between KI and H$_2$O$_2$ can be followed by', 'Measuring conductivity only', 'Measuring colour change', 'Weighing the products', 'Measuring the volume of O$_2$ evolved or titrating unreacted H$_2$O$_2$ with KMnO$_4$ at intervals', 'option4', 'H$_2$O$_2$ + 2KI $\to$ 2KOH + I$_2$. Follow by: (1) titrating H$_2$O$_2$ with KMnO$_4$, or (2) titrating I$_2$ with Na$_2$S$_2$O$_3$.', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Coagulation (flocculation) of a sol is caused by', 'Cooling only', 'Heating only', 'Adding an electrolyte that neutralizes the charge on colloidal particles', 'Adding more solvent', 'option3', 'Colloidal particles carry charge (electrical double layer). Electrolyte ions neutralize this charge, allowing particles to aggregate and settle.', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Hardy-Schulze rule states that the coagulating power of an ion increases with', 'Decreasing concentration', 'Decreasing charge', 'Increasing size only', 'Increasing charge of the ion (higher valency ions coagulate more effectively)', 'option4', 'For a negatively charged sol: Al$^{3+} >$ Ca$^{2+} >$ Na$^+$ in coagulating power. Higher charge $=$ more effective.', 'c_prac_experiments', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the calorimetric determination of enthalpy of neutralization, the heat capacity of the calorimeter is accounted for by', 'Ignoring it', 'Determining the water equivalent of the calorimeter', 'Assuming it is zero', 'Measuring the mass of the calorimeter', 'option2', 'Water equivalent: mass of water that absorbs the same heat as the calorimeter for the same $\Delta T$. $q = (m_{water} + W) \times c \times \Delta T$.', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Preparation of ferric hydroxide sol involves', 'Adding FeCl$_3$ solution dropwise to boiling water (hydrolysis/condensation)', 'Mixing FeCl$_3$ with NaOH in excess', 'Electrolysis', 'Distillation', 'option1', 'FeCl$_3$ + 3H$_2$O $\to$ Fe(OH)$_3$ (sol) + 3HCl. The colloidal Fe(OH)$_3$ is stabilized by adsorbed Fe$^{3+}$ ions (positive sol).', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Preparation of As$_2$S$_3$ sol involves', 'Mixing As$_2$O$_3$ with NaOH', 'Passing H$_2$S through arsenious oxide solution', 'Electrolysis', 'Heating As$_2$O$_3$', 'option2', 'As$_2$O$_3$ + 3H$_2$S $\to$ As$_2$S$_3$ (sol, yellow) + 3H$_2$O. Negative sol (adsorbed S$^{2-}$ ions).', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The rate of the KI + H$_2$O$_2$ reaction can be determined by plotting', '$\ln[\text{H}_2\text{O}_2]$ vs time (if first order in H$_2$O$_2$) — slope gives $-k$', 'Temperature vs time', 'Volume vs mass', 'Colour vs time', 'option1', 'If first order: $\ln[H_2O_2] = -kt + \ln[H_2O_2]_0$. Linear plot confirms first order. Slope $= -k$.', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Dialysis is used to purify colloidal solutions by', 'Filtering', 'Removing dissolved ions/molecules through a semipermeable membrane while retaining colloidal particles', 'Distilling', 'Centrifuging', 'option2', 'Colloidal particles are too large to pass through the membrane. Small ions and molecules diffuse out. Electrodialysis speeds up the process.', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The enthalpy of neutralization of a weak acid with a strong base is less than $57.1\;\text{kJ/mol}$ because', 'Weak acids release more heat', 'Part of the energy is used to ionize the weak acid', 'Strong bases are weaker', 'No heat is released', 'option2', '$\Delta H_{neutralization} = \Delta H_{ionization} + (-57.1)$. Since $\Delta H_{ionization} > 0$ (endothermic), the net $|\Delta H|$ is less than $57.1$.', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Brownian motion in a colloidal solution is caused by', 'Gravity', 'Unequal bombardment of colloidal particles by solvent molecules', 'Magnetic fields', 'Electric fields', 'option2', 'Random, zigzag motion of colloidal particles. Provides evidence for the kinetic molecular theory. Observed under ultramicroscope.', 'c_prac_experiments', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The activation energy of the KI + H$_2$O$_2$ reaction can be determined by', 'Measuring the colour', 'Measuring the rate constant at two different temperatures and using the Arrhenius equation', 'Measuring the volume only', 'It cannot be determined experimentally', 'option2', '$\ln(k_2/k_1) = (E_a/R)(1/T_1 - 1/T_2)$. Measure $k$ at two temperatures, solve for $E_a$.', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The protective action of lyophilic sols on lyophobic sols is measured by', 'The silver number', 'The gold number (mg of lyophilic colloid needed to prevent coagulation of $10\;\text{mL}$ of gold sol by $1\;\text{mL}$ of $10\%$ NaCl)', 'The pH', 'The conductivity', 'option2', 'Lower gold number $=$ better protective action. Gelatin ($0.005$-$0.01$) $>$ albumin ($0.1$) $>$ starch ($25$).', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the clock reaction (iodine clock), the sudden appearance of blue colour indicates', 'A new reactant has been added', 'The reaction has stopped', 'The temperature has changed', 'All the thiosulfate has been consumed and free I$_2$ now reacts with starch', 'option4', 'I$_2$ produced reacts with S$_2$O$_3^{2-}$ first (colourless). When S$_2$O$_3^{2-}$ is exhausted, I$_2$ accumulates and reacts with starch (blue). Time to blue $\propto 1/$rate.', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electrophoresis of a colloidal solution demonstrates that', 'No movement occurs', 'Colloidal particles are neutral', 'Colloidal particles are ions', 'Colloidal particles carry electric charge (they migrate towards the electrode of opposite charge)', 'option4', 'Positive sol (e.g., Fe(OH)$_3$): particles move to cathode. Negative sol (e.g., As$_2$S$_3$): particles move to anode.', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The emulsifying agent in an oil-in-water emulsion', 'Dissolves the oil', 'Increases the interfacial tension', 'Lowers the interfacial tension and forms a protective film around oil droplets', 'Has no effect', 'option3', 'Emulsifiers (soap, detergent, proteins): amphiphilic molecules. Hydrophilic head in water, hydrophobic tail in oil. Stabilizes the emulsion.', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Freundlich adsorption isotherm is expressed as', '$x/m = kP^{1/n}$ (where $x/m$ is mass adsorbed per unit mass of adsorbent, $P$ is pressure)', '$x/m = kP$', '$x/m = k/P$', '$x/m = kP^2$', 'option1', 'Empirical: $\log(x/m) = \log k + (1/n)\log P$. Plot of $\log(x/m)$ vs $\log P$ is linear. $1/n$ is between 0 and 1.', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Langmuir adsorption isotherm assumes', 'Infinite layers', 'Multilayer adsorption', 'No adsorption', 'Monolayer adsorption on a surface with a finite number of identical sites', 'option4', '$\theta = KP/(1 + KP)$ where $\theta$ is fractional coverage. At low $P$: $\theta \propto P$ (Henry''s law). At high $P$: $\theta \to 1$ (saturation).', 'c_prac_experiments', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved');
